00:00:04.604 --> 00:00:07.040 align:start size:84% position:16%
It's sort of
traumatic to read it.

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:08.441
Because I struggle with it.

00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:17.584
Because I'm in pain
most of the time.

00:00:17.584 --> 00:00:22.589
But I know that it's not
helpful to be resentful.

00:00:23.923 --> 00:00:25.325
It's better to be grateful.

00:00:27.794 --> 00:00:30.530
(dramatic music)

00:00:32.599 --> 00:00:34.234 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
- I'm Dave Rubin,
and joining me today

00:00:34.234 --> 00:00:38.972 align:start size:97% position:3% line:0%
is a clinical psychologist,
a lecturer an author,

00:00:38.972 --> 00:00:40.306
and perhaps most importantly,

00:00:40.306 --> 00:00:42.809
a man who truly changed
my life for the better

00:00:42.809 --> 00:00:44.444
Dr. Jordan Peterson.

00:00:44.444 --> 00:00:46.046
Welcome back to
the Rubin Report.

00:00:47.614 --> 00:00:49.049
- It's really good
to see you Dave.

00:00:49.049 --> 00:00:52.218 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Gosh, well, we connected
for about a minute

00:00:52.218 --> 00:00:54.687
before we started this
officially and you said,

00:00:54.687 --> 00:00:57.090
boy it's been about two years.

00:00:57.090 --> 00:00:58.558
We've we've texted a little bit.

00:00:58.558 --> 00:01:00.293
It's been a crazy two years.

00:01:00.293 --> 00:01:01.828
It goes without saying.

00:01:01.828 --> 00:01:04.998 align:start size:88% position:13%
So before I get into
any of the meat of this,

00:01:04.998 --> 00:01:07.467 align:start size:84% position:16%
and I wanna talk
obviously about the...

00:01:07.467 --> 00:01:08.701
About the new book
and everything else

00:01:08.701 --> 00:01:10.070
I think what's on
everyone's mind

00:01:10.070 --> 00:01:11.905 align:start size:81% position:19%
more than anything
is how are you.

00:01:11.905 --> 00:01:13.306
Just, how are you?

00:01:15.075 --> 00:01:17.444 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
- Well, I'm at home
and I'm working

00:01:17.444 --> 00:01:19.779 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
about a third of the
time I would say.

00:01:19.779 --> 00:01:21.948 line:0%
I'm working hard on my podcasts

00:01:21.948 --> 00:01:23.983
and YouTube discussions.

00:01:23.983 --> 00:01:27.487
And I can manage about
two hours of work a day.

00:01:27.487 --> 00:01:29.422
And so I'm not in the hospital.

00:01:29.422 --> 00:01:34.060 align:start size:88% position:13%
And I'm sharp again,
reasonably sharp, again.

00:01:34.060 --> 00:01:35.395
Trying to write again.

00:01:36.563 --> 00:01:37.430
I'm planning a new book.

00:01:37.430 --> 00:01:38.898
We'll see how that goes.

00:01:38.898 --> 00:01:40.233
It's nice to be back.

00:01:40.233 --> 00:01:41.768
It's nice to be doing
these discussions.

00:01:41.768 --> 00:01:43.269
I've talked to lots
of interesting people

00:01:43.269 --> 00:01:45.071
over the last couple of months.

00:01:45.071 --> 00:01:49.742 align:start size:97% position:3%
Douglas Murray, Ian
Hazili, [indistinct] Ian...

00:01:51.811 --> 00:01:54.547
I'm gonna forget Ian's
last name McGilchrist.

00:01:55.748 --> 00:01:57.717
He wrote The master
and His Emissary.

00:01:59.152 --> 00:02:02.188 align:start size:97% position:3%
So I'm working a bit and I
have this new book coming out,

00:02:02.188 --> 00:02:05.158
as you mentioned, and that's
coming out on the 2nd of March.

00:02:05.158 --> 00:02:10.163
So that's interesting and
exciting and stressful.

00:02:11.331 --> 00:02:13.133
All of those things.

00:02:13.133 --> 00:02:14.601
I hope it's well-received.

00:02:14.601 --> 00:02:16.436
I can't evaluate it.

00:02:16.436 --> 00:02:18.538
You know, I'm too close
to it to evaluate it.

00:02:18.538 --> 00:02:21.174
- Yeah, well, I have no
doubt that there will be

00:02:21.174 --> 00:02:23.042
plenty of people evaluating it.

00:02:23.042 --> 00:02:25.712
And you've already
sort of dove back

00:02:25.712 --> 00:02:27.547
into dealing with the media.

00:02:27.547 --> 00:02:31.284
So all that stuff
is kind of common.

00:02:31.284 --> 00:02:34.120
But you know obviously
over the last two years

00:02:34.120 --> 00:02:35.321
so much has happened,

00:02:35.321 --> 00:02:36.623
and it feels like we're
in a different world

00:02:36.623 --> 00:02:38.057
and everything else.

00:02:38.057 --> 00:02:40.026 align:start size:97% position:3%
And you know, so many people,
they asked me about you.

00:02:40.026 --> 00:02:41.528
They asked me about
our time together

00:02:41.528 --> 00:02:43.429
and the tour and
everything else.

00:02:43.429 --> 00:02:46.266
And truly for me when
people ask me that,

00:02:46.266 --> 00:02:48.268
or just when I think
back to that year.

00:02:48.268 --> 00:02:51.804 align:start size:97% position:3%
I mean, we did about 120
stops in almost 20 countries.

00:02:51.804 --> 00:02:54.941 align:start position:0%
You did a bunch more that I
couldn't attend even after that.

00:02:55.942 --> 00:02:57.877
It feels like a dream to me.

00:02:57.877 --> 00:03:01.047 align:start size:88% position:13%
Like that year it was
so intense and wonderful

00:03:01.047 --> 00:03:04.417 align:start size:97% position:3%
and magical and sometimes
stressful, sometimes tiring,

00:03:04.417 --> 00:03:07.387 align:start size:94% position:6%
whatever it was, but it
was just in the summation.

00:03:07.387 --> 00:03:08.655
It was just wonderful.

00:03:08.655 --> 00:03:13.059
What do you think back
on mostly about that?

00:03:13.059 --> 00:03:15.495
- Well, it seems like
a dream to me too.

00:03:15.495 --> 00:03:19.299 align:start position:0%
You know being the last two
years have been mostly hospitals

00:03:19.299 --> 00:03:20.600
for one reason or another.

00:03:20.600 --> 00:03:22.936
And I suppose it's
partly dreamlike

00:03:22.936 --> 00:03:24.404
because of the vast contrast

00:03:24.404 --> 00:03:28.408
between 2018 and
then 2019 and 2020.

00:03:28.408 --> 00:03:30.777
I mean, that tour was
an amazing experience.

00:03:30.777 --> 00:03:35.248
It was so positive, apart
from the odd press blast.

00:03:36.416 --> 00:03:39.686
Which were islands of
negativity in a sea

00:03:39.686 --> 00:03:41.554
of what was positive.

00:03:41.554 --> 00:03:44.757
It was really something to
travel from city to city

00:03:44.757 --> 00:03:46.826
and to talk to all those people.

00:03:46.826 --> 00:03:49.162
And yeah, it was an
amazing experience.

00:03:49.162 --> 00:03:51.064 align:start size:84% position:16%
And I'd certainly
like to do it again.

00:03:51.064 --> 00:03:53.533
If I could gather
myself together

00:03:55.568 --> 00:03:57.904
I know Tammy too had a really...

00:03:57.904 --> 00:04:00.273
She thought it was
a great adventure.

00:04:00.273 --> 00:04:02.542 align:start size:84% position:16%
And I thought we
worked well together.

00:04:02.542 --> 00:04:05.278
You and I, it was nice to
have some levity associated

00:04:05.278 --> 00:04:06.679
with all the seriousness.

00:04:06.679 --> 00:04:07.747
That's a good combination.

00:04:07.747 --> 00:04:09.249
If you could pull it off.

00:04:09.249 --> 00:04:11.851 align:start size:84% position:16%
It was so nice to
see all those people

00:04:11.851 --> 00:04:15.421
who were trying to put
their lives together.

00:04:16.522 --> 00:04:19.192 align:start size:84% position:16%
And such a remarkable
range of people.

00:04:20.393 --> 00:04:21.794
I don't wanna use
the word diverse

00:04:21.794 --> 00:04:23.730 align:start size:97% position:3%
because it's been completely
poisoned of course.

00:04:23.730 --> 00:04:27.200
But there were every sort
of person in our audiences

00:04:27.200 --> 00:04:32.205 align:start size:97% position:3%
and they'd all come together
for positive reasons,

00:04:32.939 --> 00:04:33.773
as far as I could tell.

00:04:33.773 --> 00:04:35.475
It sure felt that way.

00:04:35.475 --> 00:04:37.243
It was so nice to
meet people too.

00:04:37.243 --> 00:04:39.912
And they were always so
friendly and welcoming.

00:04:41.547 --> 00:04:45.752
You know, when you say
I've been so fortunate

00:04:45.752 --> 00:04:47.920 align:start size:94% position:6%
in the response that
I've received from people

00:04:47.920 --> 00:04:51.124 align:start position:0%
who've watched my YouTube videos
or listened to the podcast

00:04:51.124 --> 00:04:52.325
or read the book.

00:04:52.325 --> 00:04:55.328
It's really sustained
me, kept me going.

00:04:55.328 --> 00:04:57.130
- Yeah, it was great.

00:04:57.130 --> 00:04:59.132 align:start size:84% position:16%
- It was a ridiculous
experience.

00:04:59.132 --> 00:04:59.966
It was unbelievable.

00:04:59.966 --> 00:05:00.767
- That's it?

00:05:01.834 --> 00:05:03.770 align:start size:84% position:16%
- It was a ridiculous
experience.

00:05:03.770 --> 00:05:05.838
People ask me this
too and I actually

00:05:05.838 --> 00:05:07.106
don't have a good
answer for this.

00:05:07.106 --> 00:05:10.643
But beyond just the
occasional hit piece

00:05:10.643 --> 00:05:12.578 align:start size:84% position:16%
and the strange things
the New York times,

00:05:12.578 --> 00:05:14.814 align:start size:88% position:13%
Enforcement [indistinct]
and the rest of that,

00:05:14.814 --> 00:05:16.482
was there anything negative?

00:05:16.482 --> 00:05:18.518
I mean, there were
some emotional stuff
that was happening

00:05:18.518 --> 00:05:21.120
and obviously you found
out that Tammy was ill

00:05:21.120 --> 00:05:22.755
and thank God she's better now.

00:05:22.755 --> 00:05:23.756
And you were having-

00:05:23.756 --> 00:05:25.825
- She says hello
by the way Dave.

00:05:25.825 --> 00:05:26.759 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Oh, please send
her send her my love.

00:05:26.759 --> 00:05:27.960
Mine and David's please do.

00:05:28.928 --> 00:05:31.130
But beyond some of that...

00:05:31.130 --> 00:05:34.267
The hit piece stuff and
some of the health stuff

00:05:34.267 --> 00:05:36.969 align:start size:97% position:3%
that you started to deal
with and that Tammy was ill.

00:05:36.969 --> 00:05:38.905 align:start size:88% position:13%
Do you remember anything
that was negative?

00:05:38.905 --> 00:05:40.573
I truly don't.

00:05:40.573 --> 00:05:43.509
It was like every day
that we went somewhere

00:05:43.509 --> 00:05:46.946
it was more love, more people
putting their lives together.

00:05:46.946 --> 00:05:49.349
And the stories, just
the incredible stories

00:05:49.349 --> 00:05:51.117
that we heard everywhere.

00:05:51.117 --> 00:05:54.120
- Yeah, well, and we did the
meet and greets afterwards

00:05:54.120 --> 00:05:56.723
and I also really liked that.

00:05:56.723 --> 00:05:59.425 align:start size:88% position:13%
I only 10 to 15 seconds
with each person

00:05:59.425 --> 00:06:01.160
or couple or family
whatever it was,

00:06:01.160 --> 00:06:04.063 align:start size:88% position:13%
but that was all
extremely positive too.

00:06:04.063 --> 00:06:05.998
So, no, I mean,

00:06:05.998 --> 00:06:09.202
I've tried to figure out
why I've become so ill.

00:06:09.202 --> 00:06:10.636
There's a variety of reasons.

00:06:10.636 --> 00:06:12.338
Some of it's associated

00:06:12.338 --> 00:06:17.343 align:start size:97% position:3%
with this familial proclivity
for depression, no doubt.

00:06:18.478 --> 00:06:20.580 align:start size:88% position:13%
But I don't believe that
it was a consequence

00:06:20.580 --> 00:06:23.416
of certainly not of that tour.

00:06:23.416 --> 00:06:27.220
I mean, and I've always liked
my whole life since I was 25.

00:06:27.220 --> 00:06:28.588
I've gone flat out.

00:06:28.588 --> 00:06:29.789
I like that.

00:06:29.789 --> 00:06:31.257
I like to live like that.

00:06:31.257 --> 00:06:34.594 align:start size:84% position:16%
To work 15 or 16
hours a day flat out.

00:06:34.594 --> 00:06:36.796 align:start size:84% position:16%
And I mean, I did
learn how to do that.

00:06:36.796 --> 00:06:39.232
I thought and be
able to sustain it.

00:06:39.232 --> 00:06:44.237 align:start size:94% position:6%
And so, no, my health
wasn't 100% on that tour.

00:06:46.005 --> 00:06:47.407
That was probably
a prodrome drama

00:06:47.407 --> 00:06:48.674 align:start size:84% position:16%
for everything that
happened later.

00:06:48.674 --> 00:06:50.476
But I really don't
think it had anything

00:06:50.476 --> 00:06:51.611
to do with the tour.

00:06:52.912 --> 00:06:55.815
Now all the controversy
that swirled around me,

00:06:55.815 --> 00:06:56.983
that's a different thing.

00:06:56.983 --> 00:06:59.318 align:start size:84% position:16%
That has been
extremely stressful.

00:06:59.318 --> 00:07:02.488
And I don't really know
how to calibrate that.

00:07:02.488 --> 00:07:04.690
I watch people on Twitter

00:07:04.690 --> 00:07:08.060
and if they say something
and 20 people mob them,

00:07:08.060 --> 00:07:11.497
that's usually enough
to really set them back

00:07:11.497 --> 00:07:12.432
on their heels.

00:07:12.432 --> 00:07:14.500
And generally people apologize

00:07:14.500 --> 00:07:17.036 align:start size:94% position:6%
and well make things
worse often by doing that.

00:07:17.036 --> 00:07:20.873
But it doesn't take many
people to go after you

00:07:20.873 --> 00:07:24.177 align:start size:97% position:3%
before it has a real
pronounced emotional effect.

00:07:24.177 --> 00:07:29.182
And that happened a lot
and it was somewhat...

00:07:30.249 --> 00:07:31.851 align:start size:84% position:16%
I can't really
understand it exactly,

00:07:31.851 --> 00:07:33.553
why it happened so much.

00:07:35.955 --> 00:07:38.090
And I'm sure that had
some effect on me.

00:07:38.090 --> 00:07:42.929
Because when it happens now
it certainly wallops me.

00:07:44.197 --> 00:07:46.199 align:start size:84% position:16%
We had a run in with
the London Times,

00:07:46.199 --> 00:07:48.100
the Sunday Times a while back,

00:07:48.100 --> 00:07:51.804 align:start size:88% position:13%
and it was unbelievably
stressful two weeks
because of that.

00:07:51.804 --> 00:07:54.340
Cause you never know of
course how the public

00:07:54.340 --> 00:07:56.042
is going to respond either.

00:07:56.042 --> 00:08:00.980
And so far as I said,
I've been so fortunate

00:08:01.848 --> 00:08:03.316
in people's response to me.

00:08:03.316 --> 00:08:05.585 align:start size:94% position:6%
But I thought, well maybe
I've gone to the well

00:08:05.585 --> 00:08:08.688
of public sympathy one
time, too many this time.

00:08:10.456 --> 00:08:14.560 align:start size:84% position:16%
And it was undoubtedly
a mistake perhaps.

00:08:14.560 --> 00:08:15.828
Oh, Christ, I don't know,

00:08:15.828 --> 00:08:17.463
to have talked to
the Sunday times.

00:08:17.463 --> 00:08:20.199
But I thought I had to talk

00:08:20.199 --> 00:08:22.869
about what happened
to me to someone.

00:08:22.869 --> 00:08:25.938
And we thought, well
they're hypothetically

00:08:26.839 --> 00:08:29.575
stayed and reliable.

00:08:29.575 --> 00:08:33.579
And it's hurtful to me
to see these institutions

00:08:33.579 --> 00:08:34.847
become corrupted.

00:08:36.048 --> 00:08:39.151
That I certainly take no
pleasure in seeing that.

00:08:40.319 --> 00:08:43.456 align:start size:84% position:16%
It's appalling in
fact, to watch these

00:08:43.456 --> 00:08:46.626 align:start size:88% position:13%
once great institutions
deteriorate

00:08:46.626 --> 00:08:50.429 align:start size:84% position:16%
into tabloid style
journalism and deceit.

00:08:51.664 --> 00:08:53.833
But no, the tour was
great Dave, I thought.

00:08:53.833 --> 00:08:56.335
Jesus, I mean, it's complicated.

00:08:56.335 --> 00:08:59.939 align:start size:84% position:16%
Because it also, my
life got so disrupted.

00:08:59.939 --> 00:09:02.441
Because I was a professor and
I had my clinical practice

00:09:02.441 --> 00:09:07.346 align:start size:97% position:3%
and my writing and a
business that I ran as well.

00:09:07.346 --> 00:09:10.149 align:start size:88% position:13%
And now I'm not a
professor at the moment,

00:09:10.149 --> 00:09:13.386 align:start size:97% position:3%
I'm on leave still and I don't
have my clinical practice.

00:09:13.386 --> 00:09:16.255
And so I'm still...

00:09:16.255 --> 00:09:19.191 align:start size:88% position:13%
I'm a drift to some
degree because of that.

00:09:19.191 --> 00:09:20.993
Because that was
so much of my life.

00:09:22.595 --> 00:09:25.264
And then of course the
tour filled that up.

00:09:25.264 --> 00:09:28.634 align:start size:94% position:6%
And then, well there's
prolonged bout of illness.

00:09:29.702 --> 00:09:30.903
My wife and then me.

00:09:32.305 --> 00:09:33.839
I'm just starting to come
to terms with whatever

00:09:33.839 --> 00:09:35.474
my new life looks like.

00:09:35.474 --> 00:09:37.710 align:start size:88% position:13%
I think about going
back to the university,

00:09:37.710 --> 00:09:39.211
but I'm not sure I can do it.

00:09:40.179 --> 00:09:41.347
And that's very...

00:09:42.248 --> 00:09:43.516
What would you say?

00:09:44.917 --> 00:09:47.987
I don't know how to
think about the fact

00:09:47.987 --> 00:09:49.822
that maybe I can't do it.

00:09:49.822 --> 00:09:51.991
I'm certainly not
happy about it.

00:09:51.991 --> 00:09:53.259
It's appalling.

00:09:53.259 --> 00:09:54.727
- Do you mean because
of the physical rigors,

00:09:54.727 --> 00:09:55.828
or just because you're-

00:09:55.828 --> 00:09:58.664
- Yeah, yeah.

00:09:58.664 --> 00:10:00.299
Just, I don't think my...

00:10:00.299 --> 00:10:02.368
I don't think I'm
reliable enough

00:10:03.302 --> 00:10:06.405
to take on that responsibility.

00:10:06.405 --> 00:10:07.373
I don't know for sure,

00:10:07.373 --> 00:10:11.010
but I'm still very ill.

00:10:11.010 --> 00:10:12.778 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Yeah, I mean, I don't
even know how to ask this,

00:10:12.778 --> 00:10:14.614 align:start size:97% position:3%
but I mean is there anything
else you wanna address

00:10:14.614 --> 00:10:15.815
with any of that stuff?

00:10:15.815 --> 00:10:16.916 align:start size:84% position:16%
I actually don't wanna
belabor the point,

00:10:16.916 --> 00:10:17.950 align:start size:88% position:13%
but if there's
anything that you wanna

00:10:17.950 --> 00:10:18.784
get off your chest

00:10:18.784 --> 00:10:19.619
I know-

00:10:19.619 --> 00:10:20.453
- We'll see how it goes.

00:10:20.453 --> 00:10:21.921
No, I don't think so.

00:10:21.921 --> 00:10:26.626 align:start size:94% position:6%
I mean, if it comes up
while we talk that's fine.

00:10:28.561 --> 00:10:30.496
No, I'm so bored of it.

00:10:30.496 --> 00:10:35.101 align:start size:88% position:13%
It's so dull and
appalling that we better

00:10:36.235 --> 00:10:37.770 align:start size:84% position:16%
talk about virtually
anything else.

00:10:37.770 --> 00:10:40.139 align:start size:97% position:3%
- All right, well, fear not,
I got a couple of things-

00:10:41.607 --> 00:10:44.510
- I guess there is one thing
I should address Dave really.

00:10:44.510 --> 00:10:48.648
Well, I've been an advocate

00:10:48.648 --> 00:10:50.916 align:start size:84% position:16%
for people to put
their lives together

00:10:50.916 --> 00:10:52.752 align:start size:84% position:16%
and of course my life
has fallen apart.

00:10:52.752 --> 00:10:56.822 align:start size:97% position:3%
And so you might ask
yourself, well are you aware

00:10:56.822 --> 00:10:58.124
of the irony of that?

00:10:58.124 --> 00:11:00.626
And the answer to that
is, well, absolutely.

00:11:00.626 --> 00:11:02.128
You know, it's shameful.

00:11:03.963 --> 00:11:06.198
I feel that frequently.

00:11:06.198 --> 00:11:08.768
And then, so you might say,
and people tweet this out

00:11:08.768 --> 00:11:12.638
fairly regularly, who
am I to give advice?

00:11:12.638 --> 00:11:15.875 align:start size:97% position:3%
And I suppose that's a
perfectly relevant question.

00:11:15.875 --> 00:11:17.343
I guess I've always thought.

00:11:17.343 --> 00:11:19.945
And I said this too, in the
lectures during the tour

00:11:19.945 --> 00:11:22.481 align:start size:94% position:6%
that I don't really
think that what I'm doing

00:11:22.481 --> 00:11:23.683
is giving advice.

00:11:23.683 --> 00:11:26.752
Or if I am I'm also
giving it to me.

00:11:26.752 --> 00:11:28.921 align:start size:81% position:19%
You know, it's
not like I believe

00:11:28.921 --> 00:11:30.456
that I have all the answers.

00:11:30.456 --> 00:11:33.659
I have answers that
I've found useful

00:11:33.659 --> 00:11:35.428
while attempting to move ahead.

00:11:35.428 --> 00:11:37.730
With this new book,
I can show you this.

00:11:39.398 --> 00:11:40.933
That's what it looks like.

00:11:40.933 --> 00:11:43.235
The black counterpart to the
white book, the previous book.

00:11:43.235 --> 00:11:44.737
- Out today.

00:11:44.737 --> 00:11:48.040 align:start size:94% position:6%
- I wrote a lot of it
while I was extremely ill.

00:11:48.040 --> 00:11:50.309
And there were some
advantages to that.

00:11:50.309 --> 00:11:55.314 align:start size:84% position:16%
I think in that I only
kept that material

00:11:55.314 --> 00:11:59.418 align:start size:84% position:16%
that I felt was
reliable under duress.

00:12:00.619 --> 00:12:05.257
Now I think probably,
perhaps my creativity

00:12:05.257 --> 00:12:06.859
was somewhat compromised.

00:12:06.859 --> 00:12:10.529 align:start size:97% position:3%
And so that may have
impacted the book negatively.

00:12:10.529 --> 00:12:13.299 align:start size:84% position:16%
But it made for
more severe editing.

00:12:13.299 --> 00:12:16.502
For example, the last
chapter is Be Grateful

00:12:16.502 --> 00:12:18.537
in Spite of Your Suffering.

00:12:18.537 --> 00:12:22.475 align:start size:94% position:6%
And certainly maintaining
that gratitude

00:12:22.475 --> 00:12:24.310
is something I've
struggled with.

00:12:24.310 --> 00:12:27.646
I think anybody who's
in pain chronically,

00:12:27.646 --> 00:12:30.282
struggles with resentment.

00:12:30.282 --> 00:12:32.818 align:start size:84% position:16%
But I know that
resentment poisons.

00:12:33.853 --> 00:12:35.921 align:start size:88% position:13%
And I think
[indistinct] of courage.

00:12:37.123 --> 00:12:41.927
And so I stand by
what I've written.

00:12:45.097 --> 00:12:46.332
- You know, it's funny.

00:12:46.332 --> 00:12:47.867
I would hear some
version of that.

00:12:47.867 --> 00:12:49.969
People would ask me when
they would ask about you.

00:12:49.969 --> 00:12:51.470
And they would say,

00:12:51.470 --> 00:12:52.905
"Oh, well he was telling people
to get their lives together.

00:12:52.905 --> 00:12:54.673
And here look what
happened to him."

00:12:54.673 --> 00:12:56.876 align:start size:88% position:13%
And my answer was always
the same with people

00:12:56.876 --> 00:13:00.112
which is, well, first off, he
never professed to be Jesus.

00:13:00.112 --> 00:13:01.480 align:start size:81% position:19%
He never professed
to be perfect.

00:13:01.480 --> 00:13:03.582
You actually, and this
is one of the things

00:13:03.582 --> 00:13:05.818 align:start size:94% position:6%
that I think really helped
me throughout the tour.

00:13:05.818 --> 00:13:08.254
You would often address
if you were struggling

00:13:08.254 --> 00:13:09.088
with something.

00:13:10.356 --> 00:13:11.891 align:start size:84% position:16%
From what I saw
virtually every night

00:13:11.891 --> 00:13:13.392 align:start size:84% position:16%
gave a different
lecture every night.

00:13:13.392 --> 00:13:16.095
And if you were frustrated
by something on a given day,

00:13:16.095 --> 00:13:17.363
you would address it that night.

00:13:17.363 --> 00:13:19.098 align:start size:84% position:16%
If something happened
with the media

00:13:19.098 --> 00:13:21.433 align:start size:84% position:16%
or something was
happening personally.

00:13:21.433 --> 00:13:24.603 align:start size:88% position:13%
And I think people, they
see us in these boxes

00:13:24.603 --> 00:13:26.739
where people that write
books or whatever it is,

00:13:26.739 --> 00:13:28.140
and they think that
we're supposed to be

00:13:28.140 --> 00:13:29.708
perfect all the time.

00:13:29.708 --> 00:13:32.378
Probably in your case,
certainly more than mine.

00:13:32.378 --> 00:13:35.381 align:start size:97% position:3%
And it's just not something
that I ever saw you

00:13:35.381 --> 00:13:36.649
say about yourself.

00:13:36.649 --> 00:13:38.184
- Well, luckily, luckily...

00:13:38.184 --> 00:13:40.586
And I'm really stunned by this.

00:13:40.586 --> 00:13:42.054 align:start size:84% position:16%
You know, I thought
the other day

00:13:42.054 --> 00:13:44.790 align:start size:97% position:3%
I'm gonna make an announcement
about the book on Tuesday.

00:13:44.790 --> 00:13:46.292
I think that's March 2nd.

00:13:46.292 --> 00:13:47.927
And I thought, well, what
do I wanna say about it?

00:13:47.927 --> 00:13:50.429
And what I really wanted to
say when I thought about it.

00:13:50.429 --> 00:13:54.200
I thought really what I
wanna do is thank people.

00:13:54.200 --> 00:13:57.469
Because you know, this
criticism has come up.

00:13:57.469 --> 00:13:59.138
And I think it's
a valid criticism.

00:13:59.138 --> 00:14:00.873 align:start size:88% position:13%
It's like, who the hell
is he to talk about

00:14:00.873 --> 00:14:04.276
getting people's act together?

00:14:04.276 --> 00:14:06.679 align:start size:84% position:16%
I mean, everyone gets
ill and you know,

00:14:06.679 --> 00:14:10.049 align:start size:84% position:16%
and everyone has
periods of their life

00:14:10.049 --> 00:14:13.085
where things are going worse
than they might normally go.

00:14:13.085 --> 00:14:15.788 align:start size:84% position:16%
But people have
been on on the whole

00:14:15.788 --> 00:14:18.057
unbelievably patient with me.

00:14:18.057 --> 00:14:21.093
And so I'm not sure why that is.

00:14:21.093 --> 00:14:25.564
I mean, of course, I'm not
sure why any of this is.

00:14:25.564 --> 00:14:27.166
Another thing I struggle with

00:14:27.166 --> 00:14:31.270 align:start size:97% position:3%
is just trying to understand
what the hell is going on

00:14:31.270 --> 00:14:32.538
generally speaking.

00:14:32.538 --> 00:14:36.942
And why I've become such
a target for attention.

00:14:36.942 --> 00:14:38.477
Both negative and positive.

00:14:40.779 --> 00:14:43.249
Mostly positive.

00:14:43.249 --> 00:14:44.483
Thank God.

00:14:44.483 --> 00:14:47.086
I don't know how I could
tolerate it otherwise.

00:14:47.086 --> 00:14:50.322 align:start size:84% position:16%
So I still really
don't understand it.

00:14:50.322 --> 00:14:52.424
You know, I've been recently

00:14:52.424 --> 00:14:54.593
I was reading some
material this week.

00:14:54.593 --> 00:14:57.730 align:start size:84% position:16%
That's a anti-racist
math curricula.

00:14:59.498 --> 00:15:04.370 align:start size:97% position:3%
And it claims for example,
that getting the right answer,

00:15:04.370 --> 00:15:06.972
insisting that there's a
right answer in mathematics

00:15:06.972 --> 00:15:09.975
is a sign of white
supremacy culture.

00:15:11.110 --> 00:15:13.178 align:start size:88% position:13%
And that this isn't
some fringe publication.

00:15:13.178 --> 00:15:16.181 align:start position:0%
Like the LA County of Education
has put its name behind.

00:15:16.181 --> 00:15:18.984
It's funded by the Bill and
Melinda Gates Foundation.

00:15:18.984 --> 00:15:20.719
And I do think that
the Gates Foundation

00:15:20.719 --> 00:15:22.221
does some good work.

00:15:24.323 --> 00:15:27.760 align:start size:84% position:16%
I've been thinking
about that again more.

00:15:27.760 --> 00:15:32.431
And I think that we're
in a war on competence.

00:15:32.431 --> 00:15:33.933
It's something like that.

00:15:33.933 --> 00:15:36.635
We're certainly in a war
on the idea of competence.

00:15:36.635 --> 00:15:39.405 align:start size:84% position:16%
That's why people
are criticizing the
idea of meritocracy.

00:15:39.405 --> 00:15:41.807
But I don't think that
it's just a criticism

00:15:41.807 --> 00:15:44.076
of the idea of meritocracy.

00:15:44.076 --> 00:15:47.913
I think it's an assault
on the idea of merit.

00:15:47.913 --> 00:15:51.150
And well we can
get back to that.

00:15:51.150 --> 00:15:53.419
I don't think I've come up

00:15:53.419 --> 00:15:55.621 align:start size:84% position:16%
with a clearer
formulation than that.

00:15:55.621 --> 00:15:58.290
For some reason I'm
tangled up in that.

00:15:58.290 --> 00:16:02.061
Well, I suppose because I
believe that merit exists.

00:16:02.061 --> 00:16:04.263
- So do you see a
direct through line

00:16:04.263 --> 00:16:08.300
to sort of what put you on
the map in the larger sense,

00:16:08.300 --> 00:16:11.203
meaning Bill C 16 in Canada,

00:16:11.203 --> 00:16:12.671 align:start size:84% position:16%
and you started
talking about that.

00:16:12.671 --> 00:16:16.608
And you didn't want the
government to enforce

00:16:16.608 --> 00:16:19.244 align:start size:88% position:13%
or restrict your
ability to speak freely.

00:16:19.244 --> 00:16:22.281 align:start size:84% position:16%
This was people
pegged you as somehow

00:16:22.281 --> 00:16:23.782 align:start size:84% position:16%
anti-trans or
something like that.

00:16:23.782 --> 00:16:26.352
But you didn't want them
to be able to just tell you

00:16:26.352 --> 00:16:28.487 align:start size:88% position:13%
what pronouns you
could or could not use.

00:16:28.487 --> 00:16:31.256
Do you see a through
line from that moment?

00:16:32.458 --> 00:16:33.692
And that's sort of when
I first heard about you.

00:16:33.692 --> 00:16:35.794
This is now four
or five years ago.

00:16:35.794 --> 00:16:40.065
To where we're at in terms of
the assault on STEM and the-

00:16:40.065 --> 00:16:41.900
- There's some kind
of through line.

00:16:45.304 --> 00:16:48.741
It eludes me that the
totality of the motive

00:16:52.578 --> 00:16:55.014
for the culture wars eludes me.

00:16:55.014 --> 00:16:58.917
But I do think assault on
the idea of competence.

00:16:58.917 --> 00:17:03.122
It's a replay of the
Cane and Abel story.

00:17:04.390 --> 00:17:06.025
Bitter attacks on...

00:17:07.993 --> 00:17:10.229
It's motivated by
some resentment.

00:17:10.229 --> 00:17:11.697
Some deep resentment.

00:17:11.697 --> 00:17:15.300 align:start size:97% position:3%
And it's easy to be
resentful about competence.

00:17:15.300 --> 00:17:16.535
The thing is though there's

00:17:16.535 --> 00:17:18.303 align:start size:84% position:16%
lots of different
forms of competence.

00:17:20.706 --> 00:17:23.509 align:start size:84% position:16%
You can allow
society to do exist,

00:17:23.509 --> 00:17:25.144 align:start size:94% position:6%
where there's hierarchies
of competence,

00:17:25.144 --> 00:17:26.578
as long as there's many of them.

00:17:26.578 --> 00:17:31.150 align:start position:0%
And then people can thrive
according to their own ability.

00:17:32.584 --> 00:17:34.286
And that's good.

00:17:34.286 --> 00:17:36.121
As far as I can tell.

00:17:36.121 --> 00:17:39.625 align:start size:97% position:3%
Everyone should be able to
capitalize on their strengths,

00:17:39.625 --> 00:17:42.027
and strengths are
always relative.

00:17:42.027 --> 00:17:45.731
And relative strength has merit.

00:17:45.731 --> 00:17:48.467 align:start size:88% position:13%
The assault on merit
leaves, everyone bereft.

00:17:50.069 --> 00:17:53.639
If people have relative
strengths which they do,

00:17:53.639 --> 00:17:55.507
and if there is such
a thing as strength,

00:17:55.507 --> 00:17:58.610 align:start size:94% position:6%
which should be something
like relative ability

00:17:58.610 --> 00:18:01.146 align:start size:84% position:16%
to make a contribution
in a given domain.

00:18:01.146 --> 00:18:02.781
That's what you trade on.

00:18:02.781 --> 00:18:04.083
That's what you capitalize on.

00:18:04.083 --> 00:18:06.251
That's what you have
to offer the world.

00:18:06.251 --> 00:18:08.420
And his assault on
merit is an assault

00:18:08.420 --> 00:18:10.055
on everyone's strength.

00:18:10.055 --> 00:18:12.624 align:start size:84% position:16%
Because where you're
relatively strong

00:18:12.624 --> 00:18:15.861 align:start size:88% position:13%
is where you're
relatively meritorious.

00:18:15.861 --> 00:18:20.866 align:start size:88% position:13%
And there's a hard
drive to tear that down.

00:18:21.733 --> 00:18:23.836
And it's driven by resentment.

00:18:30.209 --> 00:18:32.511
And I'm tangled up in it somehow

00:18:32.511 --> 00:18:35.180
well, so many people
are tangled up in it.

00:18:35.180 --> 00:18:36.615
The whole political situation

00:18:36.615 --> 00:18:39.852
seems radically unstable.

00:18:39.852 --> 00:18:42.454
Far more so than two
years ago, I would say.

00:18:43.889 --> 00:18:45.157
- You know, an awful lot
of people would say to me

00:18:45.157 --> 00:18:46.792
over the last two years,

00:18:46.792 --> 00:18:49.027
as the politics got
crazier and crazier,

00:18:49.027 --> 00:18:51.130
"Man, if Jordan was around,

00:18:51.130 --> 00:18:53.799 align:start size:84% position:16%
he'd be fixing
some of this stuff"

00:18:53.799 --> 00:18:55.734
That's an unbelievable burden.

00:18:55.734 --> 00:18:58.270
And yet I believe it to be true.

00:18:58.270 --> 00:19:01.673 align:start size:84% position:16%
That I think when your
voice wasn't there

00:19:01.673 --> 00:19:06.011
there was suddenly this hole
that none of us could fill.

00:19:06.011 --> 00:19:07.679
And a lot of people who
I think were sort of

00:19:07.679 --> 00:19:11.783 align:start size:97% position:3%
veering towards order, I think
oddly veered towards chaos.

00:19:12.718 --> 00:19:14.153
- Well, it's very frustrating

00:19:14.153 --> 00:19:16.555
not to be able to
participate fully.

00:19:16.555 --> 00:19:21.326
And it's, I have a
very difficult time

00:19:21.326 --> 00:19:22.861
coming to terms with it.

00:19:22.861 --> 00:19:24.296
I'm doing what I can.

00:19:25.297 --> 00:19:26.965
And I don't think this is over

00:19:26.965 --> 00:19:28.433
by any stretch of
the imagination.

00:19:28.433 --> 00:19:30.435
I really think that the tide

00:19:30.435 --> 00:19:32.037 align:start size:81% position:19%
that swept through
the humanities,

00:19:32.037 --> 00:19:34.540 align:start size:88% position:13%
and the social sciences
is going to sweep

00:19:34.540 --> 00:19:39.478
through the STEM fields and
with far worse repercussions.

00:19:39.478 --> 00:19:40.879
- Well, you can feel it coming.

00:19:40.879 --> 00:19:42.781 align:start size:94% position:6%
I mean, you just mentioned
math two plus two

00:19:42.781 --> 00:19:44.283
no longer equals four.

00:19:44.283 --> 00:19:46.451
I mean, if we've lost
that, what do we got?

00:19:49.988 --> 00:19:53.458
- And the document itself
is a very strange document.

00:19:57.863 --> 00:19:59.298
I don't understand it.

00:19:59.298 --> 00:20:01.400
I don't understand it.

00:20:01.400 --> 00:20:05.837
- So I wanna ask you just
one or two more things

00:20:05.837 --> 00:20:08.740
relative to our past
and everything we did.

00:20:08.740 --> 00:20:10.075
And then I wanna
focus on the future

00:20:10.075 --> 00:20:12.477
and I wanna focus
on the new book.

00:20:12.477 --> 00:20:14.746 align:start size:94% position:6%
But two of the things that
you really moved me on,

00:20:14.746 --> 00:20:17.816
and I wrote about it in
my book, both of them.

00:20:17.816 --> 00:20:20.686
Well, one of them
was the core...

00:20:20.686 --> 00:20:23.422 align:start size:88% position:13%
The idea of having
something to believe in

00:20:23.422 --> 00:20:25.224
beyond just logic and reason.

00:20:25.224 --> 00:20:28.927 align:start position:0%
And this became you had
several debates with Sam Harris

00:20:28.927 --> 00:20:30.462
while in the midst of our tour.

00:20:30.462 --> 00:20:34.533
I mean, you left to go do
these incredible debates.

00:20:34.533 --> 00:20:37.169
But you completely
moved me on this one.

00:20:37.169 --> 00:20:40.839 align:start size:97% position:3%
I definitely came from the Sam
Harris or the Steven Pinker

00:20:40.839 --> 00:20:44.643 align:start size:97% position:3%
or the Jonathan Haidt
school of logic and reason.

00:20:44.643 --> 00:20:46.578 align:start size:84% position:16%
Are enough to
organize societies.

00:20:46.578 --> 00:20:49.214
And that's what humans have.

00:20:49.214 --> 00:20:51.283 align:start size:81% position:19%
And after 100 plus
shows with you,

00:20:51.283 --> 00:20:55.887 align:start size:97% position:3%
hearing it over and over
again, you moved me on that.

00:20:55.887 --> 00:20:57.356
Truly moved me on that.

00:20:57.356 --> 00:20:58.991
And I wonder if you think-

00:20:58.991 --> 00:21:01.293 align:start size:88% position:13%
- What's the consequence
of that been for you?

00:21:02.461 --> 00:21:06.698
- Well, A, I would say
that it's given me order

00:21:06.698 --> 00:21:09.668 align:start size:94% position:6%
in the sense that I
can't control everything.

00:21:09.668 --> 00:21:11.970
Not only can I not
control everything,

00:21:11.970 --> 00:21:14.406
but I think this may
sound a little strange.

00:21:14.406 --> 00:21:18.343
I think I have more respect for
the past, than I had before.

00:21:18.343 --> 00:21:20.746 align:start size:97% position:3%
Where I thought I could sort
of figure everything out.

00:21:20.746 --> 00:21:23.148 align:start size:94% position:6%
And figure out, certainly
what was best for me

00:21:23.148 --> 00:21:24.650
and everyone about me
and all those things.

00:21:24.650 --> 00:21:27.185
I still believe that, but
there's a context to it.

00:21:27.185 --> 00:21:28.086
I guess that's what it is.

00:21:28.086 --> 00:21:29.154
More than anything.

00:21:29.154 --> 00:21:30.689
It gave me a context.

00:21:30.689 --> 00:21:33.558
And a link to my ancestors.

00:21:33.558 --> 00:21:37.095 align:start position:0%
Who I don't think I'm
necessarily so much better than.

00:21:37.095 --> 00:21:38.497 align:start size:84% position:16%
You know, I think part
of the problem now

00:21:38.497 --> 00:21:40.165
is that everyone seems to think,

00:21:40.165 --> 00:21:42.034
"Oh, everyone who
came before you

00:21:42.034 --> 00:21:45.137 align:start size:88% position:13%
was racist or backwards
or un-evolved."

00:21:45.137 --> 00:21:47.372
And that the stories, I mean,

00:21:47.372 --> 00:21:49.308
look you did all the
biblical lectures,

00:21:49.308 --> 00:21:50.709
which I watched separately.

00:21:50.709 --> 00:21:54.279
And why did you always
talk about Pinocchio

00:21:54.279 --> 00:21:55.380
and wish upon a star.

00:21:55.380 --> 00:21:57.849
That there is something else

00:21:57.849 --> 00:22:01.787
that sits beneath our rationale.

00:22:01.787 --> 00:22:06.325 align:start size:97% position:3%
And without that, the
rational mind is sort of lost.

00:22:06.325 --> 00:22:09.061
You really moved me on that.

00:22:09.061 --> 00:22:11.363 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Yeah, and I certainly
haven't stopped

00:22:11.363 --> 00:22:13.031
believing that that's true.

00:22:15.400 --> 00:22:17.402 align:start size:84% position:16%
In some sense we
live inside a story.

00:22:17.402 --> 00:22:20.272
We live inside the hero
myth for better or worse.

00:22:20.272 --> 00:22:22.007
And that doesn't mean it's true.

00:22:22.007 --> 00:22:25.677 align:start size:88% position:13%
Because maybe the human
race isn't correct,

00:22:25.677 --> 00:22:27.012
in some final sense.

00:22:27.012 --> 00:22:29.815
But for better or worse
that's still our story

00:22:29.815 --> 00:22:32.084
to forge a path
into the unknown.

00:22:32.084 --> 00:22:35.754
To face the darkness, to
gather what's valuable,

00:22:35.754 --> 00:22:39.791
and to disseminate it
among our compatriots.

00:22:39.791 --> 00:22:42.094
That's us when we're
functioning properly.

00:22:42.094 --> 00:22:43.395
And if the more you do that,

00:22:43.395 --> 00:22:44.963 align:start size:84% position:16%
the more meaningful
your life is.

00:22:44.963 --> 00:22:47.933
And that seems not to
be a trivial matter.

00:22:47.933 --> 00:22:50.769 align:start size:94% position:6%
In fact, it's the absolute
opposite of trivial.

00:22:50.769 --> 00:22:53.305 line:0%
And I don't know what the
metaphysical significance

00:22:53.305 --> 00:22:54.272 line:0%
of that is.

00:22:54.272 --> 00:22:55.173 line:0%
And that's the final question.

00:22:55.173 --> 00:22:56.808 line:0%
The religious types.

00:22:56.808 --> 00:22:59.544 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
I talked to Jonathan
[indistinct] and
Orthodox icon carver.

00:22:59.544 --> 00:23:00.779
I've talked to him
quite a few times.

00:23:00.779 --> 00:23:03.048
He's very devout,
Orthodox Christian.

00:23:03.048 --> 00:23:04.783
Very very intelligent man.

00:23:06.218 --> 00:23:09.654 align:start size:97% position:3%
We've discussed the potential
metaphysical significance

00:23:09.654 --> 00:23:10.722
of the hero myth.

00:23:10.722 --> 00:23:12.657
You know, how does it tie into

00:23:12.657 --> 00:23:14.326
the ultimate nature of reality?

00:23:14.326 --> 00:23:17.028
And I don't have
an answer for that.

00:23:17.028 --> 00:23:21.333
The Christians believe that
the hero myth, so to speak

00:23:24.403 --> 00:23:27.472 align:start size:97% position:3%
was manifest in its totality
in the figure of Christ.

00:23:27.472 --> 00:23:31.843
And that's their explanation
for how the narrative world

00:23:31.843 --> 00:23:34.579
and the empirical world touch.

00:23:45.424 --> 00:23:47.159
And our whole culture
much of our culture

00:23:47.159 --> 00:23:49.027
is predicated on that idea.

00:23:49.027 --> 00:23:51.863 align:start size:88% position:13%
And it's a deep idea,
and it's not like I know

00:23:51.863 --> 00:23:56.001 align:start size:97% position:3%
how to cope with the
paradoxes that it produces.

00:23:56.001 --> 00:23:57.969 align:start size:84% position:16%
You know, I read
recently, for example

00:23:57.969 --> 00:23:59.404
many people will know this.

00:23:59.404 --> 00:24:04.142 align:start size:94% position:6%
That according to
current Catholic doctrine

00:24:05.343 --> 00:24:08.513
the theory of evolution
is entirely acceptable.

00:24:08.513 --> 00:24:10.182
I have no idea how
to integrate that

00:24:10.182 --> 00:24:12.851
with classic Christianity.

00:24:12.851 --> 00:24:14.953
The timeframes are so
radically different.

00:24:22.494 --> 00:24:25.063
But I also don't think
that the empirical types.

00:24:25.063 --> 00:24:26.498
The people that you mentioned,

00:24:26.498 --> 00:24:28.600 align:start size:94% position:6%
this is the constant
discussion I had with Sam.

00:24:28.600 --> 00:24:30.268
They haven't come to
terms with the fact

00:24:30.268 --> 00:24:33.572 align:start size:97% position:3%
that we see the world
through a structure of value

00:24:33.572 --> 00:24:35.574
and we can't get
outside of that.

00:24:35.574 --> 00:24:36.675
- So is that why-

00:24:36.675 --> 00:24:38.710
- Even to look at something.

00:24:40.145 --> 00:24:41.980
- You said to me from the
day we started the tour

00:24:41.980 --> 00:24:44.382
and you said it to me on
almost any given night.

00:24:45.750 --> 00:24:47.586 align:start position:0%
You didn't wanna know the
questions that I was gonna ask.

00:24:47.586 --> 00:24:49.688
You would always say
nothing's off limits.

00:24:50.856 --> 00:24:52.090 align:start size:84% position:16%
Well, actually, what
you would usually

00:24:52.090 --> 00:24:53.992
say to me is have fun
or have fun tonight,

00:24:53.992 --> 00:24:55.227
something like that.

00:24:55.227 --> 00:24:57.596
But I knew that the only
question that I sensed

00:24:57.596 --> 00:24:59.631 align:start size:88% position:13%
and I probably only
asked you once or twice

00:24:59.631 --> 00:25:01.099
once I realized it.

00:25:01.099 --> 00:25:04.669
The only question that I
sensed you didn't wanna do,

00:25:04.669 --> 00:25:06.905 align:start size:84% position:16%
because it would take
up too much time,

00:25:06.905 --> 00:25:08.440
sort of was this.

00:25:08.440 --> 00:25:10.442
Because when we would do the
Q&amp;A, and we did it by an app

00:25:10.442 --> 00:25:13.245 align:start size:94% position:6%
so people in the audience
could vote on them.

00:25:13.245 --> 00:25:14.980
There would often be the, okay.

00:25:14.980 --> 00:25:17.148
So do you believe in God?

00:25:17.148 --> 00:25:18.617
What does that mean?

00:25:18.617 --> 00:25:20.318 align:start size:94% position:6%
And it's sort of what
you're talking about here.

00:25:20.318 --> 00:25:22.354
Once you whittle it all down.

00:25:23.655 --> 00:25:25.790
We could do this for
the next six hours,

00:25:25.790 --> 00:25:27.392
if not the rest of our lives.

00:25:29.861 --> 00:25:33.498
- Well I've said
a lot about that.

00:25:33.498 --> 00:25:36.868 align:start position:0%
I wouldn't say that my
thinking has really progressed.

00:25:36.868 --> 00:25:40.572 align:start size:94% position:6%
I mean, beyond what I
had thought about before.

00:25:40.572 --> 00:25:43.775 align:start size:88% position:13%
I try to conduct myself
as if God exists.

00:25:45.343 --> 00:25:48.313
I try to conduct myself
as if being is good.

00:25:49.414 --> 00:25:50.916
That's faith, I suppose.

00:25:50.916 --> 00:25:54.119 align:start size:97% position:3%
And I think to some degree,
that's also a kind of courage.

00:25:54.119 --> 00:25:56.955 align:start size:84% position:16%
I mean, I'm not
claiming that my faith

00:25:56.955 --> 00:25:58.490
is entirely courageous.

00:25:58.490 --> 00:26:02.827
Because I waiver, of course,
and I've especially wavered.

00:26:02.827 --> 00:26:04.396 align:start size:81% position:19%
I would say in the
last two years,

00:26:04.396 --> 00:26:06.031
given everything
that's happened.

00:26:06.031 --> 00:26:10.902
All the catastrophe
that's rained down
upon my family and I.

00:26:12.871 --> 00:26:17.876 align:start size:88% position:13%
But I still believe that
you become corrupted.

00:26:18.944 --> 00:26:21.212 align:start size:88% position:13%
If you become resentful
about existence.

00:26:23.481 --> 00:26:26.518
You have to act as
if existence is good.

00:26:27.385 --> 00:26:29.487
And that the truth is good.

00:26:31.823 --> 00:26:33.592
Because otherwise
things get worse.

00:26:34.993 --> 00:26:39.164
And so there's a limit.

00:26:40.765 --> 00:26:43.001
There's limits past
which no one can see.

00:26:44.469 --> 00:26:47.005
And the questions that we're
discussing are at the limit.

00:26:50.108 --> 00:26:51.610 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Yeah, well that's
it right there.

00:26:51.610 --> 00:26:53.645
The answer is not clean.

00:26:53.645 --> 00:26:56.114 align:start size:84% position:16%
And I think everyone,
or so many of us

00:26:56.114 --> 00:26:58.249 align:start size:84% position:16%
seem to want the
clean answer on that.

00:26:58.249 --> 00:27:00.652 align:start size:88% position:13%
When you had those
debates with Sam, like,

00:27:00.652 --> 00:27:02.187 align:start size:84% position:16%
"Oh that's the answer,
that's the winner,

00:27:02.187 --> 00:27:04.122
that's the knockout blow."

00:27:04.122 --> 00:27:07.759 align:start position:0%
And I think you're acknowledging
an uncomfortable truth.

00:27:08.960 --> 00:27:10.495 align:start size:81% position:19%
- A friend of mine
the other day.

00:27:10.495 --> 00:27:14.766
And he was raised at communist
in Poland and was an atheist.

00:27:14.766 --> 00:27:17.736
And he went after
his family one year

00:27:17.736 --> 00:27:19.471
for celebrating Christmas,

00:27:19.471 --> 00:27:23.108 align:start position:0%
because it was logically
inconsistent with their beliefs.

00:27:24.142 --> 00:27:25.677
Which it was.

00:27:27.579 --> 00:27:29.848
But what he told
me when we walked

00:27:29.848 --> 00:27:31.983
was that he came to
realize at one point

00:27:31.983 --> 00:27:35.487
that all that meant was that
they would lose Christmas.

00:27:37.722 --> 00:27:39.991 align:start size:84% position:16%
Yeah, it was logically
inconsistent,

00:27:39.991 --> 00:27:44.796 align:start size:84% position:16%
but what are you gonna
replace this with?

00:27:45.930 --> 00:27:47.732 align:start size:88% position:13%
And it's clearly the
case that people need...

00:27:47.732 --> 00:27:50.602 align:start size:84% position:16%
You need beliefs to
organize your actions.

00:27:50.602 --> 00:27:51.870
And then I see well what happens

00:27:51.870 --> 00:27:53.204
when we don't turn

00:27:53.204 --> 00:27:55.273
to more traditional
forms of belief,

00:27:55.273 --> 00:27:59.811
as we turn to forms of belief
that are far more dangerous.

00:27:59.811 --> 00:28:02.080 align:start size:84% position:16%
Or at least that lurks
as a possibility.

00:28:04.149 --> 00:28:07.352
I also don't think it's that
easy for people to defend...

00:28:07.352 --> 00:28:09.054 align:start size:84% position:16%
Like the rationalist,
for example.

00:28:09.054 --> 00:28:12.524 align:start size:97% position:3%
They're not doing a great
job of defending the culture.

00:28:12.524 --> 00:28:14.092
It doesn't sell well.

00:28:14.092 --> 00:28:16.428 align:start size:94% position:6%
- No, they're doing a
horrible job, I would say.

00:28:16.428 --> 00:28:18.563
I would say they're
doing almost no job.

00:28:18.563 --> 00:28:22.734
That woke ism is in
many ways the end

00:28:22.734 --> 00:28:27.739
of the purely rational, and
the purely secular order

00:28:29.174 --> 00:28:33.278
ends with what we're seeing
now, in some ways I think.

00:28:33.278 --> 00:28:36.114
- Well, let's hope not,

00:28:37.549 --> 00:28:40.418
but it it doesn't seem to
be losing its momentum.

00:28:41.386 --> 00:28:42.587
I don't think.

00:28:42.587 --> 00:28:46.524
I mean, it's so hard
to get an accurate take

00:28:46.524 --> 00:28:48.293
on the movement of the culture.

00:28:51.496 --> 00:28:54.933 align:start size:94% position:6%
It still seems to me that
the education systems

00:28:54.933 --> 00:28:58.603
are being corrupted,
from top to bottom.

00:28:58.603 --> 00:28:59.971 align:start size:84% position:16%
You know, the
University of Kansas

00:28:59.971 --> 00:29:02.741
closed its humanities
department a week ago.

00:29:02.741 --> 00:29:04.509 align:start size:97% position:3%
Well, that's partly because
people aren't enrolling

00:29:04.509 --> 00:29:05.543
in the humanities anymore.

00:29:05.543 --> 00:29:07.245
So maybe that's...

00:29:07.245 --> 00:29:08.880
Maybe the market in some sense

00:29:08.880 --> 00:29:10.115
will correct some of this.

00:29:10.115 --> 00:29:14.753
But it's proving very
difficult for defenders

00:29:16.221 --> 00:29:20.492
of enlightenment values to
put up an effective fight

00:29:20.492 --> 00:29:24.462
against this assault on
the idea of competence.

00:29:25.830 --> 00:29:29.434 align:start size:97% position:3%
There's nothing solid enough
there for some reason.

00:29:29.434 --> 00:29:33.571
And well, so here we are.

00:29:33.571 --> 00:29:34.873
- That's it.

00:29:34.873 --> 00:29:37.308
That's what belief sits upon.

00:29:37.308 --> 00:29:38.777
All right, well, you know what.

00:29:38.777 --> 00:29:40.178
Yeah, go ahead.

00:29:40.178 --> 00:29:41.846
Well, let's dive into the book.

00:29:41.846 --> 00:29:43.047
Because actually so
many of the things

00:29:43.047 --> 00:29:45.250 align:start size:88% position:13%
that we're talking about
now are sort of part

00:29:45.250 --> 00:29:47.252
and parcel to all of
the chapters here.

00:29:47.252 --> 00:29:49.988
So chapter one, and what I
really liked about this also

00:29:49.988 --> 00:29:51.556
is the way you organized it.

00:29:52.423 --> 00:29:53.992
The new topics.

00:29:53.992 --> 00:29:55.827 align:start size:97% position:3%
Because one of the
questions we would get often

00:29:55.827 --> 00:29:57.695
in the Q&amp;A that
you would get is,

00:29:57.695 --> 00:30:00.198
"Oh, was there a 13th rule

00:30:00.198 --> 00:30:01.599
that you couldn't
get in the book?

00:30:01.599 --> 00:30:03.368 align:start size:94% position:6%
And you actually mentioned
this in the book.

00:30:03.368 --> 00:30:04.402
That you had...

00:30:04.402 --> 00:30:05.804
You had what?

00:30:05.804 --> 00:30:07.105
About 46 other, was it 46?

00:30:07.105 --> 00:30:07.939
- I had 42?

00:30:07.939 --> 00:30:08.773
- 42.

00:30:08.773 --> 00:30:09.774
You had 42.

00:30:09.774 --> 00:30:11.276
- Yeah, the same
as Douglas Adams

00:30:11.276 --> 00:30:13.011
Answer to The Universe, life,
the universe and everything.

00:30:13.011 --> 00:30:14.345
42 rules.

00:30:14.345 --> 00:30:16.014 align:start size:81% position:19%
Which was actually
a coincidence,

00:30:16.014 --> 00:30:18.449 align:start size:94% position:6%
but I thought it was
comical in the aftermath.

00:30:18.449 --> 00:30:19.450
There's 42 rules.

00:30:19.450 --> 00:30:20.685
So now I've written about 20...

00:30:20.685 --> 00:30:23.421 align:start size:84% position:16%
I've written essays
on 24 of them.

00:30:23.421 --> 00:30:25.423 align:start size:88% position:13%
- When you got to 42,
how long did you give it

00:30:25.423 --> 00:30:27.926
before you said,
okay 42 is enough.

00:30:27.926 --> 00:30:29.294 align:start size:94% position:6%
Because I know you
you're pretty methodical.

00:30:29.294 --> 00:30:31.763
- Yeah, I just did
it in an afternoon.

00:30:31.763 --> 00:30:34.599 align:start size:94% position:6%
The original list of rules
I was playing on Quora.

00:30:36.100 --> 00:30:39.070
I wrote about 40 answers for
Quora, something like that.

00:30:39.070 --> 00:30:42.240
And I really haven't
partaken in that forum

00:30:42.240 --> 00:30:44.776
for a long time now.

00:30:44.776 --> 00:30:49.013
I was investigating it
and some kid had asked,

00:30:49.013 --> 00:30:51.816
what do you need to know in
order to lead a good life?

00:30:51.816 --> 00:30:54.018
Or what's most important to
know something like that.

00:30:54.018 --> 00:30:56.120
And I thought, well, you know

00:30:56.120 --> 00:30:57.388
I'll take a crack
at answering this.

00:30:57.388 --> 00:30:58.857
And I made this
list of 42 rules,

00:30:58.857 --> 00:31:01.593
and it got very
popular on Quora.

00:31:01.593 --> 00:31:04.796 align:start size:94% position:6%
Much more popular, typical
Pareto Distribution.

00:31:04.796 --> 00:31:06.931 align:start size:84% position:16%
Like that one answer
got more views

00:31:06.931 --> 00:31:09.934
than all of my other
answers put together.

00:31:09.934 --> 00:31:11.936 align:start size:88% position:13%
And so I thought that
was kind of interesting.

00:31:11.936 --> 00:31:14.272 align:start size:88% position:13%
I had touched something
for some reason.

00:31:15.874 --> 00:31:18.877
And out of that, when
I was asked by an agent

00:31:18.877 --> 00:31:20.712
who contacted me, Sally Harding.

00:31:24.716 --> 00:31:26.251
Of Cook Agency.

00:31:27.151 --> 00:31:28.720
She asked me if I was interested

00:31:28.720 --> 00:31:30.154 align:start size:84% position:16%
in writing something
more popular.

00:31:30.154 --> 00:31:33.391 align:start size:97% position:3%
And I knew that those rules
had found an audience.

00:31:33.391 --> 00:31:35.827
So that seemed to me to
be a place to dive in.

00:31:37.228 --> 00:31:40.031 align:start size:94% position:6%
So anyways, I've only
written about 24 of them.

00:31:40.031 --> 00:31:42.033
In the two books, I
have them both here.

00:31:42.033 --> 00:31:45.436 align:start size:84% position:16%
You can see how
they're structured.

00:31:45.436 --> 00:31:46.704
- Yeah, it's beautiful.

00:31:46.704 --> 00:31:47.538
- One's white, and
the other is black.

00:31:47.538 --> 00:31:48.840
They make a match set.

00:31:48.840 --> 00:31:50.241
You can read each of
them independently.

00:31:50.241 --> 00:31:51.542
And one concentrates.

00:31:51.542 --> 00:31:56.481
The first one, An
Antidote to Chaos.

00:31:57.682 --> 00:31:59.450
12 Rules for Life:
An Antidote to Chaos

00:31:59.450 --> 00:32:03.988
does concentrate on
the consequences of
excess uncertainty.

00:32:03.988 --> 00:32:05.723
And the second book concentrates

00:32:05.723 --> 00:32:09.260
more on an excess of order.

00:32:09.260 --> 00:32:11.963 align:start size:97% position:3%
Both of those are fundamental
existential dangers,

00:32:11.963 --> 00:32:13.431
as far as I'm concerned.

00:32:13.431 --> 00:32:16.968 align:start size:97% position:3%
And in this universe of
value in the world of value.

00:32:16.968 --> 00:32:18.670
There are two major domains

00:32:18.670 --> 00:32:21.205 align:start size:84% position:16%
and one domain is
the domain of order.

00:32:21.205 --> 00:32:23.608 align:start size:84% position:16%
And you can
technically define it.

00:32:23.608 --> 00:32:26.878
The domain of order is
where you find yourself,

00:32:26.878 --> 00:32:31.816 align:start size:97% position:3%
when what you're doing
produces the results you want.

00:32:31.816 --> 00:32:34.352
And that's a really
tight formulation.

00:32:34.352 --> 00:32:38.222
Because it gives you
a particular idea,

00:32:38.222 --> 00:32:41.392
of what a place in time is.

00:32:41.392 --> 00:32:44.629 align:start size:94% position:6%
A place in time you
occupy at any given moment

00:32:44.629 --> 00:32:48.333
is the place and time that's
defined by your current goal.

00:32:48.333 --> 00:32:52.270 align:start size:97% position:3%
And you have a map of value
that guides you through

00:32:52.270 --> 00:32:54.405 align:start size:94% position:6%
the actions that are
necessary in that domain.

00:32:54.405 --> 00:32:56.574
And if the result
is what you want,

00:32:56.574 --> 00:32:59.344
which brings motivation and
emotion into the picture,

00:32:59.344 --> 00:33:01.913 align:start size:84% position:16%
then while you get
what you need or want.

00:33:01.913 --> 00:33:05.316 align:start size:94% position:6%
But you also validate
your theory of existence.

00:33:05.316 --> 00:33:07.018
Because it's good
enough to produce

00:33:07.018 --> 00:33:08.720
the results that you desire.

00:33:08.720 --> 00:33:10.355
And given that you're fallible

00:33:10.355 --> 00:33:12.457
and that you don't
know everything,

00:33:12.457 --> 00:33:14.926
you have to use proximal...

00:33:14.926 --> 00:33:17.662
You have to use proximal truths.

00:33:17.662 --> 00:33:20.398
And so something
it's a pragmatic...

00:33:20.398 --> 00:33:22.800 align:start size:94% position:6%
It's a form of
philosophical pragmatism.

00:33:24.902 --> 00:33:26.938 align:start size:84% position:16%
If you make a bridge
and it stands up,

00:33:26.938 --> 00:33:29.107
then you know how
to make a bridge.

00:33:29.107 --> 00:33:30.341
Why?

00:33:30.341 --> 00:33:31.809
Because the bridge stood up.

00:33:31.809 --> 00:33:33.211
Now, maybe you overbuilt it.

00:33:33.211 --> 00:33:34.746
You could have built
it more elegantly.

00:33:34.746 --> 00:33:37.982
But it's sufficiently true,
so that the bridge functions.

00:33:37.982 --> 00:33:40.718 line:0%
And we're like that,
we're like engineers.

00:33:40.718 --> 00:33:42.720 line:0%
We're cobbling together
solutions all the time.

00:33:42.720 --> 00:33:44.389 align:start size:84% position:16%
And as long as those
solutions work,

00:33:44.389 --> 00:33:46.624
we assume that we're right.

00:33:46.624 --> 00:33:48.826
Well, that's the
domain of order.

00:33:48.826 --> 00:33:50.361
The domain of chaos emerges

00:33:50.361 --> 00:33:53.898
when you lay out a
plan and into action,

00:33:53.898 --> 00:33:56.934
and something other than
what you wanted emerges.

00:33:56.934 --> 00:33:59.337
And sometimes that
can be a catastrophe.

00:33:59.337 --> 00:34:00.738
An absolute catastrophe.

00:34:00.738 --> 00:34:05.710
And your brain, our
psychophysiology

00:34:07.478 --> 00:34:10.415
is actually adapted
to those two domains.

00:34:12.016 --> 00:34:14.018 align:start size:84% position:16%
When something
unexpected happens,

00:34:15.453 --> 00:34:17.822
all sorts of emotions and
motivations break loose.

00:34:17.822 --> 00:34:19.290
Fight and flight among them.

00:34:19.290 --> 00:34:20.224
Anger among them.

00:34:20.224 --> 00:34:21.492
It gets disinhibited.

00:34:21.492 --> 00:34:23.995
Because when you don't
know what's happening,

00:34:23.995 --> 00:34:26.064 align:start size:84% position:16%
you have to prepare
for everything.

00:34:26.064 --> 00:34:29.033
So you get anxious and
then you hyper prepare,

00:34:29.033 --> 00:34:31.002
which is extremely stressful.

00:34:31.002 --> 00:34:33.971 align:start size:94% position:6%
And so the domain of chaos
is extremely stressful.

00:34:35.139 --> 00:34:37.909 align:start size:81% position:19%
In small doses,
it's exhilarating.

00:34:37.909 --> 00:34:41.479 align:start size:88% position:13%
And that's because,
well when you're aware,

00:34:41.479 --> 00:34:43.081
you don't know what's happening,

00:34:43.081 --> 00:34:47.919
you have the opportunity to
learn and to expand your map.

00:34:47.919 --> 00:34:49.821 align:start size:84% position:16%
And so there's
always an interplay,

00:34:49.821 --> 00:34:51.756
between the domain of chaos
and the domain of order.

00:34:51.756 --> 00:34:53.324
But they can each pathologize.

00:34:53.324 --> 00:34:56.360 align:start position:0%
And the pathologies of
uncertainty are more associated,

00:34:56.360 --> 00:34:58.696 align:start size:88% position:13%
with anxiety and
nihilism and depression.

00:34:58.696 --> 00:35:02.867
And where's the pathologies
of order more totalitarian.

00:35:02.867 --> 00:35:06.771
And then I would say as
well, the liberal types

00:35:06.771 --> 00:35:09.407 align:start size:94% position:6%
the more left leaning
types are quite sensitive

00:35:09.407 --> 00:35:11.742
to pathologies of order.

00:35:13.111 --> 00:35:14.479
They don't like them.

00:35:14.479 --> 00:35:16.747
That's the patriarchy.

00:35:16.747 --> 00:35:20.151
The patriarchy is the
pathology of order.

00:35:20.151 --> 00:35:21.719
And it's symbolically masculine.

00:35:21.719 --> 00:35:25.723 align:start size:94% position:6%
Something I've been taken
to task for claiming.

00:35:25.723 --> 00:35:28.059
But the patriarchy itself.

00:35:28.059 --> 00:35:31.229 align:start size:94% position:6%
The idea of the patriarchy
itself is a symbol.

00:35:31.229 --> 00:35:32.930
The patriarchy is a symbol.

00:35:32.930 --> 00:35:34.732
That's why it has such power.

00:35:34.732 --> 00:35:38.035 align:start size:97% position:3%
And it's a symbol that refers
to the domain of order.

00:35:38.035 --> 00:35:40.938 align:start size:84% position:16%
Now the domain of
order is protective
as well as oppressive.

00:35:40.938 --> 00:35:44.142
But when it degenerates,
it becomes oppressive.

00:35:44.142 --> 00:35:46.310 align:start size:97% position:3%
And I would say it
degenerates when it's based

00:35:46.310 --> 00:35:48.546
on power rather than competence.

00:35:48.546 --> 00:35:50.948 align:start size:84% position:16%
But it can be based
on competence.

00:35:52.383 --> 00:35:57.288
The Marxist critics and the
politically correct types,

00:35:58.689 --> 00:36:02.493
they insist that every
element of the patriarchy

00:36:02.493 --> 00:36:05.763
is only a consequence of
the imposition of order.

00:36:05.763 --> 00:36:07.131
Forceful imposition of order.

00:36:07.131 --> 00:36:08.533
It's all power.

00:36:08.533 --> 00:36:10.168
Well, no.

00:36:10.168 --> 00:36:11.302
No it's not.

00:36:11.302 --> 00:36:12.904 align:start size:84% position:16%
When it degenerates,
that's true.

00:36:14.772 --> 00:36:17.708
You can tell that because the
thing is the domain of order

00:36:17.708 --> 00:36:22.180 align:start size:97% position:3%
will be upheld by those who
inhabit it if it's functional.

00:36:22.180 --> 00:36:25.149 align:start size:97% position:3%
If you have to use force,
that's already an indication

00:36:25.149 --> 00:36:27.185
that it's become pathological.

00:36:27.185 --> 00:36:29.987
Because people aren't
playing voluntarily.

00:36:29.987 --> 00:36:34.992
- So, would you say we're in a
degenerative cycle right now.

00:36:36.227 --> 00:36:38.963 align:start size:84% position:16%
That the cycle seems
more degenerative

00:36:38.963 --> 00:36:40.898
than say a building cycle.

00:36:40.898 --> 00:36:44.335
Or you think that that's just
the play that's always going

00:36:44.335 --> 00:36:47.605 align:start size:94% position:6%
on and you have to figure
out your role in it?

00:36:47.605 --> 00:36:49.807 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Well, I think the
play is always going on.

00:36:51.309 --> 00:36:53.477 align:start size:81% position:19%
The antidote to
chaos isn't order.

00:36:53.477 --> 00:36:55.546
And the antidote to
order isn't chaos,

00:36:55.546 --> 00:36:58.182
the antidote to both is
the balancing of them.

00:36:58.182 --> 00:37:00.251
The act of balancing of them.

00:37:00.251 --> 00:37:02.153
- You used to do this a lot.

00:37:02.153 --> 00:37:05.389
You would say the balance,
the struggle between liberal,

00:37:05.389 --> 00:37:06.591
and conservative.

00:37:06.591 --> 00:37:07.558 align:start size:84% position:16%
So people see me doing
this all the time.

00:37:07.558 --> 00:37:08.492
I always [indistinct].

00:37:08.492 --> 00:37:09.560
Jordan was talking about...

00:37:09.560 --> 00:37:11.062
He was talking about this.

00:37:11.062 --> 00:37:12.363
So you think that's it

00:37:12.363 --> 00:37:14.699 align:start size:88% position:13%
- It's really
important to understand

00:37:14.699 --> 00:37:19.470 align:start size:94% position:6%
that the antidote to chaos
in the final analysis

00:37:19.470 --> 00:37:20.771
can't just be ordered.

00:37:20.771 --> 00:37:23.140 align:start size:84% position:16%
Because order itself
can degenerate.

00:37:23.140 --> 00:37:27.478 align:start size:97% position:3%
And so I believe that the
antidote is active engagement

00:37:27.478 --> 00:37:28.613
with the world.

00:37:28.613 --> 00:37:30.381 align:start size:88% position:13%
Honest active engagement
with the world.

00:37:30.381 --> 00:37:31.616
Truth.

00:37:31.616 --> 00:37:33.884 align:start size:88% position:13%
And I think it's also
truth motivated by love.

00:37:33.884 --> 00:37:35.353
Which is a motif

00:37:35.353 --> 00:37:37.188 align:start size:94% position:6%
that runs through this
second book in particular.

00:37:37.188 --> 00:37:42.193 align:start size:94% position:6%
And love is the desire for
all things to flourish.

00:37:44.095 --> 00:37:46.230 align:start size:94% position:6%
Christ says that you
should love your enemies.

00:37:46.230 --> 00:37:50.301
And what does that mean?

00:37:50.301 --> 00:37:51.836 align:start size:84% position:16%
And it's really
worth thinking about.

00:37:51.836 --> 00:37:54.739 align:start size:88% position:13%
It's you shouldn't
wish your enemies harm.

00:37:57.808 --> 00:38:00.645
What I mean by that is that it
would be better for everyone

00:38:00.645 --> 00:38:02.980
if they would conduct themselves

00:38:02.980 --> 00:38:04.782
so that they would flourish.

00:38:06.951 --> 00:38:08.753
And that doesn't mean you
shouldn't defend yourself.

00:38:08.753 --> 00:38:11.422 align:start size:94% position:6%
It doesn't mean that
soldiers aren't necessary

00:38:11.422 --> 00:38:12.356
or the police.

00:38:12.356 --> 00:38:13.357
It doesn't mean any of that.

00:38:13.357 --> 00:38:14.725
It's not a weak need statement.

00:38:14.725 --> 00:38:16.193
It means that if you're...

00:38:16.193 --> 00:38:20.898 align:start size:97% position:3%
If you have yourself pointed
in the right direction,

00:38:24.001 --> 00:38:28.272 align:start size:88% position:13%
you don't wish an excess
of harm on the world.

00:38:28.272 --> 00:38:29.407
You want it to flourish.

00:38:29.407 --> 00:38:30.608
That's love.

00:38:30.608 --> 00:38:32.443 align:start size:81% position:19%
So you weren't
yourself that way.

00:38:32.443 --> 00:38:34.812
And I do believe that that
requires a certain courage.

00:38:34.812 --> 00:38:38.015
Because the world is so
flawed and so painful.

00:38:38.015 --> 00:38:39.650
There's so much suffering in it.

00:38:39.650 --> 00:38:41.986
That it's very difficult
to fall in love with it.

00:38:41.986 --> 00:38:43.487
You keep getting bounced off.

00:38:43.487 --> 00:38:44.789
You think this is so terrible

00:38:44.789 --> 00:38:47.058
that maybe it shouldn't even be.

00:38:47.058 --> 00:38:49.460
But that takes you
down a very bad road.

00:38:49.460 --> 00:38:50.695
So it's love first.

00:38:50.695 --> 00:38:53.030
And then truth serves that.

00:38:53.030 --> 00:38:54.665
And I do think that's the motif

00:38:54.665 --> 00:38:57.034
that runs through the
old and new testaments.

00:38:57.034 --> 00:38:59.337
The combination of
those two things.

00:38:59.337 --> 00:39:03.040
Love is the desire,
that being flourish.

00:39:04.175 --> 00:39:08.346
And I do believe that
truth serves that.

00:39:09.847 --> 00:39:12.149
(indistinct chatter)

00:39:12.149 --> 00:39:13.584
- Please finish your thought.

00:39:15.219 --> 00:39:17.955 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Well, I also think that
people find meaning in that.

00:39:19.757 --> 00:39:21.792 align:start size:88% position:13%
And everyone can answer
this for them self.

00:39:21.792 --> 00:39:24.695
It's like you have to watch,
and you have to see where it is

00:39:24.695 --> 00:39:26.864
that you find the meaning
that sustains your life.

00:39:26.864 --> 00:39:29.600 align:start position:0%
And I would say, it's certainly
not been my experience

00:39:29.600 --> 00:39:32.603
that people find that
in deceit or hatred.

00:39:33.771 --> 00:39:35.039
I mean they may be
tempted by that.

00:39:35.039 --> 00:39:37.007
They may have the reasons for it

00:39:37.007 --> 00:39:41.512
but everyone is ashamed of that,

00:39:41.512 --> 00:39:44.148 align:start size:84% position:16%
and wishes it
could be otherwise.

00:39:44.148 --> 00:39:46.450
Even if they don't know
what to do about that.

00:39:47.718 --> 00:39:51.455 align:start size:84% position:16%
So well, so this book,
the first chapter

00:39:56.327 --> 00:39:59.563 align:start size:97% position:3%
is do not casually denigrate
social institutions

00:39:59.563 --> 00:40:00.798
or creative achievement.

00:40:00.798 --> 00:40:03.033
And I picked that
quite carefully

00:40:03.033 --> 00:40:05.903 align:start size:94% position:6%
because again, the liberal
types are more likely

00:40:05.903 --> 00:40:07.872 align:start size:84% position:16%
to criticize social
institutions.

00:40:11.342 --> 00:40:12.777
So you don't wanna
do that casually

00:40:12.777 --> 00:40:15.312 align:start size:97% position:3%
because they structure things
and protect you in a way

00:40:15.312 --> 00:40:18.382
that you're likely
not even aware of.

00:40:18.382 --> 00:40:19.917 align:start size:84% position:16%
And the conservative
always says,

00:40:19.917 --> 00:40:22.920
look be careful when
you change something.

00:40:22.920 --> 00:40:24.288 align:start size:88% position:13%
Because you're changing
a bunch of things

00:40:24.288 --> 00:40:26.624 align:start size:88% position:13%
and you don't know
what's going to happen.

00:40:26.624 --> 00:40:27.992
So be careful.

00:40:27.992 --> 00:40:30.194 align:start size:88% position:13%
But social institutions
can become corrupt

00:40:30.194 --> 00:40:32.963 align:start size:84% position:16%
even just as a
consequence of aging.

00:40:32.963 --> 00:40:34.331
And so they have to be updated.

00:40:34.331 --> 00:40:36.167
So they can't stay static.

00:40:36.167 --> 00:40:38.536
But that doesn't mean you
shouldn't respect them.

00:40:38.536 --> 00:40:40.471 align:start size:97% position:3%
And then creative
achievements on the other side

00:40:40.471 --> 00:40:42.006
and conservatives, for example

00:40:42.006 --> 00:40:46.877
they have a harder time with
open people, creative people.

00:40:46.877 --> 00:40:50.181
The best personality
predictor of liberalism

00:40:50.181 --> 00:40:51.148
is high openness.

00:40:51.148 --> 00:40:52.716
Which is a creativity dimension.

00:40:52.716 --> 00:40:56.954 align:start size:94% position:6%
Well, it's easy to
dismiss art, for example.

00:40:58.656 --> 00:41:00.591 align:start size:88% position:13%
Especially if it doesn't
exactly speak to you.

00:41:00.591 --> 00:41:02.693
But it's through
artistic endeavors,

00:41:02.693 --> 00:41:03.928
through creative achievement,

00:41:03.928 --> 00:41:06.864
that the process
of update occurs.

00:41:06.864 --> 00:41:10.701
And so regardless of your
political temperament,

00:41:10.701 --> 00:41:14.705
you need to see these forces...

00:41:14.705 --> 00:41:15.940
You need to see the value

00:41:15.940 --> 00:41:18.476
in these forces and have
some respect for them.

00:41:18.476 --> 00:41:20.177
I think what happens
if you get educated,

00:41:20.177 --> 00:41:21.912
hopefully is that
you get educated

00:41:21.912 --> 00:41:24.915
beyond the confines
of your temperament.

00:41:28.786 --> 00:41:30.287 align:start size:84% position:16%
- So that's why I
thought the ordering,

00:41:30.287 --> 00:41:32.656 align:start size:97% position:3%
it's so interesting that you
said you intentionally...

00:41:32.656 --> 00:41:34.391
of course intentionally did it.

00:41:34.391 --> 00:41:36.894 align:start size:97% position:3%
So do not carelessly
denigrate social institutions

00:41:36.894 --> 00:41:37.728
or creative achievements.

00:41:37.728 --> 00:41:38.996
So that's chapter one.

00:41:38.996 --> 00:41:40.898
And I thought it was
interesting because,

00:41:40.898 --> 00:41:42.867
one of the things that
I've been talking about

00:41:42.867 --> 00:41:44.235
for the last couple of years,

00:41:44.235 --> 00:41:45.769
but especially in the last year.

00:41:45.769 --> 00:41:48.005
And our friend Ben Shapiro
wrote a whole book about it,

00:41:48.005 --> 00:41:52.176
is the disintegration of so
many of our institutions.

00:41:52.176 --> 00:41:55.179
And that I think the
great debate right now

00:41:55.179 --> 00:41:58.182
is can some of these
institutions survive,

00:41:58.182 --> 00:42:00.284
or do we need all
new institutions?

00:42:00.284 --> 00:42:02.286
Now, I know you're talking
about social institutions,

00:42:02.286 --> 00:42:04.655
not just academic institutions,

00:42:05.890 --> 00:42:07.758
or we're talking about
cultural institutions.

00:42:07.758 --> 00:42:10.761
What do you feel
in that argument?

00:42:10.761 --> 00:42:14.532 align:start size:97% position:3%
Whether can some things
just be left to disintegrate

00:42:14.532 --> 00:42:19.169
and we rebuild, or do
we constantly end up

00:42:19.169 --> 00:42:21.839
in a destruction and a rebirth?

00:42:23.240 --> 00:42:25.509 align:start size:97% position:3%
Because right now we're
watching so many institutions

00:42:25.509 --> 00:42:27.011
just crumble.

00:42:27.011 --> 00:42:31.215
- Well, I tend to start
local when I'm thinking.

00:42:32.416 --> 00:42:33.918
Because it simplifies things.

00:42:33.918 --> 00:42:36.554
Well the first institution
is the sovereign individual.

00:42:36.554 --> 00:42:38.155
We don't wanna let that crumble.

00:42:41.325 --> 00:42:44.595 align:start size:97% position:3%
And the more that you're able
to live in a relationship

00:42:44.595 --> 00:42:48.532 align:start size:97% position:3%
with truth, I would say
the better job you're doing

00:42:48.532 --> 00:42:50.568
of protecting your integrity

00:42:50.568 --> 00:42:52.770 align:start size:94% position:6%
as a sovereign individual,
start with that.

00:42:54.071 --> 00:42:56.140
It would be a shame
to lose the family.

00:42:58.442 --> 00:43:00.044
People derive a
tremendous amount

00:43:00.044 --> 00:43:02.212
of the meaning of their
life from their family.

00:43:02.212 --> 00:43:04.048 align:start size:84% position:16%
And then those
intense relationships.

00:43:04.048 --> 00:43:06.784 align:start size:84% position:16%
I don't think we
can get beyond that.

00:43:09.019 --> 00:43:12.256 align:start size:88% position:13%
I think you have to knit
your family together,

00:43:12.256 --> 00:43:13.624
to the best of your ability.

00:43:13.624 --> 00:43:15.259
And I know that people

00:43:15.259 --> 00:43:17.094
often have terribly
fractured families.

00:43:17.094 --> 00:43:19.363 align:start size:88% position:13%
But we don't have a good
substitute for that.

00:43:22.600 --> 00:43:25.369 align:start size:97% position:3%
You need to exist in
relationship to your culture.

00:43:25.369 --> 00:43:28.305 align:start size:94% position:6%
You need a job or a career
or something like that.

00:43:28.305 --> 00:43:30.274
We need political institutions.

00:43:32.409 --> 00:43:34.411
I think part of the
problem of course,

00:43:34.411 --> 00:43:36.914
is that everything is
changing so rapidly,

00:43:36.914 --> 00:43:38.682
that it's very difficult to...

00:43:40.351 --> 00:43:42.386
It's very difficult to
say what should be kept

00:43:42.386 --> 00:43:44.121
and what shouldn't be.

00:43:44.121 --> 00:43:47.491
And we're not in control of
it to some degree as well.

00:43:48.659 --> 00:43:50.327 align:start size:81% position:19%
I mean, there's
an all out assault

00:43:50.327 --> 00:43:53.163
on the integrity of
cultural structures,

00:43:53.163 --> 00:43:57.101 align:start position:0%
but there's also a technological
assault on everything.

00:43:57.101 --> 00:44:00.604
And so what do you do in
a situation like that?

00:44:00.604 --> 00:44:04.108 align:start size:97% position:3%
Well, I think my sense is
you revert to the individual.

00:44:05.743 --> 00:44:07.544
You try to make better people.

00:44:07.544 --> 00:44:09.980 align:start position:0%
- How much of this do you
think has to do with the speed?

00:44:09.980 --> 00:44:12.616 align:start size:97% position:3%
Because actually I remember
on sort of the last

00:44:12.616 --> 00:44:14.184
maybe quarter of the tour.

00:44:14.184 --> 00:44:16.387
One of the things that
you talked about a lot

00:44:16.387 --> 00:44:18.355 align:start size:84% position:16%
was how the internet
was changing us.

00:44:18.355 --> 00:44:20.491 align:start size:97% position:3%
How the speed of
information was changing us.

00:44:20.491 --> 00:44:22.926
How you as a random person,

00:44:22.926 --> 00:44:24.928
no matter where you
are in the world.

00:44:24.928 --> 00:44:26.997
You might be able
to send out a tweet

00:44:26.997 --> 00:44:31.902
or create a meme that could
change the world like that.

00:44:31.902 --> 00:44:35.039
So how much do you think the
speed is all part of this.

00:44:35.039 --> 00:44:38.709 align:start position:0%
In ways that were literally
unimaginable three decades ago?

00:44:38.709 --> 00:44:40.911 align:start size:84% position:16%
- I think it's a
tremendous part of it.

00:44:40.911 --> 00:44:42.346 align:start size:81% position:19%
We don't know what
to do with any

00:44:42.346 --> 00:44:44.048
of the new technologies
that we've produced.

00:44:44.048 --> 00:44:46.316 align:start size:97% position:3%
And by the time we adapt to
them, they'll have transformed

00:44:46.316 --> 00:44:48.118 align:start size:84% position:16%
into something
completely different.

00:44:49.553 --> 00:44:54.458
So my kids are in their
late 20s and they're more

00:44:56.994 --> 00:45:00.164
a part of the internet
generation than I am,

00:45:00.164 --> 00:45:04.435 align:start size:97% position:3%
but they're being supplanted
in their knowledge

00:45:04.435 --> 00:45:06.036
by younger people already.

00:45:06.036 --> 00:45:07.871
They can both feel it.

00:45:07.871 --> 00:45:10.040 align:start size:84% position:16%
It's changing
unbelievably quickly.

00:45:11.442 --> 00:45:16.080 align:start size:97% position:3%
So that puts a tremendous
amount of stress on everyone.

00:45:17.848 --> 00:45:21.251 align:start size:94% position:6%
- When you talk
about the creative
achievement part of this,

00:45:21.251 --> 00:45:22.920 align:start size:94% position:6%
one of the things I've
been thinking about lately

00:45:22.920 --> 00:45:25.389 align:start size:84% position:16%
is that I don't
remember the last time

00:45:25.389 --> 00:45:28.225
I heard a new musician,
that I really loved,

00:45:28.225 --> 00:45:32.029
or saw a piece of art that
was new, that I really loved.

00:45:32.029 --> 00:45:34.098
You know, it seems so rare.

00:45:34.098 --> 00:45:36.834 align:start size:94% position:6%
Because of what's happened
with cancel culture,

00:45:36.834 --> 00:45:40.537 align:start size:97% position:3%
that the people that
should be showing us things

00:45:40.537 --> 00:45:41.805
are not showing them.

00:45:43.540 --> 00:45:45.709
What do we do about that?

00:45:45.709 --> 00:45:47.511
How do we make the
artists brave again?

00:45:47.511 --> 00:45:49.646 align:start size:88% position:13%
How do we make the
people who will give us

00:45:49.646 --> 00:45:50.914
the creative achievement?

00:45:50.914 --> 00:45:53.817
How do we make them
see that star again?

00:45:53.817 --> 00:45:57.221
- Well you do whatever
you can, by example.

00:45:57.221 --> 00:46:00.758
That's the best you've got.

00:46:01.925 --> 00:46:04.294
So hopefully you try
to bring integrity

00:46:04.294 --> 00:46:06.897
to your endeavors.

00:46:06.897 --> 00:46:10.667 align:start size:84% position:16%
And hopefully that
has a salutary effect.

00:46:10.667 --> 00:46:12.970 align:start size:88% position:13%
You don't have a better
option than that.

00:46:14.972 --> 00:46:19.576 align:start size:94% position:6%
- So chapter two is,
imagine who you could be,

00:46:19.576 --> 00:46:21.879 align:start size:88% position:13%
and then aim
single-mindedly at that.

00:46:21.879 --> 00:46:24.381 align:start size:88% position:13%
And for me, after
being on tour with you,

00:46:26.183 --> 00:46:27.484
I think that's something

00:46:27.484 --> 00:46:30.220
that got into me
through osmosis.

00:46:30.220 --> 00:46:31.722
That I would be on stage.

00:46:31.722 --> 00:46:34.258 align:start size:97% position:3%
And even though everyone was
there for you, I thought,

00:46:34.258 --> 00:46:36.760
"Hey I'm part of this, somehow.

00:46:36.760 --> 00:46:39.429
This thing, somehow I
became part of this."

00:46:39.429 --> 00:46:43.300
And then once I realized
that when the PA announcer

00:46:43.300 --> 00:46:45.235
said my name, that
those people knew me,

00:46:45.235 --> 00:46:47.004
I thought that I'm me.

00:46:47.004 --> 00:46:48.472 align:start size:81% position:19%
I'm the guy you're
talking about.

00:46:48.472 --> 00:46:49.740
I'm doing it.

00:46:49.740 --> 00:46:53.410
And then just that
it helped my aim.

00:46:53.410 --> 00:46:54.912
It helped my aim.

00:46:54.912 --> 00:46:58.215
And I wonder how many
people just don't...

00:46:58.215 --> 00:46:59.616
They don't know how to aim,

00:46:59.616 --> 00:47:01.652
because they have no
experience like that.

00:47:01.652 --> 00:47:02.920
Something like that.

00:47:02.920 --> 00:47:04.454
- Well that's part
of what tradition

00:47:04.454 --> 00:47:07.758
is supposed to teach you by
presenting you with examples

00:47:07.758 --> 00:47:10.127
of great people of the past.

00:47:10.127 --> 00:47:13.063 align:start size:94% position:6%
The lesson is not supposed
to be exactly bow down

00:47:13.063 --> 00:47:14.364
and worship these people.

00:47:14.364 --> 00:47:18.368
It's be like them, be like them.

00:47:18.368 --> 00:47:19.870
And you could be.

00:47:19.870 --> 00:47:22.072
And I mean, that's really
the goal of the humanities.

00:47:22.072 --> 00:47:23.307
When it's the humanities.

00:47:23.307 --> 00:47:25.609
If it's not, if that's
the goal, then students

00:47:25.609 --> 00:47:27.110
will study the humanities.

00:47:27.110 --> 00:47:29.546 align:start size:84% position:16%
As soon as that
ceases to be the goal,

00:47:29.546 --> 00:47:33.383 align:start size:84% position:16%
then there's nothing
of value there.

00:47:33.383 --> 00:47:35.219 align:start size:88% position:13%
I mean, great
literature tells you...

00:47:35.219 --> 00:47:38.722 align:start size:97% position:3%
It tells you the great story
of good and evil, always.

00:47:38.722 --> 00:47:40.657
It's good and evil
against a background

00:47:40.657 --> 00:47:42.860
of chaos and order, always.

00:47:42.860 --> 00:47:46.830 align:start position:0%
And the evil characters are
there to do to be bad examples.

00:47:46.830 --> 00:47:48.832 line:0%
And the good characters are
there to be good examples.

00:47:48.832 --> 00:47:50.067 line:0%
Or you see the interplay

00:47:50.067 --> 00:47:52.302 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
of those forces within
a single person.

00:47:54.738 --> 00:47:57.107
It's a reminder of
who you could be.

00:47:57.107 --> 00:47:59.509
And you can find out
who you should be.

00:47:59.509 --> 00:48:03.614 align:start size:97% position:3%
And this is something quite
mysterious, I believe.

00:48:03.614 --> 00:48:06.316
- And part of the
proof let's say,

00:48:06.316 --> 00:48:08.685
that we exist in
a world of value.

00:48:08.685 --> 00:48:11.755
Your conscience tells
you who you should be.

00:48:11.755 --> 00:48:14.758 align:start size:84% position:16%
Now that doesn't
mean that necessarily
that it's infallible,

00:48:14.758 --> 00:48:17.728 align:start size:84% position:16%
but people wrestle
with their conscience.

00:48:17.728 --> 00:48:19.363
- You know, there
isn't anyone...

00:48:19.363 --> 00:48:21.265
I've never met anyone who is...

00:48:24.501 --> 00:48:26.870 align:start size:84% position:16%
Narcissists accepted
it, let's say

00:48:28.171 --> 00:48:30.507 align:start size:97% position:3%
people are generally
tormented by their conscience.

00:48:30.507 --> 00:48:35.512
And the reason for that
is that they're not...

00:48:37.414 --> 00:48:41.852
They're deviating from the
path that is their destiny.

00:48:41.852 --> 00:48:44.621
And if you don't think that,
well then what do you think?

00:48:44.621 --> 00:48:46.657
What do you think
that conscience is?

00:48:46.657 --> 00:48:48.625
I mean, I've asked my
classes repeatedly.

00:48:48.625 --> 00:48:50.928
Do you have a little voice
in your head that tells you

00:48:50.928 --> 00:48:53.096
when you've done something
wrong or you're about to,

00:48:53.096 --> 00:48:57.200
or a feeling, and they all
immediately agree with that.

00:48:57.200 --> 00:48:59.069
No one finds that
a foreign concept.

00:48:59.069 --> 00:49:01.838 align:start size:97% position:3%
And so if you don't know
who you are, your conscience

00:49:01.838 --> 00:49:03.807
will remind you when you're...

00:49:03.807 --> 00:49:05.042
Sorry.

00:49:05.042 --> 00:49:06.476
If you don't know
who you could be

00:49:06.476 --> 00:49:07.678 align:start size:97% position:3%
your conscience will remind
you when you deviate.

00:49:07.678 --> 00:49:09.746 align:start size:84% position:16%
And then you can start
to attend to that.

00:49:11.148 --> 00:49:13.750 align:start size:97% position:3%
Like well look, I'm actually
ashamed when I do this,

00:49:15.786 --> 00:49:17.220
I should stop.

00:49:17.220 --> 00:49:19.289 align:start size:84% position:16%
Unless I wanna be
ashamed all the time.

00:49:19.289 --> 00:49:21.091
It looks like I should stop.

00:49:21.091 --> 00:49:23.026
And then maybe you
stop doing that.

00:49:23.026 --> 00:49:25.529
And then your conscience
objects to something else.

00:49:25.529 --> 00:49:27.030 align:start size:84% position:16%
And maybe you
stopped doing that.

00:49:27.030 --> 00:49:29.333
And as that happens you
start to develop a vision

00:49:29.333 --> 00:49:31.034
of who could be.

00:49:31.034 --> 00:49:34.037
And the chapter indicates...

00:49:36.473 --> 00:49:39.242 align:start size:84% position:16%
It looks at symbolic
representations.

00:49:39.242 --> 00:49:42.446 align:start position:0%
It's an examination of a
certain symbolic representation

00:49:42.446 --> 00:49:43.680
of the ideal.

00:49:43.680 --> 00:49:47.617 align:start size:84% position:16%
And so it's my attempt
to assess tradition

00:49:47.617 --> 00:49:50.253 align:start size:94% position:6%
for what it can tell us
about what the ideal human

00:49:50.253 --> 00:49:51.521
being might be like.

00:49:51.521 --> 00:49:54.324
And the ideal human
being is the person

00:49:54.324 --> 00:49:57.594
who forthrightly upholds

00:49:57.594 --> 00:49:59.696
the traditions of the
culture and forges

00:49:59.696 --> 00:50:00.797
a way into the unknown.

00:50:00.797 --> 00:50:02.032
We went through that.

00:50:04.501 --> 00:50:06.636
And pulls new information in

00:50:06.636 --> 00:50:09.673 align:start size:84% position:16%
and rebuilds himself
and the world.

00:50:09.673 --> 00:50:11.408
And that's who you could be.

00:50:11.408 --> 00:50:14.811
And now the difficulty comes in,

00:50:14.811 --> 00:50:17.047
figuring out how to do
that within the confines

00:50:17.047 --> 00:50:18.548
of your own life.

00:50:18.548 --> 00:50:20.550
So in some sense, that's how
to bring the divine to earth.

00:50:20.550 --> 00:50:22.652
There's this divine pattern

00:50:22.652 --> 00:50:25.288
But it's general.

00:50:25.288 --> 00:50:27.891
See this is one of
the mysterious things
about Christianity,

00:50:27.891 --> 00:50:29.292
that's so remarkable about it.

00:50:29.292 --> 00:50:34.297 align:start size:84% position:16%
Is that there's the
Christ that's eternal.

00:50:35.032 --> 00:50:36.533
The word of God say.

00:50:36.533 --> 00:50:38.035
That's a representation

00:50:38.035 --> 00:50:40.570 align:start size:88% position:13%
of something absolutely
transcendent.

00:50:40.570 --> 00:50:43.373
But it's married
to the particulars

00:50:43.373 --> 00:50:45.375
of one particular
time and space..

00:50:45.375 --> 00:50:48.812 align:start size:84% position:16%
And obviously critics
of Christianity

00:50:48.812 --> 00:50:52.049
regard that as one
of its major flaws.

00:50:52.049 --> 00:50:54.918
That there's this idea of God

00:50:54.918 --> 00:50:58.355 align:start size:94% position:6%
who was a carpenter in
some out of the way place,

00:50:58.355 --> 00:51:00.857
in some out of the way time.

00:51:00.857 --> 00:51:05.295
But you're someone in
an out of the way place

00:51:05.295 --> 00:51:07.497
at a particular time and place.

00:51:07.497 --> 00:51:11.468
And for you, what
that means is that,

00:51:11.468 --> 00:51:16.473 align:start size:97% position:3%
for you to make contact
with the highest of values,

00:51:17.641 --> 00:51:20.077
you have to bring that
down to your particulars

00:51:20.077 --> 00:51:21.812
and figure out how you do that.

00:51:21.812 --> 00:51:23.780 align:start size:88% position:13%
It's gonna be a way that
no one else does it.

00:51:23.780 --> 00:51:26.850
Because you're the
only one that's you.

00:51:26.850 --> 00:51:29.553 align:start size:97% position:3%
But you can aim at
something, aim at something.

00:51:29.553 --> 00:51:32.522 align:start size:97% position:3%
And the point of the chapter
is you aim at something

00:51:32.522 --> 00:51:35.358
and that will shape you
as you move towards it.

00:51:35.358 --> 00:51:37.360 align:start size:88% position:13%
And then you're aim will
change, you'll move.

00:51:37.360 --> 00:51:38.862
But that doesn't matter.

00:51:38.862 --> 00:51:40.664
It gets you going.

00:51:40.664 --> 00:51:42.332 align:start size:84% position:16%
And you'll be
molded across time.

00:51:42.332 --> 00:51:45.268
More and more into the
person you could be.

00:51:45.268 --> 00:51:46.269
- Can you talk about that

00:51:46.269 --> 00:51:47.871
just from a personal perspective

00:51:47.871 --> 00:51:49.406
as someone that I've seen do it.

00:51:49.406 --> 00:51:52.209
I mean, that's what I
saw you do every night.

00:51:52.209 --> 00:51:56.947 align:start size:84% position:16%
You would take your
intellectual curiosity

00:51:56.947 --> 00:51:58.315
to the end of where it would go.

00:51:58.315 --> 00:52:00.383
Sometimes you would get
off stage and say to me,

00:52:00.383 --> 00:52:03.253
"Oh you know, I took that
as far as I could tonight."

00:52:03.253 --> 00:52:04.921
And then the next
night you would go

00:52:04.921 --> 00:52:06.957 align:start size:97% position:3%
a little bit further with
it, or a little bit further.

00:52:06.957 --> 00:52:09.493
And I knew there were moments,
because we did so many shows.

00:52:09.493 --> 00:52:11.595 align:start size:81% position:19%
I knew when you
were a little past

00:52:11.595 --> 00:52:12.829
where you would wanna go,

00:52:12.829 --> 00:52:14.431
and then I could
see you come back.

00:52:14.431 --> 00:52:17.134 align:start size:94% position:6%
But can you talk about
what that was like for you

00:52:17.134 --> 00:52:18.201
in terms of your life.

00:52:18.201 --> 00:52:20.137
How you felt, how time felt.

00:52:20.137 --> 00:52:22.539
How the relationship
with the audience felt.

00:52:22.539 --> 00:52:24.274
When you're doing it right.

00:52:24.274 --> 00:52:25.842 align:start size:88% position:13%
Cause I feel like
people don't know that.

00:52:25.842 --> 00:52:28.812 align:start size:97% position:3%
When you're doing it right,
what does it feel like?

00:52:28.812 --> 00:52:31.615 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Well, to begin with,
and this happened

00:52:31.615 --> 00:52:32.749
when I was in graduate school.

00:52:32.749 --> 00:52:35.218
I had a lot of bad habits.

00:52:35.218 --> 00:52:38.388
I smoked like a pack
of cigarettes a day,
and I drank a lot.

00:52:38.388 --> 00:52:40.624
I came from this little
town in Northern Alberta.

00:52:40.624 --> 00:52:43.827 align:start size:97% position:3%
And like many little towns,
especially in Northern Canada.

00:52:45.829 --> 00:52:49.332
Alcohol overuse is [indistinct].

00:52:49.332 --> 00:52:54.337
And so I noticed when
I was in my early 20s,

00:52:55.572 --> 00:52:59.209
that the only time
I really regretted

00:52:59.209 --> 00:53:02.412
what I had done was
when I was drinking.

00:53:04.281 --> 00:53:06.883 align:start size:97% position:3%
Now, it was also
interfering with me writing

00:53:07.984 --> 00:53:09.452 align:start size:88% position:13%
because I couldn't
concentrate well enough

00:53:09.452 --> 00:53:10.387
if I was hung over.

00:53:10.387 --> 00:53:11.655
But I also couldn't really...

00:53:11.655 --> 00:53:15.725
I couldn't tolerate
the emotional strain

00:53:15.725 --> 00:53:18.662 align:start size:97% position:3%
of what I was writing
about when I was hung over.

00:53:18.662 --> 00:53:20.430
I couldn't handle
being on the edge

00:53:21.565 --> 00:53:23.333 align:start size:84% position:16%
because I destabilized
my nervous system.

00:53:23.333 --> 00:53:25.335
In any case, I stopped drinking.

00:53:25.335 --> 00:53:27.604
The reason for that
was, well I decided

00:53:27.604 --> 00:53:30.407
I didn't want to be ashamed
of what I was doing anymore.

00:53:30.407 --> 00:53:33.810
It seemed, I thought, well
maybe I could not do things

00:53:33.810 --> 00:53:36.746
that were shameful, and then
see what my life was like.

00:53:36.746 --> 00:53:40.517
So that was on the negative
end, the constraint end.

00:53:47.624 --> 00:53:49.125
I think people get...

00:53:49.125 --> 00:53:52.195
On the more positive end
people get deeply involved

00:53:52.195 --> 00:53:53.463
in what they're doing,

00:53:53.463 --> 00:53:55.565
if they're in the right
place in the right time.

00:53:55.565 --> 00:53:58.868
So I would say, you can
tell, this is the idea

00:53:58.868 --> 00:54:01.004 align:start size:81% position:19%
of heaven on earth
to some degree.

00:54:01.004 --> 00:54:03.173 align:start size:84% position:16%
When time stops,
when you're not aware

00:54:03.173 --> 00:54:05.075
of the duration of time.

00:54:05.075 --> 00:54:06.943
When you're so engaged
with what you're doing.

00:54:06.943 --> 00:54:09.713 align:start size:88% position:13%
That you're not aware
of the duration of time.

00:54:09.713 --> 00:54:14.017 align:start size:84% position:16%
Then you've got
the forces of chaos

00:54:14.017 --> 00:54:15.819
and order balanced properly.

00:54:16.686 --> 00:54:18.655
You're not stultified and board.

00:54:18.655 --> 00:54:20.624
That's an excessive order.

00:54:20.624 --> 00:54:22.192
Everything's too predictable.

00:54:22.192 --> 00:54:24.227
You're not overwhelmed.

00:54:24.227 --> 00:54:25.996
You're dealing with...

00:54:27.163 --> 00:54:29.266
You're playing tennis at
the peak of your game.

00:54:29.266 --> 00:54:30.800
That's partly what
people experience

00:54:30.800 --> 00:54:32.402 align:start size:94% position:6%
when they're great
athletes, when they play.

00:54:32.402 --> 00:54:33.903
- The zone yeah.

00:54:33.903 --> 00:54:35.905 align:start position:0%
- And they're always stretching
themselves to their limit.

00:54:35.905 --> 00:54:38.308 align:start size:97% position:3%
You can tell that if you
watch a gymnast, for example.

00:54:38.308 --> 00:54:39.809
Who has a brilliant performance.

00:54:39.809 --> 00:54:41.478
They've stretched themselves

00:54:41.478 --> 00:54:45.181
beyond their domain
of competence during
the performance.

00:54:45.181 --> 00:54:47.817 align:start size:97% position:3%
And that's what makes
everybody leap to their feet.

00:54:47.817 --> 00:54:52.289 align:start size:84% position:16%
That's the incarnation
given embodiment.

00:54:52.289 --> 00:54:54.891
Right there in front of
you for some moments.

00:54:54.891 --> 00:54:57.427
And everyone cheers that on.

00:54:57.427 --> 00:54:59.095
- Do you think it's
weird how it becomes

00:54:59.095 --> 00:55:00.730
a fleeting moment in a way?

00:55:00.730 --> 00:55:04.734 align:start size:81% position:19%
I know what you're
saying is true.

00:55:04.734 --> 00:55:07.003
When I'm doing what I'm
supposed to be doing,

00:55:07.003 --> 00:55:10.006 align:start size:81% position:19%
and I'm on my game
and my thoughts

00:55:10.006 --> 00:55:11.341
are right and straight.

00:55:11.341 --> 00:55:13.376
Time just moves.

00:55:13.376 --> 00:55:16.146 align:start size:97% position:3%
And then I go, "Whoa, a
month passed a month passed.

00:55:16.146 --> 00:55:17.647
And I was good that whole time.

00:55:17.647 --> 00:55:19.983 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I did write that whole
time and I was happier.

00:55:19.983 --> 00:55:21.851
And my relationship with David

00:55:21.851 --> 00:55:24.587
or whoever else is is
better in that time."

00:55:24.587 --> 00:55:26.456
But that it becomes fleeting.

00:55:26.456 --> 00:55:28.358 align:start size:94% position:6%
In that suddenly you
could have a great month,

00:55:28.358 --> 00:55:31.995 align:start size:97% position:3%
and then suddenly something
happens, chaos returns.

00:55:31.995 --> 00:55:34.631
It's that we almost
forget that moment.

00:55:34.631 --> 00:55:35.598
You can't hold it.

00:55:37.033 --> 00:55:40.236
- Well, it requires a lot of
it requires even to some degree

00:55:40.236 --> 00:55:41.805
some good fortune to maintain.

00:55:41.805 --> 00:55:44.641
I certainly haven't been able
to do that while I was ill.

00:55:47.544 --> 00:55:50.447 align:start size:88% position:13%
One of the consequences
of my illness,

00:55:50.447 --> 00:55:54.784 align:start size:84% position:16%
whatever it was or
is, was time dilation.

00:55:54.784 --> 00:55:56.953
Days lasted weeks
it seemed like.

00:55:56.953 --> 00:55:58.488
Minutes lasted hours.

00:55:58.488 --> 00:56:02.792 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I mean that literally,
and that was terrible.

00:56:02.792 --> 00:56:06.830 align:start size:97% position:3%
The weight of time, it's the
weight of brut mortality.

00:56:06.830 --> 00:56:09.699
It's the weight of
self-consciousness.

00:56:09.699 --> 00:56:12.869
And you escape that
immersed properly.

00:56:16.005 --> 00:56:18.241
And that second chapter, it's
a pretty practical chapter.

00:56:18.241 --> 00:56:21.144 align:start size:94% position:6%
It's like, well, if you're
not who you wanna be,

00:56:21.144 --> 00:56:24.481 align:start size:84% position:16%
then think about how
you could better.

00:56:24.481 --> 00:56:29.018 align:start position:0%
Take a chance, aim at that,
work at it and see what happens.

00:56:29.018 --> 00:56:30.420
So that's a-

00:56:30.420 --> 00:56:33.690 align:start size:97% position:3%
- And that's a disciplinary
routine I would say.

00:56:33.690 --> 00:56:35.191
It takes you out of
your current order.

00:56:35.191 --> 00:56:37.093
- I think that's also,
it's a perfect segue

00:56:37.093 --> 00:56:38.495
to chapter three.

00:56:38.495 --> 00:56:41.531 align:start size:97% position:3%
Which is do not hide
unwanted things in the fog.

00:56:41.531 --> 00:56:42.899
Which seems to me, we live

00:56:42.899 --> 00:56:44.901
in a constant state
of distraction now.

00:56:44.901 --> 00:56:46.469 align:start size:88% position:13%
You've mentioned Twitter
a couple of times.

00:56:46.469 --> 00:56:49.205 align:start size:97% position:3%
It's like is Twitter
bringing any of us happiness?

00:56:49.205 --> 00:56:52.175
Or is it keeping us all in
a constant state of fog.

00:56:52.175 --> 00:56:55.912 align:start size:97% position:3%
The overload and the endless
obsession with politics.

00:56:55.912 --> 00:56:58.248
That all seems like a fog to me.

00:56:58.248 --> 00:57:00.984 align:start size:84% position:16%
I do my August off
the grid and I do that

00:57:00.984 --> 00:57:03.920
to get out of the fog.

00:57:06.189 --> 00:57:08.792
- Yeah, distraction
is definitely fog.

00:57:09.793 --> 00:57:11.594
And you'll distract yourself.

00:57:11.594 --> 00:57:13.430 align:start size:84% position:16%
I think you distract
yourself mostly

00:57:13.430 --> 00:57:15.865 align:start size:84% position:16%
when your conscience
is bothering you.

00:57:15.865 --> 00:57:19.669 align:start size:97% position:3%
Because you don't want to
face what it is in your life

00:57:19.669 --> 00:57:21.771
that it is uncomfortable.

00:57:21.771 --> 00:57:24.174 align:start size:84% position:16%
That chapter is again
quite practical.

00:57:24.174 --> 00:57:29.179 align:start position:0%
It's a reminder to pay attention
often to negative emotion.

00:57:30.313 --> 00:57:31.247 align:start size:84% position:16%
Resentment, and
that sort of thing.

00:57:31.247 --> 00:57:32.115
Because it can tell you...

00:57:33.183 --> 00:57:35.652
Well, resentment is very useful.

00:57:35.652 --> 00:57:38.788 align:start size:88% position:13%
Maybe maybe your partner
is talking to someone

00:57:38.788 --> 00:57:41.257 align:start size:97% position:3%
and they're a little bit more
animated than you'd like,

00:57:41.257 --> 00:57:42.592
and you get jealous.

00:57:42.592 --> 00:57:44.727
And that jealousy is associated

00:57:44.727 --> 00:57:46.863
with a whole set
of insecurities.

00:57:48.798 --> 00:57:52.068
Or maybe they're flirting
and they shouldn't be.

00:57:52.068 --> 00:57:53.670
It's not that easy to determine

00:57:53.670 --> 00:57:55.305
and maybe you'll have
a big fight about that.

00:57:55.305 --> 00:57:59.609
But you could just as well
pretend that didn't happen.

00:57:59.609 --> 00:58:02.412
The emotion comes up, I'm
jealous, I'm resentful.

00:58:02.412 --> 00:58:05.081 align:start position:0%
It's associated with experiences
like that in the past.

00:58:05.081 --> 00:58:07.650
The psychoanalysts would
have called that a complex.

00:58:07.650 --> 00:58:09.118
You could notice that.

00:58:09.118 --> 00:58:11.254 align:start size:94% position:6%
You could think of, "Well
should I be jealous?"

00:58:11.254 --> 00:58:12.489 align:start size:81% position:19%
Is there something
wrong with me?

00:58:12.489 --> 00:58:14.357
Or there's something
wrong with my partner.

00:58:14.357 --> 00:58:16.526
There's something wrong
with the relationship.

00:58:16.526 --> 00:58:19.696
And you have to untangle that.

00:58:19.696 --> 00:58:21.664 align:start size:88% position:13%
And who knows what
you'll have to untangle

00:58:21.664 --> 00:58:24.400
to get that straight, or
you can bear the jealousy

00:58:24.400 --> 00:58:26.069 align:start size:88% position:13%
and see what will happen
in the relationship.

00:58:26.069 --> 00:58:27.203
Or maybe it'll disintegrate

00:58:27.203 --> 00:58:28.838 align:start size:84% position:16%
because your
partner is flirting.

00:58:28.838 --> 00:58:30.073
You ignore that.

00:58:30.073 --> 00:58:32.075
It's not like you
repress it exactly.

00:58:32.075 --> 00:58:34.711 align:start size:97% position:3%
And this chapter is an attempt
to distinguish repression

00:58:34.711 --> 00:58:36.579
from this hiding in the fog.

00:58:36.579 --> 00:58:39.482
It's that you get a hint
that something's wrong.

00:58:40.650 --> 00:58:43.620
And then you have
to unpack that hint

00:58:43.620 --> 00:58:45.255
to pull the information out.

00:58:46.489 --> 00:58:48.791 align:start size:84% position:16%
So maybe your
partner is flirting.

00:58:48.791 --> 00:58:50.260
And they shouldn't be.

00:58:50.260 --> 00:58:52.161
And so then you have to find out

00:58:52.161 --> 00:58:54.197 align:start size:88% position:13%
why they're dissatisfied
with the relationship

00:58:54.197 --> 00:58:56.032
or what's tempting them.

00:58:56.032 --> 00:58:59.269 align:start size:94% position:6%
Or what is crooked in
their soul at the moment.

00:58:59.269 --> 00:59:02.305 align:start size:84% position:16%
or what they're
dissatisfied about.

00:59:02.305 --> 00:59:06.476 align:start size:94% position:6%
Terrible journey of
exploration and discovery.

00:59:06.476 --> 00:59:08.511
That's always presented as
something that's positive.

00:59:08.511 --> 00:59:11.214
It's often not at all.

00:59:11.214 --> 00:59:12.448
It's so hard.

00:59:12.448 --> 00:59:15.552
It's like doing surgery
on a separating wound.

00:59:15.552 --> 00:59:17.687
It's no wonder people avoid it.

00:59:17.687 --> 00:59:19.222
But it's not helpful.

00:59:20.356 --> 00:59:22.559
Because all it does
is leave that...

00:59:22.559 --> 00:59:25.228
Those things grow and
multiply in the dark.

00:59:25.228 --> 00:59:27.297 align:start size:88% position:13%
And if you ignore them,
they just cascade.

00:59:27.297 --> 00:59:30.366 align:start position:0%
- Do you think that's just self
protection for most people?

00:59:30.366 --> 00:59:32.168
That most people, they see it,

00:59:32.168 --> 00:59:35.038 align:start size:81% position:19%
they know that
truth behind them.

00:59:35.038 --> 00:59:37.373 align:start position:0%
Whether it's about their
partner or whatever it might be.

00:59:37.373 --> 00:59:38.308
But they just...

00:59:38.308 --> 00:59:39.542
It's just self protection.

00:59:39.542 --> 00:59:40.910
Like, oh, I just got
to keep moving on,

00:59:40.910 --> 00:59:42.412
as things are.

00:59:42.412 --> 00:59:44.447
That we're just creatures of
habit or something like that.

00:59:44.447 --> 00:59:46.282
- Sometimes it's that.

00:59:46.282 --> 00:59:48.184
It especially gets to be that.

00:59:48.184 --> 00:59:51.220
If it's accumulated
for a long time.

00:59:51.220 --> 00:59:52.722
Because if you wouldn't have...

00:59:52.722 --> 00:59:54.524
If you wouldn't deal with
it when it was a kitten,

00:59:54.524 --> 00:59:57.427
you're not gonna deal with it
when it's a full grown lion.

00:59:59.662 --> 01:00:01.397
But I think mostly it's...

01:00:02.599 --> 01:00:03.967
It's something else I
return to in the book

01:00:03.967 --> 01:00:06.803 align:start size:94% position:6%
is it's deceit,
resentment, and arrogance.

01:00:06.803 --> 01:00:10.039 align:start size:84% position:16%
I already know what
I need to know.

01:00:10.039 --> 01:00:11.507
That's arrogance.

01:00:11.507 --> 01:00:14.611
Deceit is I don't have
to pay attention to that.

01:00:14.611 --> 01:00:17.313
And resentment is, well,
things can go to hell.

01:00:17.313 --> 01:00:18.915
And so can he or she.

01:00:19.849 --> 01:00:22.151
And that's a pretty dark triad.

01:00:23.553 --> 01:00:26.889
And you don't want the
spirit that embodies that

01:00:26.889 --> 01:00:28.491
to take over your life.

01:00:28.491 --> 01:00:29.759
That's for sure.

01:00:31.628 --> 01:00:33.930
- Well that gets
us to four then.

01:00:33.930 --> 01:00:36.366
I think you got the
order right on these.

01:00:36.366 --> 01:00:40.203 align:start size:97% position:3%
Because chapter four is
noticed that opportunity lurks

01:00:40.203 --> 01:00:42.505
where responsibility
has been abdicated.

01:00:42.505 --> 01:00:45.975
Now I think we're seeing an
awful lot of that these days.

01:00:45.975 --> 01:00:47.777
- Well, that's a nice inversion.

01:00:47.777 --> 01:00:49.545
Because people are
often resentful.

01:00:49.545 --> 01:00:52.482 align:start position:0%
If they see that responsibility
has been abdicated.

01:00:52.482 --> 01:00:55.151 align:start size:84% position:16%
Why isn't that person
doing their job?

01:00:55.151 --> 01:00:57.320
It's like, well,
hey man step in.

01:00:58.521 --> 01:01:00.990
At your workplace
in your family.

01:01:00.990 --> 01:01:02.992
When you think, well,
I shouldn't have to do

01:01:02.992 --> 01:01:03.926
that extra work.

01:01:03.926 --> 01:01:05.695
Well, you shouldn't be a slave.

01:01:05.695 --> 01:01:07.897 align:start size:94% position:6%
You shouldn't allow
yourself to be tyrannized.

01:01:07.897 --> 01:01:10.400
But if something bugs you

01:01:10.400 --> 01:01:13.302 align:start size:94% position:6%
because the responsibility
is going unfulfilled,

01:01:13.302 --> 01:01:15.271 align:start size:84% position:16%
there's a great
opportunity for you.

01:01:15.271 --> 01:01:17.407 align:start size:84% position:16%
And this is something
I don't think

01:01:17.407 --> 01:01:19.108
we teach young people well.

01:01:19.108 --> 01:01:20.977
And one of the things
you may remember this

01:01:20.977 --> 01:01:23.146 align:start size:84% position:16%
that was so striking
about the tour

01:01:23.146 --> 01:01:26.683
was that I made a case
fairly consistently,

01:01:26.683 --> 01:01:30.319
that most people find the
meaning that sustains them

01:01:30.319 --> 01:01:32.488 align:start size:84% position:16%
through the
vicissitudes of life,

01:01:32.488 --> 01:01:35.558
not in happiness, but
in responsibility.

01:01:35.558 --> 01:01:37.460
And that would bring
everyone to a halt.

01:01:37.460 --> 01:01:39.962 align:start size:88% position:13%
It would always make the
whole theater silent.

01:01:39.962 --> 01:01:42.832 align:start size:97% position:3%
It's like, oh, I never
thought of that connection.

01:01:42.832 --> 01:01:45.168
Because maybe you wanna
avoid responsibility.

01:01:45.168 --> 01:01:47.170
And you can understand why.

01:01:47.170 --> 01:01:48.638
You can understand why.

01:01:48.638 --> 01:01:49.906
You hide from it.

01:01:49.906 --> 01:01:53.976
Like Abraham, one of
the biblical stories.

01:01:53.976 --> 01:01:57.113 align:start position:0%
Abraham, he stays in his
father's tent till he's like 80.

01:01:57.113 --> 01:02:00.950
And then God gets fed up and
tells him to get the hell out

01:02:00.950 --> 01:02:02.385
and grow up.

01:02:02.385 --> 01:02:04.787 align:start size:97% position:3%
And everything that he
encounters is catastrophic.

01:02:04.787 --> 01:02:06.756
He encounters tyranny.

01:02:06.756 --> 01:02:09.025 align:start size:88% position:13%
The Egyptians conspired
to steal his wife.

01:02:09.025 --> 01:02:12.061 align:start size:84% position:16%
He encounters
starvation and war.

01:02:12.061 --> 01:02:13.730
That is what you encounter
if you go out in the world.

01:02:13.730 --> 01:02:15.431 align:start size:84% position:16%
And you think who
the hell wants that?

01:02:15.431 --> 01:02:16.933
I don't want the responsibility.

01:02:16.933 --> 01:02:20.369 align:start size:88% position:13%
It's like, well, yes
you do, as it turns out.

01:02:20.369 --> 01:02:23.139
There's nothing better
than responsibility.

01:02:23.139 --> 01:02:24.874 align:start size:81% position:19%
Now I say that
with some caution,

01:02:24.874 --> 01:02:28.978
I think that it's hard to say.

01:02:28.978 --> 01:02:33.082
The responsibility
I've been overwhelmed

01:02:33.082 --> 01:02:35.318
by my apparent responsibility.

01:02:36.452 --> 01:02:38.588
But I think it's
also kept me alive.

01:02:38.588 --> 01:02:39.989
And I mean that literally.

01:02:42.825 --> 01:02:44.627 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Wait, can you go a
little deeper with that?

01:02:44.627 --> 01:02:46.295
I mean, when you were
at your darkest moments

01:02:46.295 --> 01:02:47.730
in the last two years,

01:02:47.730 --> 01:02:49.532
whatever that might be.

01:02:50.633 --> 01:02:52.268
Was it the feeling of-

01:02:52.268 --> 01:02:53.970 align:start size:84% position:16%
- There's been many
times in the last year

01:02:53.970 --> 01:02:55.505 align:start size:88% position:13%
where it would have
been much easier for me

01:02:55.505 --> 01:02:56.639
just to be dead.

01:02:58.841 --> 01:03:00.076
That's constant.

01:03:04.280 --> 01:03:06.282
But no.

01:03:08.017 --> 01:03:10.119 align:start size:97% position:3%
- But is it but no,
because of something in you

01:03:10.119 --> 01:03:13.623 align:start size:84% position:16%
or is it but no,
because of the people.

01:03:13.623 --> 01:03:16.926 align:start size:97% position:3%
That you would have abdicated
that responsibility.

01:03:16.926 --> 01:03:18.995 align:start size:84% position:16%
Or is that ultimately
the same thing?

01:03:18.995 --> 01:03:20.530
- Yeah.

01:03:20.530 --> 01:03:23.266
Well, I do think that the two
aren't easily distinguishable

01:03:23.266 --> 01:03:25.468
but it's definitely that.

01:03:25.468 --> 01:03:26.903
It's definitely that.

01:03:34.877 --> 01:03:36.345
One of the chapters,

01:03:36.345 --> 01:03:38.748 align:start position:0%
the last chapter is Be Grateful
in Spite of Your Suffering.

01:03:41.818 --> 01:03:44.654 align:start size:97% position:3%
You need a meaning to sustain
you through suffering.

01:03:44.654 --> 01:03:47.757
And it is the case that you
find that in responsibility.

01:03:47.757 --> 01:03:52.261 align:start size:97% position:3%
Well, and so it's good advice
for anyone who's at work.

01:03:52.261 --> 01:03:54.530
You're resentful
about this and that

01:03:54.530 --> 01:03:56.199
because people aren't
pulling their weight.

01:03:56.199 --> 01:03:59.268
Pull it, see what happens.

01:03:59.268 --> 01:04:02.371 align:start size:94% position:6%
You become
indispensable, instantly.

01:04:02.371 --> 01:04:03.773
You'll know everything.

01:04:05.942 --> 01:04:07.543
And maybe that job
won't be for you.

01:04:07.543 --> 01:04:08.978 align:start size:94% position:6%
Or maybe that relationship
won't be for you.

01:04:08.978 --> 01:04:11.280
But you'll take your
hard acquired wisdom,

01:04:11.280 --> 01:04:13.115
go elsewhere and flourish there.

01:04:14.383 --> 01:04:16.919
So, this kid stopped me,

01:04:16.919 --> 01:04:18.821
I think I wrote about
this in this book.

01:04:18.821 --> 01:04:21.257
Kid stopped me in a restaurant
one day when I was walking in

01:04:21.257 --> 01:04:22.425
and he said...

01:04:22.425 --> 01:04:24.760 align:start size:84% position:16%
And he had an
undergraduate degree

01:04:24.760 --> 01:04:28.464 align:start size:97% position:3%
and he was working as a
waiter in a chain steak place.

01:04:29.999 --> 01:04:32.435
And he stopped me and
he said, "You know

01:04:32.435 --> 01:04:34.670 align:start size:97% position:3%
about six months ago, I was
watching one of your lectures.

01:04:34.670 --> 01:04:37.607
And I decided to stop being
resentful about my job."

01:04:37.607 --> 01:04:39.175
And he was resentful because
he had a university degree

01:04:39.175 --> 01:04:41.811
and he's working as a waiter.

01:04:41.811 --> 01:04:43.679 align:start size:94% position:6%
Look, I don't have
anything against waiters.

01:04:43.679 --> 01:04:47.617
It's like a waiter can
make your evening hooray.

01:04:49.452 --> 01:04:52.521
He said, "Well, I decided
I'd start trying at my job.

01:04:52.521 --> 01:04:55.658
Like really trying as
if it was worthwhile."

01:04:55.658 --> 01:04:57.693 align:start size:94% position:6%
He said he got three
promotions in six months.

01:04:57.693 --> 01:05:00.763
Like he's just rocketing
up the power hierarchy.

01:05:00.763 --> 01:05:02.865 align:start size:84% position:16%
Well it's not power
it's competence

01:05:02.865 --> 01:05:04.033
if it's well run.

01:05:05.201 --> 01:05:08.371
And and places are
full of opportunity.

01:05:08.371 --> 01:05:13.376 align:start size:84% position:16%
Now that doesn't mean
social structures

01:05:14.243 --> 01:05:15.378
aren't sometimes corrupt.

01:05:15.378 --> 01:05:17.079
But you can discover that too.

01:05:17.079 --> 01:05:21.784
If you start to take
responsibility and
that goes sideways,

01:05:21.784 --> 01:05:23.786
you're not being
credited with that.

01:05:23.786 --> 01:05:27.757
Well, then that's an indication
that you need to restructure

01:05:27.757 --> 01:05:30.259 align:start size:94% position:6%
the situation something's
corrupt about it.

01:05:30.259 --> 01:05:32.828 align:start size:97% position:3%
Or if you can't do that, you
should go somewhere else.

01:05:32.828 --> 01:05:34.697
Where that is valued.

01:05:34.697 --> 01:05:36.365 align:start size:84% position:16%
It's a sign that
things aren't right.

01:05:36.365 --> 01:05:39.635
If you bring your
best to the table

01:05:39.635 --> 01:05:41.237
and that isn't appreciated,

01:05:41.237 --> 01:05:43.706 align:start size:84% position:16%
there's something
wrong with the table.

01:05:45.141 --> 01:05:46.909 align:start size:94% position:6%
Now that doesn't mean that
there's something wrong

01:05:46.909 --> 01:05:51.447 align:start position:0%
with tables, always and
everywhere, but it is the case.

01:05:51.447 --> 01:05:54.050
And this is something we
should all get straight.

01:05:54.050 --> 01:05:55.685
When we think about hierarchies,

01:05:55.685 --> 01:05:57.520
it's an axiom of the left.

01:05:57.520 --> 01:06:01.424
That hierarchies are
predicated on power.

01:06:01.424 --> 01:06:03.492
It's like, no.

01:06:03.492 --> 01:06:05.361
Only when they're corrupt.

01:06:05.361 --> 01:06:06.595
Well they're always corrupt.

01:06:06.595 --> 01:06:10.166 align:start size:88% position:13%
Well, yes, that means
they're all hierarchies,

01:06:10.166 --> 01:06:12.601
are contaminated with power.

01:06:12.601 --> 01:06:15.404 align:start size:94% position:6%
Well, that isn't the
essence of the hierarchy.

01:06:15.404 --> 01:06:18.507 align:start size:97% position:3%
The essence of the hierarchy
is distinction of value.

01:06:20.109 --> 01:06:21.777 align:start size:84% position:16%
And you need the
distinction of value

01:06:21.777 --> 01:06:23.779
because you can't
tell what to do.

01:06:23.779 --> 01:06:26.382
Otherwise you have to do
the first things first.

01:06:26.382 --> 01:06:27.883
That's a hierarchy of value.

01:06:27.883 --> 01:06:31.320
That's not power unless
you're being compelled.

01:06:31.320 --> 01:06:33.856 align:start size:94% position:6%
And then it's corrupt
and you should resist it.

01:06:35.658 --> 01:06:37.226
- I'm reminded of...

01:06:37.226 --> 01:06:39.095
I don't think I've thought of
this probably in two years.

01:06:39.095 --> 01:06:40.363
But do you remember, we were

01:06:40.363 --> 01:06:42.131 align:start size:88% position:13%
in one of the
Scandinavian countries.

01:06:42.131 --> 01:06:44.467 align:start size:84% position:16%
I think maybe we
were leaving Sweden.

01:06:44.467 --> 01:06:46.802 align:start size:94% position:6%
And we were sitting in the
first row of the plane.

01:06:46.802 --> 01:06:48.304
And do you remember the guy.

01:06:48.304 --> 01:06:51.540
It was a young black man,
sort of ran onto the plane.

01:06:51.540 --> 01:06:52.908
He was wearing a yellow vest.

01:06:52.908 --> 01:06:55.678
He was a worker working
the bridge or something.

01:06:55.678 --> 01:06:57.346
And he came up to
you and he said,

01:06:57.346 --> 01:06:59.148
"I'm not supposed to be
on the plane right now.

01:06:59.148 --> 01:07:00.383
I'm not supposed
to say anything.

01:07:00.383 --> 01:07:01.517 align:start size:84% position:16%
But you helped me
turn my life around.

01:07:01.517 --> 01:07:03.119
I got this job because of you."

01:07:03.119 --> 01:07:04.754 align:start size:88% position:13%
And what I remember more
than anything else is

01:07:04.754 --> 01:07:07.356
he had this huge
smile on his face.

01:07:07.356 --> 01:07:09.392
But it wasn't because you
told him to go out there

01:07:09.392 --> 01:07:10.593
and be happy.

01:07:10.593 --> 01:07:12.395 align:start size:88% position:13%
It was because you told
him to go out there

01:07:12.395 --> 01:07:14.030
and fix some stuff.

01:07:14.030 --> 01:07:15.264
And I think that's exactly

01:07:15.264 --> 01:07:17.066
what you're talking
about right now.

01:07:17.066 --> 01:07:22.071 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Those experiences
are so life sustaining.

01:07:23.606 --> 01:07:25.708
It's so remarkable
to have that happen.

01:07:25.708 --> 01:07:27.410
And that's something I've had...

01:07:27.410 --> 01:07:30.579 align:start size:97% position:3%
I've been immensely privileged
to constantly experience.

01:07:30.579 --> 01:07:32.181
Although there's also
something about it.

01:07:32.181 --> 01:07:34.984
That's extremely painful.

01:07:34.984 --> 01:07:39.989 align:start position:0%
You know, the magnitude of that
joy is sort of proportional

01:07:41.157 --> 01:07:43.893
to the obstacles
it had to overcome

01:07:43.893 --> 01:07:47.063 align:start size:88% position:13%
while it was attempting
to manifest itself.

01:07:47.063 --> 01:07:50.332
You know, and one of the things
I'm sure you saw on the tour

01:07:50.332 --> 01:07:51.801
and I don't know how
this affected you.

01:07:51.801 --> 01:07:56.172 align:start size:88% position:13%
Was the depth of hunger
for encouragement

01:07:56.172 --> 01:07:59.241
that exists everywhere
as far as I can tell.

01:07:59.241 --> 01:08:03.012
And everyone wants
to be appreciated.

01:08:03.012 --> 01:08:05.047
When they bring their
best to the table.

01:08:05.047 --> 01:08:07.550
That's why the assault, the
current assault on merit

01:08:07.550 --> 01:08:10.653
is so unbelievably brutal.

01:08:10.653 --> 01:08:14.423
Because it will deny
everyone the right

01:08:14.423 --> 01:08:18.094 align:start size:84% position:16%
to be appreciated
for being their best.

01:08:18.094 --> 01:08:21.530
And well, you might ask,

01:08:21.530 --> 01:08:23.299 align:start size:88% position:13%
well when does the
best become your enemy?

01:08:23.299 --> 01:08:24.366
And that's easy.

01:08:24.366 --> 01:08:25.935
It's when you flee from it.

01:08:27.303 --> 01:08:29.672 align:start size:97% position:3%
Because the best then
tortures and tremendous you.

01:08:29.672 --> 01:08:32.875 align:start size:84% position:16%
It's your conscience
or the judge.

01:08:32.875 --> 01:08:35.077 align:start size:84% position:16%
And it's easy to see
that as a tyrant.

01:08:36.479 --> 01:08:39.782
- I know that this one
was definitely in Sweden.

01:08:39.782 --> 01:08:41.283
For our first show in Sweden.

01:08:41.283 --> 01:08:44.720
I went to H&amp;M to get a hat,
cause it was a windy day.

01:08:44.720 --> 01:08:45.988
And I'm standing in line

01:08:45.988 --> 01:08:47.556
and there's a young guy
standing in front of me.

01:08:47.556 --> 01:08:48.791
And he tell...

01:08:48.791 --> 01:08:50.259
I know I told you
this way back when,

01:08:50.259 --> 01:08:53.496 align:start position:0%
but he told the cashier, he
said, "I'm buying my first suit.

01:08:53.496 --> 01:08:54.663
This is my first suit.

01:08:54.663 --> 01:08:57.333 align:start size:88% position:13%
I'm going to see Jordan
Peterson tonight."

01:08:57.333 --> 01:08:58.534
And then the cashier said,

01:08:58.534 --> 01:09:00.336 align:start size:88% position:13%
"I'm going to see Jordan
Peterson tonight."

01:09:00.336 --> 01:09:03.072
And then I tapped the kid on
the shoulder that he saw me,

01:09:03.072 --> 01:09:04.073
Oh my God, it's you.

01:09:04.073 --> 01:09:05.841
And then that night at the show

01:09:05.841 --> 01:09:08.110
I made a point of
calling him out.

01:09:08.110 --> 01:09:09.578
I can't remember his name,
but he told me his name.

01:09:09.578 --> 01:09:11.647 align:start size:84% position:16%
And I said in
somewhere here is Ben.

01:09:11.647 --> 01:09:14.450
And he was at H&amp;M and he's
in his first suit tonight.

01:09:14.450 --> 01:09:16.819
And the entire audience
stood up and applauded.

01:09:16.819 --> 01:09:17.953
And he was just a kid.

01:09:17.953 --> 01:09:18.888
He was a kid.

01:09:18.888 --> 01:09:21.757
And I thought, this is magical.

01:09:21.757 --> 01:09:23.759
This is truly magical.

01:09:23.759 --> 01:09:25.494
- Yes, well, it was such fun

01:09:25.494 --> 01:09:27.429 align:start size:84% position:16%
to see people
dressing up for that.

01:09:27.429 --> 01:09:30.499 align:start size:84% position:16%
People don't dress up
for anything anymore
and it's too bad.

01:09:30.499 --> 01:09:32.735 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Well, now nobody
dresses up for anything.

01:09:32.735 --> 01:09:33.969
No one goes anywhere.

01:09:33.969 --> 01:09:35.171 align:start size:81% position:19%
But we actually
let's just talk...

01:09:35.171 --> 01:09:36.605
Let's just talk about
that for a moment.

01:09:36.605 --> 01:09:37.806
Just that.

01:09:37.806 --> 01:09:40.309
I mean, beyond all
of the misinformation

01:09:40.309 --> 01:09:42.978
about what they would
say about the audience.

01:09:42.978 --> 01:09:45.681
It's 90% straight white men.

01:09:45.681 --> 01:09:49.618
And it was usually 60,
40 maybe male to female,

01:09:49.618 --> 01:09:51.687 align:start size:84% position:16%
but there were
trans people there,

01:09:51.687 --> 01:09:53.189 align:start size:84% position:16%
there were black
people and gay people,

01:09:53.189 --> 01:09:56.058
and all of that stuff, it's
not even worth going into.

01:09:56.058 --> 01:09:58.928
But you always, I thought
were sort of fascinated

01:09:58.928 --> 01:10:02.398
by that concept, that people
were dressing up to go there.

01:10:02.398 --> 01:10:05.034
That that adds some extra
meaning to you I think

01:10:05.034 --> 01:10:06.802
- Well it was just
fun to see that.

01:10:06.802 --> 01:10:08.537
I had bought these suits.

01:10:08.537 --> 01:10:10.406 align:start size:84% position:16%
I spent an inordinate
amount of money

01:10:10.406 --> 01:10:12.808
on the suits that I
wore for that tour.

01:10:12.808 --> 01:10:14.843
But I thought I was
gonna bring my A game

01:10:14.843 --> 01:10:16.512
to the degree that
that was possible.

01:10:16.512 --> 01:10:18.647 align:start size:94% position:6%
And that meant to pay
attention to every detail

01:10:18.647 --> 01:10:20.216
that I could pay attention to.

01:10:20.216 --> 01:10:22.418
And it helped.

01:10:22.418 --> 01:10:23.786
I liked the suits.

01:10:23.786 --> 01:10:26.288
I'm happy about the
fact that I did that.

01:10:26.288 --> 01:10:29.358
Made that sacrifice
or took that dare.

01:10:29.358 --> 01:10:34.363 align:start size:97% position:3%
And it was great to see
people put on their best self.

01:10:35.097 --> 01:10:36.432
Even just to try it out.

01:10:37.333 --> 01:10:38.901
- And they did it.

01:10:38.901 --> 01:10:41.237
- There was a little game
that everyone was playing.

01:10:41.237 --> 01:10:43.239
And it was lovely.

01:10:43.239 --> 01:10:44.873
It was so lovely to see that.

01:10:45.874 --> 01:10:46.709
And they looked good.

01:10:46.709 --> 01:10:48.177
Man, they look good.

01:10:48.177 --> 01:10:49.778
- They did, they look damn good.

01:10:49.778 --> 01:10:50.579
You looked great.

01:10:51.780 --> 01:10:55.484 align:start size:84% position:16%
Well, number five then
this seems obvious.

01:10:55.484 --> 01:10:59.355
But I think a lot of people
don't don't fully get it.

01:10:59.355 --> 01:11:02.258
Do not do what you hate.

01:11:03.492 --> 01:11:04.660
- Well, we've talked about that

01:11:04.660 --> 01:11:05.894
a little bit already.

01:11:05.894 --> 01:11:08.030
That's a process of
self-realization.

01:11:08.030 --> 01:11:11.333
It's like well maybe...

01:11:11.333 --> 01:11:14.236 align:start size:94% position:6%
I think it's quite
common if are promiscuous

01:11:14.236 --> 01:11:17.339 align:start size:84% position:16%
that they have
post-coital regret.

01:11:17.339 --> 01:11:19.742 align:start size:88% position:13%
Now, I don't imagine
that everyone feels that

01:11:19.742 --> 01:11:21.277
to the same degree.

01:11:21.277 --> 01:11:23.078
And maybe there are people
who don't feel it at all.

01:11:24.580 --> 01:11:27.316
You should probably watch out
for those people, I would say.

01:11:27.316 --> 01:11:28.684
But perhaps not.

01:11:31.453 --> 01:11:35.424
If that regret stays with you,

01:11:35.424 --> 01:11:38.360 align:start size:94% position:6%
perhaps you should
examine what you're doing.

01:11:38.360 --> 01:11:40.129 align:start size:84% position:16%
And I use that
example specifically,

01:11:40.129 --> 01:11:44.433
because obviously sex
is immensely engaging.

01:11:45.501 --> 01:11:48.370 align:start size:84% position:16%
And so it's
tremendously tempting.

01:11:49.271 --> 01:11:51.006
But if the consequence of that

01:11:51.006 --> 01:11:55.978
is the emergence of a state
of existence that you hate.

01:11:57.880 --> 01:11:59.948
Well, maybe you shouldn't do it.

01:11:59.948 --> 01:12:01.884
And it doesn't mean,
well, if you're...

01:12:01.884 --> 01:12:05.754
It doesn't mean quit your
job because it's hard.

01:12:07.356 --> 01:12:09.191
It means watch and see.

01:12:09.191 --> 01:12:12.194 align:start size:84% position:16%
And if you hate
something that you do,

01:12:12.194 --> 01:12:15.064 align:start size:94% position:6%
if it makes you feel small
and weak and sickened

01:12:16.031 --> 01:12:19.335
and contemptible stop doing it.

01:12:20.469 --> 01:12:23.405 align:start size:84% position:16%
That's your morality
speaking to you.

01:12:24.540 --> 01:12:29.211 align:start size:88% position:13%
And this is why, again,
why it seems to me

01:12:29.211 --> 01:12:32.014 align:start size:84% position:16%
there's many reasons
that morality,

01:12:32.014 --> 01:12:34.917 align:start size:94% position:6%
it isn't merely
something that we create.

01:12:34.917 --> 01:12:36.418
It has its existence.

01:12:36.418 --> 01:12:40.189
Because when we violate our
own moral presuppositions,

01:12:40.189 --> 01:12:42.925 align:start size:84% position:16%
we pay, we make
ourselves pay for it.

01:12:42.925 --> 01:12:43.726
Why?

01:12:44.893 --> 01:12:46.862
Why don't we just let
ourselves off the hook?

01:12:48.297 --> 01:12:52.868
If there's nothing real there,
why bother with all of that?

01:12:52.868 --> 01:12:54.636 align:start size:97% position:3%
- You know, one of the things
that I think about a lot

01:12:54.636 --> 01:12:56.972
and I was going through
this during the tour.

01:12:56.972 --> 01:12:59.208 align:start size:84% position:16%
But I was dealing
with alopecia areata

01:12:59.208 --> 01:13:01.810
and I had lost huge
chunks of my hair.

01:13:01.810 --> 01:13:03.278 align:start size:84% position:16%
And there was a night,
we were in Denmark

01:13:03.278 --> 01:13:05.280 align:start size:88% position:13%
where I had this powder
that I would put on

01:13:05.280 --> 01:13:06.515
to cover the spots.

01:13:06.515 --> 01:13:09.518 align:start size:84% position:16%
I left my toiletry bag
in Sweden, I think.

01:13:09.518 --> 01:13:12.020
And I had to run to the
place, and I had to...

01:13:12.020 --> 01:13:13.522 align:start size:84% position:16%
Basically, they
didn't have powder.

01:13:13.522 --> 01:13:15.791
I had to like, literally put
marker on my head to hide it.

01:13:15.791 --> 01:13:16.892
I don't even know
that I had told you

01:13:16.892 --> 01:13:18.227
about it at that point.

01:13:18.227 --> 01:13:19.628
- No, not at that point.

01:13:19.628 --> 01:13:22.164 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Yeah, I don't
think I had told you.

01:13:22.164 --> 01:13:25.167
- It would have been
off the tour with you.

01:13:25.167 --> 01:13:26.301
- Yeah, exactly.

01:13:26.301 --> 01:13:27.569
Well actually you told...

01:13:27.569 --> 01:13:29.037
When I finally did
tell you about it,

01:13:29.037 --> 01:13:30.506 align:start size:84% position:16%
and that's also one
of those weird things.

01:13:30.506 --> 01:13:32.141
Why would I have not
told you about it?

01:13:32.141 --> 01:13:35.110
We all hold these weird things.

01:13:35.110 --> 01:13:38.313
- You were embarrassed and you
know, that's not surprising.

01:13:39.782 --> 01:13:43.919
We're very sensitive when
things mar our appearance.

01:13:43.919 --> 01:13:45.654 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Well, then I finally
told you about it.

01:13:45.654 --> 01:13:48.390 align:start size:97% position:3%
And you told me that you
actually had struggled with it

01:13:48.390 --> 01:13:50.959
when you were younger as well.

01:13:50.959 --> 01:13:52.361
But the reason I mentioned that

01:13:52.361 --> 01:13:56.231 align:start size:94% position:6%
is now that I've conquered
it's all mine now.

01:13:57.166 --> 01:13:58.066
Which is great.

01:13:59.001 --> 01:14:01.069
I do think that at some level,

01:14:01.069 --> 01:14:03.038
I thought it had only to
do with the amount of hate

01:14:03.038 --> 01:14:04.139
that I was getting online.

01:14:04.139 --> 01:14:05.774
And that caused a lot of stress.

01:14:05.774 --> 01:14:10.546
But I do think at some level,
it also was being out of...

01:14:10.546 --> 01:14:13.048
Out of either with
my moral compass

01:14:13.048 --> 01:14:16.819 align:start size:88% position:13%
or my conscience or
some of the other things

01:14:16.819 --> 01:14:17.653
that you're talking about.

01:14:17.653 --> 01:14:18.854
- Being out of sync.

01:14:18.854 --> 01:14:21.323 align:start size:84% position:16%
There's no doubt that
being out of sync

01:14:21.323 --> 01:14:24.193 align:start size:88% position:13%
is psychophysiologically
stressful.

01:14:24.193 --> 01:14:26.562
You know, if you're torturing
yourself with your conscience.

01:14:26.562 --> 01:14:28.530
If you're at odds with yourself.

01:14:28.530 --> 01:14:31.567
You're gonna experience
that in negative emotion.

01:14:31.567 --> 01:14:35.504 align:start size:84% position:16%
Like anger, for
example or sadness.

01:14:35.504 --> 01:14:39.107
Those are intense physically.

01:14:39.107 --> 01:14:41.710
They're highly demanding states.

01:14:41.710 --> 01:14:43.745
They'll do you in.

01:14:43.745 --> 01:14:47.249 align:start size:94% position:6%
So it's certainly the
case that a bad conscience

01:14:47.249 --> 01:14:49.551
can make you ill in
all sorts of ways.

01:14:49.551 --> 01:14:53.255
Leave you open to the
invasion of pathogens.

01:14:53.255 --> 01:14:55.157
Because your immune
system is suppressed

01:14:55.157 --> 01:14:57.993
or as a consequence,

01:14:57.993 --> 01:15:00.429 align:start position:0%
in a direct
psychophysiological consequence

01:15:00.429 --> 01:15:05.400 align:start size:88% position:13%
of too much
physiological activity.

01:15:06.568 --> 01:15:07.803
- You must've thought
about that for yourself

01:15:07.803 --> 01:15:11.006
during some of your
health situations

01:15:11.006 --> 01:15:12.040
over the last two years?

01:15:12.040 --> 01:15:13.876
- Yes, definitely.

01:15:15.010 --> 01:15:16.812
Like I said, I don't...

01:15:16.812 --> 01:15:19.615 align:start position:0%
I still really don't understand
what's happened to me.

01:15:19.615 --> 01:15:22.351
It's associated with some
immunological problem.

01:15:22.351 --> 01:15:24.786
Which did manifest
itself at one point

01:15:24.786 --> 01:15:26.688
when I was young in alopecia.

01:15:26.688 --> 01:15:28.357
Which is an autoimmune disorder.

01:15:28.357 --> 01:15:30.125 align:start size:84% position:16%
Other symptoms of
autoimmune disorder.

01:15:30.125 --> 01:15:32.127
But I don't know.

01:15:32.127 --> 01:15:33.962
And I certainly asked
myself to what degree

01:15:33.962 --> 01:15:35.898
I'm out of sync with myself,

01:15:35.898 --> 01:15:40.836
but I haven't been
able to sort it out.

01:15:40.836 --> 01:15:43.805
I have the odd period of time
where my symptoms disappear.

01:15:43.805 --> 01:15:46.575 align:start size:84% position:16%
The pain disappears,
then I'm fine.

01:15:46.575 --> 01:15:49.611
As soon as the pain and
the anxiety disappear

01:15:49.611 --> 01:15:51.747
I seem to get my
feet right away.

01:15:51.747 --> 01:15:55.517 align:start size:94% position:6%
But anyways, it's an
bottomless mystery as far

01:15:55.517 --> 01:15:56.752
as I can tell.

01:15:56.752 --> 01:15:58.253
- Yeah, I think a lot of
people would find that

01:15:58.253 --> 01:15:59.655
really interesting though.

01:15:59.655 --> 01:16:02.391 align:start size:94% position:6%
When the pain, the
physical pain disappears.

01:16:03.692 --> 01:16:06.762
That psychologically
you feel right now.

01:16:06.762 --> 01:16:08.864
Cause a lot of people
just have unearned pain

01:16:08.864 --> 01:16:11.033 align:start size:97% position:3%
from whatever physical
thing they're going through.

01:16:11.033 --> 01:16:15.270
But that can sort of break
the mental part of this.

01:16:15.270 --> 01:16:17.506
Or really hamper the
mental part of this.

01:16:18.674 --> 01:16:19.875
- Yes, definitely.

01:16:22.044 --> 01:16:24.012 align:start size:84% position:16%
If you break your back
in a car accident,

01:16:24.012 --> 01:16:26.882
you're gonna be in pain
regardless of your state

01:16:26.882 --> 01:16:29.251
of moral readiness, let's say.

01:16:29.251 --> 01:16:30.218
- Right.

01:16:30.218 --> 01:16:32.821
Chapter six, abandoned ideology.

01:16:32.821 --> 01:16:35.924
I think we could all use a
little bit of this right now.

01:16:35.924 --> 01:16:39.595
- Well, that chapter
is really an injunction

01:16:39.595 --> 01:16:42.664 align:start size:94% position:6%
to allow yourself to think
in more complex terms.

01:16:42.664 --> 01:16:46.935
It's useful to reduce
a complex phenomenon

01:16:46.935 --> 01:16:48.870
to its simpler elements,

01:16:48.870 --> 01:16:53.875
but it's not useful to reduce
it to the point of absurdity.

01:16:55.377 --> 01:16:59.114 align:start size:84% position:16%
And so you can
explain hierarchical
function using power.

01:17:00.415 --> 01:17:04.386
You can explain human
dynamics using sex.

01:17:04.386 --> 01:17:08.590
Before I did that Marx did
it with economics, let's say.

01:17:08.590 --> 01:17:12.160 align:start position:0%
You can boil down human
motivation to a single dimension

01:17:12.160 --> 01:17:14.363
and then you can make
a compelling story

01:17:14.363 --> 01:17:15.797
about why that's everything.

01:17:15.797 --> 01:17:17.699
But it isn't everything.

01:17:19.534 --> 01:17:21.303
Most things are multi-variate.

01:17:21.303 --> 01:17:23.905
And a good example of
that is the wage gap

01:17:24.873 --> 01:17:26.108
between men and women.

01:17:26.108 --> 01:17:27.576
There's lots of
reasons for that.

01:17:27.576 --> 01:17:29.745
Many, not one.

01:17:29.745 --> 01:17:34.750 align:start size:97% position:3%
And it's much more satisfying
in some sense if it's one

01:17:36.051 --> 01:17:38.487
partly because then you
can identify the enemy,

01:17:38.487 --> 01:17:41.923 align:start size:97% position:3%
and you can not be the enemy
and then you can be good.

01:17:41.923 --> 01:17:45.761 align:start size:94% position:6%
And so oversimplification
is satisfying.

01:17:45.761 --> 01:17:47.896
You often don't have to
test out your theories

01:17:47.896 --> 01:17:49.831 align:start size:84% position:16%
in the real world
to see if they work.

01:17:49.831 --> 01:17:54.703 align:start position:0%
And it can give you an unearned
sense of moral superiority.

01:17:54.703 --> 01:17:56.772 align:start size:84% position:16%
And so that's what
that chapter is about.

01:18:02.044 --> 01:18:07.049
It's an injunction to allow
yourself to think complexly.

01:18:08.216 --> 01:18:10.452
What is the cause of
poverty, for example?

01:18:10.452 --> 01:18:13.155 align:start size:88% position:13%
Well, systemic
corruption is one cause.

01:18:14.556 --> 01:18:16.291
(indistinct chatter)

01:18:16.291 --> 01:18:18.460 align:start size:88% position:13%
well there's alcoholism,
there's drug abuse,

01:18:18.460 --> 01:18:20.062
there's variation
in intelligence.

01:18:20.062 --> 01:18:21.830
There's variation in
conscientiousness.

01:18:21.830 --> 01:18:23.799
There's bad luck.

01:18:23.799 --> 01:18:27.436
There's socioeconomic context.

01:18:27.436 --> 01:18:28.670
There's education,

01:18:28.670 --> 01:18:31.673
there's endless contributors.

01:18:31.673 --> 01:18:34.276
Because poverty
is not one thing.

01:18:34.276 --> 01:18:35.777
It's many things.

01:18:35.777 --> 01:18:38.680
And you use it as a place
holder in conversation,

01:18:38.680 --> 01:18:41.349 align:start size:88% position:13%
but you shouldn't
confuse that placeholder

01:18:41.349 --> 01:18:44.186
with a homogenous category.

01:18:44.186 --> 01:18:46.455
And etiology does that.

01:18:49.991 --> 01:18:53.395
And well, it's a sign of
unsophisticated thinking.

01:18:53.395 --> 01:18:55.530
- Do you sense that that's why

01:18:55.530 --> 01:19:00.502
the woke ideology or
the Marxist ideology

01:19:01.636 --> 01:19:02.838
that you were talking
about for so long.

01:19:02.838 --> 01:19:05.574
Why it specifically
is so dangerous.

01:19:05.574 --> 01:19:07.476
Because there is no play.

01:19:07.476 --> 01:19:11.747
There is no ability to analyze
something in a different way.

01:19:11.747 --> 01:19:12.981
Where we're generally-

01:19:12.981 --> 01:19:15.217
- That certainly,
any of the old G...

01:19:15.217 --> 01:19:17.753 align:start size:84% position:16%
If you really wanna
solve problems,

01:19:17.753 --> 01:19:20.555 align:start size:94% position:6%
oversimplified solutions,
just get in the way.

01:19:21.757 --> 01:19:25.727
And so then you end up focusing

01:19:25.727 --> 01:19:29.197 align:start size:84% position:16%
on anti-racism in
mathematics teaching.

01:19:29.197 --> 01:19:34.202 align:start size:84% position:16%
And that's not helpful
in all likelihood.

01:19:35.403 --> 01:19:38.073
Or if it's helpful, it's
helpful in a small way.

01:19:39.508 --> 01:19:43.078
- Chapter seven is work as
hard as you possibly can

01:19:43.078 --> 01:19:47.015
at least one thing
and see what happens.

01:19:47.015 --> 01:19:49.584
I know this one worked for me.

01:19:49.584 --> 01:19:53.588
- Well, if chapter two was,
imagine who you could be.

01:19:53.588 --> 01:19:58.426
This chapter seven, it
brings it down to earth.

01:19:59.628 --> 01:20:01.096
If you don't know
who you should be

01:20:01.096 --> 01:20:03.698
pick something, pick something.

01:20:03.698 --> 01:20:06.401 align:start size:94% position:6%
Pick something that would
be an accomplishment.

01:20:06.401 --> 01:20:08.203
Even do it arbitrarily.

01:20:08.203 --> 01:20:10.405 align:start size:84% position:16%
Because maybe you
have too many choices.

01:20:10.405 --> 01:20:12.307
You don't know what
you're interested in.

01:20:12.307 --> 01:20:15.610
Start somewhere, aim
at something, do it.

01:20:15.610 --> 01:20:18.246
Maybe it's a six month
certificate at something.

01:20:18.246 --> 01:20:21.983 align:start size:84% position:16%
Maybe it's an online
course, whatever.

01:20:21.983 --> 01:20:24.486
Pick something, work hard at it.

01:20:24.486 --> 01:20:25.954
See what happens.

01:20:25.954 --> 01:20:29.324 align:start size:97% position:3%
What'll happen is, well
you'll get more disciplined

01:20:29.324 --> 01:20:30.859
as a consequence of the work.

01:20:30.859 --> 01:20:33.195
And so that'll make you a
somewhat different person.

01:20:33.195 --> 01:20:36.498
But also as you work on
it, you'll figure out

01:20:36.498 --> 01:20:41.069
in more detail, why it's
for you or why it's not.

01:20:41.069 --> 01:20:42.537 align:start size:84% position:16%
And then when you make
the next decision,

01:20:42.537 --> 01:20:44.105
you'll be more informed.

01:20:44.105 --> 01:20:45.407
That's how a self-correcting

01:20:45.407 --> 01:20:47.876
cybernetic system
works essentially.

01:20:47.876 --> 01:20:50.212 align:start size:88% position:13%
You establish a name
and you move towards it.

01:20:50.212 --> 01:20:52.681
As you move towards it,
you gather information.

01:20:52.681 --> 01:20:54.182
That may shift your aim.

01:20:54.182 --> 01:20:56.351 align:start size:84% position:16%
But you have to move
towards the aim.

01:20:56.351 --> 01:20:59.487
- So in essence, even
if you don't accomplish

01:20:59.487 --> 01:21:00.989
what you set out to do

01:21:00.989 --> 01:21:02.624
the lessons along the way,

01:21:02.624 --> 01:21:06.795
that that is the higher
lesson at some level.

01:21:06.795 --> 01:21:08.029
- Yes.

01:21:08.029 --> 01:21:10.966 align:start size:88% position:13%
And let's say you should
try to accomplish it,

01:21:10.966 --> 01:21:13.235
but let's say, while you
get halfway through it

01:21:13.235 --> 01:21:16.271
and you think, I really
know this isn't for me.

01:21:16.271 --> 01:21:18.340
then you might say,
well, how do you know

01:21:18.340 --> 01:21:19.541
you're not just copping out?

01:21:19.541 --> 01:21:23.011 align:start size:84% position:16%
And I would say,
well, you can set up

01:21:23.011 --> 01:21:24.212
a check against that.

01:21:24.212 --> 01:21:25.914
By swearing when
you start something

01:21:25.914 --> 01:21:27.616
that you won't switch it,

01:21:27.616 --> 01:21:29.684 align:start size:84% position:16%
except for something
more difficult.

01:21:31.086 --> 01:21:33.321
Because everyone
has that tendency

01:21:33.321 --> 01:21:35.323 align:start size:84% position:16%
to rationalize their
procrastination.

01:21:35.323 --> 01:21:38.526 align:start size:84% position:16%
And that's a good
way of protecting
yourself against that.

01:21:38.526 --> 01:21:40.428
I can quit this, but I
have to immediately...

01:21:40.428 --> 01:21:42.130
I have to have a better plan.

01:21:42.130 --> 01:21:43.265
That's more difficult.

01:21:43.265 --> 01:21:45.166
And I can jump into that.

01:21:45.166 --> 01:21:47.602
And this is also associated

01:21:47.602 --> 01:21:52.307
with something like
the necessary minimum

01:21:52.307 --> 01:21:54.209
of respect for tradition.

01:21:54.209 --> 01:21:56.011
If you pick a goal,
it will be a goal.

01:21:56.011 --> 01:21:59.147
That's a social institution
of some sort, virtually.

01:21:59.147 --> 01:22:01.716 align:start size:97% position:3%
With virtual certainty,
because it has to be valuable

01:22:03.084 --> 01:22:04.853 align:start size:97% position:3%
to other people to be
valuable generally speaking.

01:22:06.187 --> 01:22:07.122
You wanna have...

01:22:07.122 --> 01:22:08.590
You don't wanna be so cynical

01:22:09.791 --> 01:22:11.293
that you stop yourself
from pursuing anything

01:22:11.293 --> 01:22:13.328
because nothing's worthwhile.

01:22:13.328 --> 01:22:15.397
That's not helpful.

01:22:15.397 --> 01:22:17.198
And everyone knows that.

01:22:17.198 --> 01:22:18.600
And you might say,
well, I can't get out

01:22:18.600 --> 01:22:20.035
of that cynicism.

01:22:20.035 --> 01:22:22.504 align:start size:97% position:3%
When I would say, well
jumping out of the cynicism

01:22:22.504 --> 01:22:24.673
may be very difficult.

01:22:24.673 --> 01:22:28.410
Try just doing something
local, just pick something.

01:22:28.410 --> 01:22:30.078
If you don't have
a relationship,

01:22:31.346 --> 01:22:33.982
make a dating profile.

01:22:33.982 --> 01:22:35.850
You don't even have
to put it online.

01:22:37.018 --> 01:22:38.320
But at least you
have the profile.

01:22:38.320 --> 01:22:40.655
You're taking a step
in that direction.

01:22:40.655 --> 01:22:44.526 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I've often thought
there's these seven things

01:22:44.526 --> 01:22:46.428
that people have to
get approximately.

01:22:47.929 --> 01:22:49.497
We went through some of them.

01:22:49.497 --> 01:22:53.768 align:start size:84% position:16%
You need an intimate
relationship, family,
job or career.

01:22:53.768 --> 01:22:56.271 align:start size:97% position:3%
You have take care of yourself
mentally and physically.

01:22:56.271 --> 01:22:57.772
Your health.

01:22:57.772 --> 01:23:00.809 align:start size:94% position:6%
You have to regulate your
drug and alcohol use.

01:23:00.809 --> 01:23:03.912 align:start size:97% position:3% line:0%
You have to use your time
productively outside of work.

01:23:05.180 --> 01:23:06.982 line:0%
There's one other
I often mentioned.

01:23:06.982 --> 01:23:08.183
I can't remember it now.

01:23:09.351 --> 01:23:11.453
It's an arbitrary
list in some sense.

01:23:11.453 --> 01:23:14.222
But if you don't have a
list, it's a good one.

01:23:14.222 --> 01:23:16.992
And maybe you can pick one of
those things and work on them.

01:23:16.992 --> 01:23:18.793
And if you have all
of those things,

01:23:18.793 --> 01:23:20.762 align:start size:84% position:16%
your life is likely
to be better.

01:23:20.762 --> 01:23:22.731
Maybe you're a true radical,

01:23:22.731 --> 01:23:25.500
and you have to live in a
way that isn't traditional.

01:23:25.500 --> 01:23:27.769
And you don't need any of that.

01:23:27.769 --> 01:23:30.005
Highly improbable
that that's true.

01:23:30.005 --> 01:23:32.173 align:start size:97% position:3%
And God only knows how you're
gonna find your own way.

01:23:32.173 --> 01:23:34.609
But until you know
that for certain,

01:23:36.011 --> 01:23:38.980
it's not such a bad idea
to adopt an apprenticeship,

01:23:38.980 --> 01:23:41.216
in at least one of these areas.

01:23:41.216 --> 01:23:43.018
And you know, the purpose
of being an apprentice

01:23:43.018 --> 01:23:44.486
isn't so that you're a slave,

01:23:44.486 --> 01:23:47.589 align:start size:97% position:3%
it's so that you become
capable of your masterpiece.

01:23:47.589 --> 01:23:50.792
Of transcending the discipline.

01:23:50.792 --> 01:23:52.193
So the idea is look,

01:23:52.193 --> 01:23:54.396 align:start size:88% position:13%
there's lots of
disciplinary strategies.

01:23:54.396 --> 01:23:56.898 align:start size:84% position:16%
There's lots of
games you could play.

01:23:56.898 --> 01:23:59.234
And so you can fall into
a moral relativism say,

01:23:59.234 --> 01:24:00.668
well all games are equal.

01:24:00.668 --> 01:24:02.404
It's like, well, no.

01:24:02.404 --> 01:24:06.474 align:start size:97% position:3%
But even if that's true, the
fact that you need a game

01:24:06.474 --> 01:24:07.609
that isn't relative.

01:24:07.609 --> 01:24:09.411
That's true, you need a game.

01:24:09.411 --> 01:24:10.311
Pick a game.

01:24:10.311 --> 01:24:11.246
Which game.

01:24:11.246 --> 01:24:12.814
Anyone is better than none.

01:24:12.814 --> 01:24:14.082
Play it hard.

01:24:14.082 --> 01:24:18.353
See what happens, you'll
change as a consequence

01:24:18.353 --> 01:24:19.854
of the pursuit.

01:24:19.854 --> 01:24:24.426
And I think you have to be
in very dire straits before

01:24:25.994 --> 01:24:27.395
that won't work.

01:24:27.395 --> 01:24:29.631 align:start size:84% position:16%
You can be in straits
that are so dire

01:24:29.631 --> 01:24:32.200
that you can't move forward.

01:24:32.200 --> 01:24:37.005 align:start size:97% position:3%
But generally you're somewhere
that you could improve

01:24:37.005 --> 01:24:38.506
in some manner.

01:24:38.506 --> 01:24:40.909 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Is just getting
people to believe that.

01:24:40.909 --> 01:24:43.445 align:start size:97% position:3%
Is that harder than actually
doing it in some ways.

01:24:43.445 --> 01:24:44.913
That we seem to be...

01:24:44.913 --> 01:24:47.182 align:start size:97% position:3%
There's so many young people
that just feel so lost.

01:24:47.182 --> 01:24:48.149
That just getting them...

01:24:48.149 --> 01:24:50.218
Just convincing them to do it

01:24:50.218 --> 01:24:53.154 align:start size:88% position:13%
is in some ways more
important than doing it.

01:24:53.154 --> 01:24:55.256
- I don't think you can
convince them exactly,

01:24:55.256 --> 01:24:56.591
but you can ask them.

01:24:56.591 --> 01:24:59.027
It's like, okay, well,
think about your life.

01:24:59.027 --> 01:25:01.963 align:start size:97% position:3%
And I do think I address this
directly in that chapter.

01:25:03.798 --> 01:25:06.968 align:start position:0%
Remember some time you
accomplish something difficult.

01:25:08.203 --> 01:25:09.971
Well, was that a good...

01:25:09.971 --> 01:25:11.406
Was that good or not?

01:25:11.406 --> 01:25:14.843
And generally the
human experience.

01:25:14.843 --> 01:25:16.177
As far as I can tell

01:25:16.177 --> 01:25:19.914 align:start position:0%
is that when you've accomplished
something difficult.

01:25:19.914 --> 01:25:23.284
You regard that as worthwhile.

01:25:23.284 --> 01:25:26.888 align:start size:97% position:3%
So look, so then you say, this
is a therapeutic technique

01:25:26.888 --> 01:25:29.390
in some sense, because you
don't try to convince people.

01:25:29.390 --> 01:25:31.025
You get them to look at
their own experience.

01:25:31.025 --> 01:25:33.595
It's like, well, it turns
out that in your life,

01:25:33.595 --> 01:25:36.164 align:start size:94% position:6%
when you've done something
that stretched you

01:25:36.164 --> 01:25:37.432
beyond where you were.

01:25:37.432 --> 01:25:38.867
So that was difficult.

01:25:38.867 --> 01:25:41.536
That you regarded
that as worthwhile.

01:25:41.536 --> 01:25:45.773 align:start size:84% position:16%
Okay, so let's assume
that that's true.

01:25:45.773 --> 01:25:47.609 align:start size:81% position:19%
And that, that
can be duplicated.

01:25:50.411 --> 01:25:51.679
- Chapter eight.

01:25:51.679 --> 01:25:53.314
I think I've done
a good job at this.

01:25:53.314 --> 01:25:56.050
But I have to mostly give the
credit to David on this one.

01:25:56.050 --> 01:26:00.522
Try to make one room in your
home as beautiful as possible.

01:26:00.522 --> 01:26:01.856
See something around you.

01:26:01.856 --> 01:26:03.258
- My favorite chapter.

01:26:04.692 --> 01:26:07.095
- Yeah, well, you've got some
interesting artistic taste.

01:26:07.095 --> 01:26:08.696
- Yeah, yeah, I suppose.

01:26:11.032 --> 01:26:14.335 align:start size:84% position:16%
That's a chapter about
the value of beauty

01:26:14.335 --> 01:26:16.871
as a marker for
the transcendent.

01:26:16.871 --> 01:26:19.073
And it's great.

01:26:19.073 --> 01:26:21.009 align:start size:88% position:13%
You wanna establish a
relationship with things

01:26:21.009 --> 01:26:22.310
that sustain you.

01:26:22.310 --> 01:26:23.545
Truth.

01:26:23.545 --> 01:26:25.980
The great verity
is truth, beauty.

01:26:25.980 --> 01:26:28.049
Beauty is difficult to define.

01:26:28.049 --> 01:26:32.453
But it's an invitation to the
transcendent, essentially.

01:26:32.453 --> 01:26:35.123 align:start size:84% position:16%
Because beauty
speaks of the ideal.

01:26:35.123 --> 01:26:39.093 align:start size:81% position:19%
And it impacts you
beyond thought.

01:26:41.296 --> 01:26:44.032
It's visceral beauty.

01:26:44.032 --> 01:26:48.636
And that's an indication
of the power of the ideal.

01:26:48.636 --> 01:26:52.273
And if you try to establish
a relationship with beauty

01:26:52.273 --> 01:26:54.709 align:start size:84% position:16%
and so to do that, you
can make one corner

01:26:54.709 --> 01:26:57.212 align:start size:84% position:16%
of your room
beautiful if you can.

01:26:57.212 --> 01:26:59.113 align:start size:84% position:16%
You start to play with
the idea of beauty.

01:26:59.113 --> 01:27:01.349 align:start size:81% position:19%
You start to learn
to discriminate

01:27:01.349 --> 01:27:03.618 align:start size:88% position:13%
between what's beautiful
and what isn't.

01:27:03.618 --> 01:27:07.255
And then you invite
that into your life.

01:27:07.255 --> 01:27:08.489
And that's a scare...

01:27:08.489 --> 01:27:11.292
That's a terrifying
thing to some degree.

01:27:12.360 --> 01:27:13.962
Because beauty is very powerful.

01:27:13.962 --> 01:27:16.598
Partly because it reminds
you of who you aren't.

01:27:18.099 --> 01:27:20.568
But it's amazingly worthwhile.

01:27:20.568 --> 01:27:24.405
And it's sort of the next step
beyond cleaning up your room.

01:27:24.405 --> 01:27:25.673
You know make an orderly,

01:27:25.673 --> 01:27:28.376
but then the next step
is make it beautiful.

01:27:29.510 --> 01:27:33.848 align:start size:84% position:16%
And beauty is a higher
value than order.

01:27:33.848 --> 01:27:37.085 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Yeah, do you sense
that most people,

01:27:37.085 --> 01:27:40.255
maybe because of the speed
that we talked about before

01:27:40.255 --> 01:27:43.057
are not putting the
primacy on this.

01:27:43.057 --> 01:27:45.593
We see a lot of people
now on Skype and Zoom

01:27:45.593 --> 01:27:46.861
and everything else.

01:27:46.861 --> 01:27:48.029
And you see a lot
of people sitting

01:27:48.029 --> 01:27:49.664 align:start size:84% position:16%
in some pretty
awful looking rooms.

01:27:49.664 --> 01:27:51.866
And terrible drapes and
stuff all over the place.

01:27:51.866 --> 01:27:56.137 align:start size:97% position:3%
So it is a connection to,
clean up your room in effect.

01:27:56.137 --> 01:27:58.139
- Well, it's that.

01:27:58.139 --> 01:28:02.443
Well, it's not easy and
it's very intimidating.

01:28:02.443 --> 01:28:06.381 align:start size:97% position:3%
There's a story in there
about color on walls, paint.

01:28:06.381 --> 01:28:10.318
My house now is much different
than it was a few years ago.

01:28:10.318 --> 01:28:13.454 align:start size:84% position:16%
But it was 32
colors at one point.

01:28:13.454 --> 01:28:15.356
And people are very
afraid of color.

01:28:15.356 --> 01:28:16.791
They'll paint their house beige.

01:28:16.791 --> 01:28:21.062 align:start size:97% position:3%
Because they don't want
their tastes to be on display

01:28:21.062 --> 01:28:23.364 align:start size:84% position:16%
because then it's
subject to judgment.

01:28:23.364 --> 01:28:28.369 align:start size:94% position:6%
And often that's wise,
because generally speaking

01:28:29.237 --> 01:28:31.105
taste is very hard to develop.

01:28:31.105 --> 01:28:34.676
I bought paintings on eBay
for a long time for a decade.

01:28:34.676 --> 01:28:38.880
And the ratio of
terrible paintings

01:28:38.880 --> 01:28:41.382
to good paintings is
unimaginably high.

01:28:41.382 --> 01:28:43.384
It's like a 1000 to 1.

01:28:43.384 --> 01:28:45.019
Maybe it's even
higher than that.

01:28:46.087 --> 01:28:48.956
Almost everything is dreck.

01:28:48.956 --> 01:28:50.892
And if you're a
beginning artist,

01:28:50.892 --> 01:28:52.160
the probability
that you're gonna

01:28:52.160 --> 01:28:53.995
produce something
spectacularly ugly

01:28:53.995 --> 01:28:56.764
is virtually a 100%.

01:28:56.764 --> 01:28:59.133 align:start size:81% position:19%
And by doing that,
you demonstrate

01:28:59.133 --> 01:29:02.603
the appalling nature
of your relationship

01:29:02.603 --> 01:29:03.738
to taste and beauty.

01:29:03.738 --> 01:29:06.274 align:start size:88% position:13%
And that's very
intimidating for people.

01:29:06.274 --> 01:29:11.279
And so that's partly
why it's so difficult

01:29:12.146 --> 01:29:13.915
for artists to make their way.

01:29:13.915 --> 01:29:17.952 align:start position:0%
Because if you're an established
artist, you have a name

01:29:17.952 --> 01:29:20.955 align:start size:97% position:3%
and then people can buy your
art because of the name.

01:29:20.955 --> 01:29:23.725
And then that validates the art.

01:29:23.725 --> 01:29:24.959
If you're a new artist,

01:29:24.959 --> 01:29:27.161
people have to rely
on their own taste

01:29:27.161 --> 01:29:28.996
and then their own
taste is on display.

01:29:28.996 --> 01:29:30.231
And that's terrifying.

01:29:30.231 --> 01:29:31.699
And I'm not being
cynical about that.

01:29:31.699 --> 01:29:34.569
I understand perfectly
well why it's terrifying.

01:29:34.569 --> 01:29:37.171 align:start size:88% position:13%
But to establish a
relationship with beauty

01:29:37.171 --> 01:29:39.240
is to dare something.

01:29:39.240 --> 01:29:42.377
It's to dare establishing a
relationship with the divine.

01:29:42.377 --> 01:29:44.579
If you're atheistic, let's say,

01:29:44.579 --> 01:29:47.315 align:start size:84% position:16%
but you're hungering
for meaning,

01:29:47.315 --> 01:29:48.983
or maybe even if
you're religious

01:29:48.983 --> 01:29:49.917 align:start size:84% position:16%
and you're hungering
for meaning.

01:29:49.917 --> 01:29:51.319
You can find it by establishing

01:29:51.319 --> 01:29:53.121
your relationship with beauty.

01:29:54.288 --> 01:29:57.158
And I think people
understand this.

01:29:57.158 --> 01:30:01.863
I think music speaks it
most clearly to people.

01:30:01.863 --> 01:30:05.266 align:start size:94% position:6%
Even people who have
terrible drapes let's say,

01:30:05.266 --> 01:30:07.235
likely have a genre of music

01:30:07.235 --> 01:30:09.036 align:start size:84% position:16%
they love that they
find beautiful,

01:30:09.036 --> 01:30:11.806 align:start size:94% position:6%
that speaks to them in the
way that beauty speaks.

01:30:11.806 --> 01:30:14.375
And music it's so democratic.

01:30:14.375 --> 01:30:16.778 align:start position:0%
That's one of the things that's
so wonderful about music.

01:30:16.778 --> 01:30:21.449
Is that virtually everyone is
affected profoundly by music.

01:30:21.449 --> 01:30:26.454 align:start size:97% position:3%
And that's a good example
of the importance of beauty.

01:30:28.489 --> 01:30:33.127
And the reality that emerges

01:30:34.295 --> 01:30:36.798
when you establish a
relationship with it.

01:30:36.798 --> 01:30:38.733
I mean, I do believe that
music is one of the things

01:30:38.733 --> 01:30:41.936 align:start size:84% position:16%
that literally
keeps people alive.

01:30:41.936 --> 01:30:45.406 align:start size:88% position:13%
- You know, I'm reminded
of many of the halls

01:30:45.406 --> 01:30:46.774 align:start size:94% position:6%
that we were in, the
theaters that we were in,

01:30:46.774 --> 01:30:48.609 align:start size:81% position:19%
they would have
pianos down there.

01:30:48.609 --> 01:30:51.479
And sometimes I would
get to the theater,

01:30:51.479 --> 01:30:54.382
an hour early and I'd hear
someone playing the piano.

01:30:54.382 --> 01:30:56.617 align:start size:84% position:16%
And I quickly realized
after the first

01:30:56.617 --> 01:30:58.052
or second time that it was you.

01:30:58.052 --> 01:31:00.254
And you were usually...

01:31:00.254 --> 01:31:01.956 align:start size:84% position:16%
You are usually
playing off your head.

01:31:01.956 --> 01:31:05.726 align:start size:97% position:3%
I mean, these were just sounds
that you had in you, right?

01:31:05.726 --> 01:31:07.962
- Yeah, well, I'm kind of a...

01:31:09.564 --> 01:31:11.899
I really only know
three piano pieces.

01:31:11.899 --> 01:31:13.301
They're all Beethoven, sonatas.

01:31:13.301 --> 01:31:15.403
They're way beyond
my ability to play.

01:31:15.403 --> 01:31:17.572
And I've tried to play
them for like 30 years.

01:31:17.572 --> 01:31:20.074
And I can't play them now

01:31:20.074 --> 01:31:22.243 align:start size:84% position:16%
cause I haven't played
them for two years.

01:31:22.243 --> 01:31:26.614 align:start size:97% position:3%
But I got to the point where
I could play them badly.

01:31:26.614 --> 01:31:28.182
But I could actually play them.

01:31:28.182 --> 01:31:29.650
- You're being humble Peterson.

01:31:29.650 --> 01:31:31.419
Come on, you were pretty good.

01:31:32.720 --> 01:31:34.856 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Well, the improvisation
that you heard,

01:31:34.856 --> 01:31:36.958 align:start size:84% position:16%
I know some chords,
not very many,

01:31:36.958 --> 01:31:39.927 align:start size:94% position:6%
and I can improvise
within my limited domain,

01:31:39.927 --> 01:31:41.429
of chord knowledge,

01:31:41.429 --> 01:31:45.032
and that's quite fun but it
really is a narrow domain.

01:31:45.032 --> 01:31:49.070 align:start size:97% position:3%
And I've dreamt of
expanding it, but I haven't.

01:31:49.070 --> 01:31:50.538
And probably won't.

01:31:50.538 --> 01:31:52.340
I'm so sensitive to sound now

01:31:52.340 --> 01:31:53.808
that I can't play the piano.

01:31:55.076 --> 01:31:57.078 align:start size:84% position:16%
I couldn't listen
to music for months.

01:31:57.078 --> 01:31:59.146
It was a terrible loss.

01:31:59.146 --> 01:31:59.981
Terrible loss.

01:31:59.981 --> 01:32:01.315
It was too jarring.

01:32:01.315 --> 01:32:06.320
And that association
with the beauty went.

01:32:07.188 --> 01:32:08.589
It was like the river dried up.

01:32:08.589 --> 01:32:10.892
The chapter's full
of poetry, not mine.

01:32:13.794 --> 01:32:16.797
And so it's an aesthetic essay.

01:32:16.797 --> 01:32:19.166
It's supposed to be beautiful.

01:32:19.166 --> 01:32:21.135 align:start size:88% position:13%
I mean, I'm hoping that
there's some beauty

01:32:21.135 --> 01:32:22.503
in all of the work...

01:32:22.503 --> 01:32:23.738
In all of the book,

01:32:23.738 --> 01:32:25.306
I always read my
sentences out loud

01:32:25.306 --> 01:32:27.308
to see if they have a rhythm.

01:32:29.377 --> 01:32:31.512
That's the poetic
element of writing.

01:32:31.512 --> 01:32:33.581
So people who are out
there who wanna write,

01:32:33.581 --> 01:32:35.082
that's something to
pay attention to.

01:32:35.082 --> 01:32:37.985
Read your sentences out loud
and see if they have a rhythm.

01:32:40.121 --> 01:32:41.355
I'm fortunate, I suppose to...

01:32:41.355 --> 01:32:42.890
Do you know who
Akira the Don is?

01:32:42.890 --> 01:32:44.425
- Of course, he's amazing.

01:32:44.425 --> 01:32:45.760
The [indistinct] stuff with you

01:32:45.760 --> 01:32:47.028
and a couple of the
other guys we know.

01:32:47.028 --> 01:32:48.529
It's incredible, incredible.

01:32:48.529 --> 01:32:50.064
- I get such a kick out of that.

01:32:50.064 --> 01:32:55.036 align:start position:0%
And it seems that my
spontaneous speech has a rhythm

01:32:55.036 --> 01:32:56.971
and he's been able to
capitalize on that.

01:32:56.971 --> 01:33:00.174 align:start position:0%
And I watched that all the time
out of the corner of my eye

01:33:00.174 --> 01:33:02.076
because it's such a
strange phenomenon

01:33:02.076 --> 01:33:04.412
to be set to music like that.

01:33:04.412 --> 01:33:06.881
But anyways

01:33:06.881 --> 01:33:09.884
- How important do you think
is the creation part of it?

01:33:09.884 --> 01:33:13.854 align:start size:94% position:6%
So some people can create
incredible sonatas,

01:33:13.854 --> 01:33:15.990
and incredible paintings.

01:33:15.990 --> 01:33:18.326 align:start size:84% position:16%
Some people can just
appreciate them.

01:33:18.326 --> 01:33:20.828 align:start size:97% position:3%
Do you think it's important
that the creation part

01:33:20.828 --> 01:33:22.763 align:start size:84% position:16%
is part of what
everyone does or no?

01:33:22.763 --> 01:33:24.799 align:start size:84% position:16%
- I think it depends
on who you are.

01:33:24.799 --> 01:33:28.302 align:start size:94% position:6%
I mean, look, openness
this personality dimension

01:33:28.302 --> 01:33:30.471 align:start size:88% position:13%
that we mentioned,
creativity, essentially.

01:33:33.507 --> 01:33:36.844
Very few people are
profoundly creative.

01:33:36.844 --> 01:33:39.280
Just like very few people
are profoundly extroverted.

01:33:39.280 --> 01:33:42.249 align:start size:88% position:13%
Or profoundly agreeable
compassionate.

01:33:42.249 --> 01:33:43.484
There's variation.

01:33:43.484 --> 01:33:45.620
And I've noticed this
in my clinical practice

01:33:45.620 --> 01:33:48.322
because I've had
very open people.

01:33:48.322 --> 01:33:50.458
They're probably drawn to me.

01:33:50.458 --> 01:33:51.993 align:start size:84% position:16%
But I've also had
people in my practice

01:33:51.993 --> 01:33:53.394
who weren't open at all.

01:33:53.394 --> 01:33:57.932 align:start size:88% position:13%
And for them creativity,
isn't very important.

01:33:57.932 --> 01:34:00.401 align:start size:88% position:13%
It isn't where they
draw their nourishment.

01:34:00.401 --> 01:34:02.837
Now that doesn't mean
they couldn't establish

01:34:02.837 --> 01:34:04.405
some relationship with beauty.

01:34:04.405 --> 01:34:06.340
And I suspect that
for some people,

01:34:06.340 --> 01:34:09.010 align:start size:88% position:13%
that's a better pathway
than for others.

01:34:09.010 --> 01:34:13.781
For open people aesthetic
beauty is bread and butter.

01:34:13.781 --> 01:34:15.750
It's not optional.

01:34:15.750 --> 01:34:19.186
And that may even extend
to the creating aspect.

01:34:19.186 --> 01:34:20.855
They have to create.

01:34:20.855 --> 01:34:23.457
That's who they are.

01:34:23.457 --> 01:34:26.694
Just like an extrovert has
to go to social occasions.

01:34:26.694 --> 01:34:29.096
That's who they are,
that's their niche.

01:34:31.065 --> 01:34:33.267
But I think that beauty is...

01:34:37.271 --> 01:34:39.306
If you can establish a
relationship with it,

01:34:39.306 --> 01:34:41.208
so much the better for you.

01:34:41.208 --> 01:34:42.877
And I suppose for
everyone else too.

01:34:42.877 --> 01:34:44.845 align:start size:84% position:16%
Because then things
become more beautiful.

01:34:44.845 --> 01:34:47.048
It would be wonderful if we
paid more attention to that.

01:34:47.048 --> 01:34:48.549
Because so much
of what we create

01:34:48.549 --> 01:34:50.484 align:start size:84% position:16%
is one of the things
I'm so stunned

01:34:50.484 --> 01:34:53.721
that stuns me going to Europe.

01:34:53.721 --> 01:34:58.325
Because so much of the
old European cities.

01:35:00.561 --> 01:35:03.364 align:start size:94% position:6%
They're so beautiful that
they stop your heart.

01:35:03.364 --> 01:35:06.434
And you just can't say that
about most modern cities.

01:35:06.434 --> 01:35:07.368
That's gone.

01:35:07.368 --> 01:35:08.436
Or most modern landscapes.

01:35:08.436 --> 01:35:10.404
It's utilitarian and efficient,

01:35:10.404 --> 01:35:12.139
but it's by no means beautiful.

01:35:12.139 --> 01:35:15.042
And it's a catastrophic loss.

01:35:15.042 --> 01:35:17.378 align:start size:94% position:6%
You think of the economic
value of the beauty

01:35:17.378 --> 01:35:19.847
that's in Europe.

01:35:19.847 --> 01:35:22.683 align:start size:97% position:3%
And the tourists that flock
there, in the millions,

01:35:22.683 --> 01:35:26.587 align:start position:0%
the tens of millions to immerse
themselves in that beauty.

01:35:26.587 --> 01:35:28.856 align:start size:84% position:16%
Even not knowing why
they're doing it.

01:35:28.856 --> 01:35:31.325 align:start size:84% position:16%
It's their communion
with the divine.

01:35:31.325 --> 01:35:32.560
It really is.

01:35:32.560 --> 01:35:34.095
- I mean, just to
walk around Oxford,

01:35:34.095 --> 01:35:35.763
which I think we
did two shows in,

01:35:35.763 --> 01:35:39.266
and then I went for the the
speech at the union there.

01:35:39.266 --> 01:35:40.701
It's extraordinary.

01:35:40.701 --> 01:35:42.203
It's actually mind blowing.

01:35:42.203 --> 01:35:45.272
You cannot believe that
people put this together.

01:35:45.272 --> 01:35:46.574
- Yes, exactly.

01:35:46.574 --> 01:35:48.676
And then that tells you
something about people.

01:35:48.676 --> 01:35:49.510
Doesn't it?

01:35:49.510 --> 01:35:51.812
Look at what we can do.

01:35:51.812 --> 01:35:54.215
Look at what we can manage.

01:35:54.215 --> 01:35:59.186
And that's such a gift
to be immersed in that

01:35:59.186 --> 01:36:00.488
and to participate in that.

01:36:01.489 --> 01:36:03.424
And so that's chapter seven.

01:36:03.424 --> 01:36:05.626
- So chapter...

01:36:05.626 --> 01:36:08.696
So that's actually chapter
eight, and chapter nine.

01:36:08.696 --> 01:36:10.865
Chapter nine, I thought this
one was really interesting

01:36:10.865 --> 01:36:14.201
because this I think may be
came up more consistently

01:36:14.201 --> 01:36:16.504
in the Q&amp;A than anything else.

01:36:17.538 --> 01:36:20.207
If old memories still upset you,

01:36:20.207 --> 01:36:22.409 align:start size:94% position:6%
write them down
carefully and completely.

01:36:22.409 --> 01:36:23.811
There was a lot of questions

01:36:23.811 --> 01:36:25.212
related to this sort of thing.

01:36:25.212 --> 01:36:27.481
Where people would
say, okay, Jordan,

01:36:27.481 --> 01:36:31.318
I've followed the 12 rules and
I'm not an alcoholic anymore.

01:36:31.318 --> 01:36:32.253
I'm off drugs.

01:36:32.253 --> 01:36:33.487
I've healed this relationship.

01:36:33.487 --> 01:36:34.655
I got a better job.

01:36:34.655 --> 01:36:36.357
Some version of all of that.

01:36:36.357 --> 01:36:37.625
But what they would often say.

01:36:37.625 --> 01:36:39.593
And I think I talked
to you about this.

01:36:39.593 --> 01:36:40.828
Sometimes with myself.

01:36:40.828 --> 01:36:43.330 align:start size:88% position:13%
I still felt guilt about
things from the past.

01:36:43.330 --> 01:36:45.299
Even though my life
had moved past them.

01:36:45.299 --> 01:36:46.567
And you would always say,

01:36:47.735 --> 01:36:48.869
well you should do the
self-authoring program.

01:36:48.869 --> 01:36:50.304
Which I did do.

01:36:50.304 --> 01:36:51.872
But that in essence is
what you're saying here.

01:36:51.872 --> 01:36:53.374
I think as well.

01:36:53.374 --> 01:36:57.178
- Well, if something still
has emotional resonance.

01:36:57.178 --> 01:37:00.481
So it's plaguing you,
it's, possessing you.

01:37:00.481 --> 01:37:01.849
You can't let go of it.

01:37:01.849 --> 01:37:03.150
It's your baggage.

01:37:03.150 --> 01:37:04.718
You don't wanna think about it.

01:37:06.620 --> 01:37:09.023
Look, if you're in the
midst of a catastrophe,

01:37:09.023 --> 01:37:12.660
dwelling on the catastrophe is
not necessarily a good idea.

01:37:12.660 --> 01:37:15.863 align:start size:94% position:6%
I'm talking about we could
use as a rule of thumb

01:37:15.863 --> 01:37:17.998 align:start size:88% position:13%
things that happened
more than two years ago.

01:37:17.998 --> 01:37:19.567
That would be safe.

01:37:19.567 --> 01:37:21.769
Because otherwise maybe
they're still going on

01:37:21.769 --> 01:37:23.070
and you're immersed in them

01:37:23.070 --> 01:37:24.872 align:start size:81% position:19%
and you can't make
sense of them.

01:37:24.872 --> 01:37:27.775 align:start size:97% position:3%
And dwelling on them just
makes the catastrophe worse.

01:37:27.775 --> 01:37:30.377
Old memories, things, you
know you should be over.

01:37:30.377 --> 01:37:32.546 align:start size:84% position:16%
If they're still
emotionally resonant.

01:37:32.546 --> 01:37:35.249 align:start size:84% position:16%
Especially if the
emotion is negative.

01:37:35.249 --> 01:37:39.286 align:start position:0%
Anger, fear, hatred,
resentment, something like that.

01:37:39.286 --> 01:37:42.489 align:start size:88% position:13%
Then there's information
locked up in that

01:37:42.489 --> 01:37:44.592
that you haven't unpacked.

01:37:44.592 --> 01:37:45.993
And what that means.

01:37:45.993 --> 01:37:48.362 align:start position:0%
See, because you build yourself
out of the information,

01:37:48.362 --> 01:37:50.097
that you encounter in the world.

01:37:51.498 --> 01:37:54.501 align:start size:97% position:3%
What that means is that
part of you is still stuck.

01:37:54.501 --> 01:37:58.239 align:start position:0%
It's as if part of you is
still stuck in that experience.

01:37:58.239 --> 01:38:01.675 align:start size:97% position:3%
Or it's as if there's
territory that you traversed

01:38:01.675 --> 01:38:03.143
that you haven't mapped.

01:38:03.143 --> 01:38:06.046 align:start size:94% position:6%
And so you might fall
into the same hole again.

01:38:06.046 --> 01:38:08.048 align:start size:84% position:16%
And your emotions
are indicating that.

01:38:08.048 --> 01:38:10.284 align:start size:84% position:16%
They're saying
there's danger here.

01:38:10.284 --> 01:38:11.452
There's danger here.

01:38:11.452 --> 01:38:14.188
There's unprocessed danger here.

01:38:14.188 --> 01:38:15.022
Attend to it.

01:38:15.022 --> 01:38:16.523
But you don't want to.

01:38:16.523 --> 01:38:19.360
And no wonder, you
have to dig into it,

01:38:19.360 --> 01:38:20.828
and then you have to map it.

01:38:20.828 --> 01:38:25.666
And your map is effective
when you've rearranged

01:38:26.834 --> 01:38:28.369
your perceptions
or your behavior.

01:38:28.369 --> 01:38:30.905
So that that event is unlikely
to occur again in the future.

01:38:30.905 --> 01:38:32.473 align:start size:88% position:13%
That's really the
purpose of your memory.

01:38:32.473 --> 01:38:34.041
Is you remember things

01:38:34.041 --> 01:38:36.644 align:start size:94% position:6%
so you don't make the same
stupid mistakes again.

01:38:38.312 --> 01:38:40.581
That's the pragmatic
utility of memory.

01:38:40.581 --> 01:38:43.984
And so you see this to some
degree in psychotherapy.

01:38:45.352 --> 01:38:49.189 align:start size:97% position:3%
The [indistinct] in particular
emphasized past trauma.

01:38:49.189 --> 01:38:52.092
And you can emphasize
that far too much.

01:38:54.128 --> 01:38:56.330
It's not as if a
therapeutic process

01:38:56.330 --> 01:39:00.334
should be necessarily obsessive
concern with your childhood.

01:39:00.334 --> 01:39:03.637
But if you're still
plagued by memories

01:39:03.637 --> 01:39:05.706
you can't let them go.

01:39:05.706 --> 01:39:07.241
They're not mapped.

01:39:07.241 --> 01:39:12.146
It's an indication that you
have not fully mined the...

01:39:19.119 --> 01:39:22.990
You have not fully benefited
from what you've been offered.

01:39:23.857 --> 01:39:25.826
There's wisdom laying there.

01:39:25.826 --> 01:39:29.763
And so you can do that
by writing this happened

01:39:29.763 --> 01:39:30.698
this is what I remember.

01:39:30.698 --> 01:39:31.765
This is how it makes me feel.

01:39:31.765 --> 01:39:33.600
Well, why did it happen?

01:39:33.600 --> 01:39:34.868
What could have I
done differently?

01:39:34.868 --> 01:39:36.770
What's the significance now?

01:39:36.770 --> 01:39:38.272
Am I misinterpreting this?

01:39:38.272 --> 01:39:40.607
There's a lot of clinical
stories that I modified

01:39:40.607 --> 01:39:44.378
because for no concern
for my client's privacy.

01:39:44.378 --> 01:39:46.480
But I kept the stories intact.

01:39:51.118 --> 01:39:52.987
You reconfigure your personality

01:39:52.987 --> 01:39:55.189
by dealing with what you
have not yet dealt with.

01:39:55.189 --> 01:39:57.624 align:start size:97% position:3%
And then it stops interfering
with your present.

01:39:58.826 --> 01:40:02.529
It might be that you
view your current...

01:40:02.529 --> 01:40:05.199 align:start size:94% position:6%
The person you have a
current relationship with

01:40:05.199 --> 01:40:07.134
through eyes that
have been affected

01:40:07.134 --> 01:40:10.871 align:start size:84% position:16%
by a multitude of
relationship failures.

01:40:10.871 --> 01:40:15.275 align:start size:97% position:3%
And so you're projecting
assumptions onto this person

01:40:15.275 --> 01:40:19.380
and because you've been hurt

01:40:19.380 --> 01:40:20.948 align:start size:84% position:16%
and you have to
untangle all of that

01:40:20.948 --> 01:40:23.017
before you can get beyond that.

01:40:24.151 --> 01:40:26.820 align:start size:88% position:13%
And so you catch up
with yourself that way.

01:40:26.820 --> 01:40:28.422
So that's what that's...

01:40:28.422 --> 01:40:29.957
That's an injunction to do that.

01:40:29.957 --> 01:40:31.658
To clean up your house.

01:40:31.658 --> 01:40:32.926
- And if you don't,

01:40:32.926 --> 01:40:34.561
it leads to a lot
of the other stuff

01:40:34.561 --> 01:40:36.363
that you've been
talking about here.

01:40:36.363 --> 01:40:37.431
Number 10.

01:40:38.599 --> 01:40:41.135 align:start size:88% position:13%
Well, it sorta works off
the relationship part

01:40:41.135 --> 01:40:42.436
that you just mentioned.

01:40:42.436 --> 01:40:45.239
Plan and work diligently
to maintain the romance

01:40:45.239 --> 01:40:46.974
in your relationship.

01:40:46.974 --> 01:40:48.709 align:start size:97% position:3%
You know, one of the things
that I consistently saw

01:40:48.709 --> 01:40:51.478
on the tour is that you and
Tammy really love each other.

01:40:51.478 --> 01:40:53.180
And there was a lot
of struggles there

01:40:53.180 --> 01:40:55.115
obviously with her
health at first.

01:40:55.115 --> 01:40:57.684
But it was just obvious the
way you behave towards you...

01:40:57.684 --> 01:40:59.186
You know, after
every single show

01:40:59.186 --> 01:41:00.921
I usually go into the
green room with you.

01:41:00.921 --> 01:41:04.425 align:start size:88% position:13%
We do a little download
of what happened.

01:41:04.425 --> 01:41:06.493 align:start size:94% position:6%
And then immediately
when Tammy would walk in,

01:41:06.493 --> 01:41:09.763 align:start size:97% position:3%
you'd say the same thing every
night, how was it tonight?

01:41:09.763 --> 01:41:11.098
And you meant it.

01:41:11.098 --> 01:41:13.267 align:start size:88% position:13%
You wanted to know,
she's the most important

01:41:13.267 --> 01:41:14.468
person in your life.

01:41:14.468 --> 01:41:16.303 align:start size:84% position:16%
And you wanted to
know what she thought

01:41:16.303 --> 01:41:18.272
of what you just did.

01:41:18.272 --> 01:41:19.440
And that was important.

01:41:21.275 --> 01:41:22.609
- Yeah, and we're
still together.

01:41:22.609 --> 01:41:26.080 align:start size:84% position:16%
Despite I feel bad
for her unfortunately,

01:41:26.080 --> 01:41:28.715
having to live with
me at current time.

01:41:28.715 --> 01:41:30.184
It's very difficult.

01:41:30.184 --> 01:41:33.020 align:start size:88% position:13%
And she's certainly been
through her own hell,

01:41:33.954 --> 01:41:37.758
but has come out of it intact.

01:41:37.758 --> 01:41:40.327
So this chapter.

01:41:41.595 --> 01:41:42.930
Romance is like beauty.

01:41:42.930 --> 01:41:45.065
It's grace, it's a gift.

01:41:50.571 --> 01:41:52.139
But you have to work at it.

01:41:52.139 --> 01:41:53.440
It's a gift sometimes,

01:41:53.440 --> 01:41:55.209
you fall romantically
in love with someone.

01:41:55.209 --> 01:41:57.277
And there'll be the
romantic adventures

01:41:57.277 --> 01:41:58.545
that go along with that.

01:41:58.545 --> 01:42:01.782 align:start size:84% position:16%
But it's only a gift
for a short time.

01:42:01.782 --> 01:42:03.183
After that it has
to be something

01:42:03.183 --> 01:42:06.086
that you strive to attain.

01:42:06.086 --> 01:42:08.789
And even in the initial
phases of a relationship,

01:42:08.789 --> 01:42:11.191 align:start size:88% position:13%
you strive, you put
your best foot forward.

01:42:11.191 --> 01:42:12.726
If you're trying to
impress the person.

01:42:12.726 --> 01:42:14.328
You could say, well,
that's being false.

01:42:14.328 --> 01:42:18.031
But well maybe it's
allowing who you are

01:42:18.031 --> 01:42:20.968
to shine through briefly.

01:42:20.968 --> 01:42:24.705 align:start size:97% position:3%
If you're not a complete
psychopath, and manipulative,

01:42:24.705 --> 01:42:26.140
you put some effort into it.

01:42:26.140 --> 01:42:27.407 align:start size:94% position:6%
And then you think,
well, I've fallen in love

01:42:27.407 --> 01:42:29.009
with this person that
should just continue.

01:42:29.009 --> 01:42:30.878 align:start size:84% position:16%
It's like that
isn't how it works.

01:42:30.878 --> 01:42:32.346
You have to...

01:42:32.346 --> 01:42:34.114 align:start size:94% position:6%
Just like if you want
your room to be beautiful.

01:42:34.114 --> 01:42:36.016
You have to buy
paintings and art

01:42:36.016 --> 01:42:39.219 align:start size:94% position:6%
and furniture that's
functional and beautiful.

01:42:39.219 --> 01:42:40.621
And you have to work at it.

01:42:40.621 --> 01:42:41.555
You have to attend to it.

01:42:41.555 --> 01:42:43.824
And romance is the same thing.

01:42:43.824 --> 01:42:45.392
It's vital, but it's delicate.

01:42:45.392 --> 01:42:47.127
It's a form of play.

01:42:47.127 --> 01:42:49.329
And play in children.

01:42:49.329 --> 01:42:53.800 align:start position:0%
If any other motivational state
emerges in a child's psyche

01:42:53.800 --> 01:42:54.968
they'll stop playing.

01:42:54.968 --> 01:42:56.336
Hunger, thirst, they're tired.

01:42:56.336 --> 01:42:58.071
They're hot, they're cold.

01:42:58.071 --> 01:42:59.039
They're lonesome.

01:42:59.039 --> 01:43:00.507
All of that will disrupt play.

01:43:00.507 --> 01:43:02.242
Even though play is vital.

01:43:02.242 --> 01:43:05.546
And romance is the
adult form of play.

01:43:05.546 --> 01:43:08.482
And the conditions have
to be set up for it.

01:43:08.482 --> 01:43:11.218 align:start size:88% position:13%
And so when I was
doing my clinical work,

01:43:11.218 --> 01:43:12.452
I would say to my clients,

01:43:12.452 --> 01:43:14.788
Well set at least one
evening a week aside

01:43:14.788 --> 01:43:17.658
for a romantic adventure.

01:43:17.658 --> 01:43:19.927
And then figure out what
that's gonna look like.

01:43:19.927 --> 01:43:21.128
What would you like?

01:43:21.128 --> 01:43:22.596
Well, then you have
to ask yourself,

01:43:22.596 --> 01:43:24.565 align:start size:97% position:3%
"Well what is it that I
would like, what do I need?"

01:43:24.565 --> 01:43:25.799 align:start size:84% position:16%
And then that's
often real difficult

01:43:25.799 --> 01:43:27.467
because you won't even admit it.

01:43:27.467 --> 01:43:29.069
You wanna keep it in the fog.

01:43:30.270 --> 01:43:32.506
You don't wanna have the
fight with your partner.

01:43:32.506 --> 01:43:35.275 align:start size:97% position:3%
You don't wanna have the
discussion with your partner.

01:43:35.275 --> 01:43:37.844
You don't want to light candles.

01:43:37.844 --> 01:43:39.313
You feel foolish.

01:43:39.313 --> 01:43:41.949 align:start position:0%
You don't wanna put on some
clothes that are semi attractive

01:43:41.949 --> 01:43:44.518 align:start size:94% position:6%
because you're embarrassed
about your body

01:43:44.518 --> 01:43:46.787
or whatever it might be.

01:43:46.787 --> 01:43:49.323
This is all extremely difficult.

01:43:49.323 --> 01:43:53.894 align:start size:84% position:16%
But you can get better
and better at it.

01:43:53.894 --> 01:43:55.529
And maybe it can be more than...

01:43:55.529 --> 01:43:58.632 align:start size:84% position:16%
Hell do it once a
month to begin with.

01:43:58.632 --> 01:44:00.601
And do it badly.

01:44:00.601 --> 01:44:05.606
Allow your partner to be crabby
and not fully immersed in it

01:44:06.473 --> 01:44:07.407
until you get good at it.

01:44:07.407 --> 01:44:08.909
You might have to do it 40 times

01:44:08.909 --> 01:44:11.278
before you're even
vaguely good at it.

01:44:11.278 --> 01:44:13.380 align:start size:81% position:19%
Like anything else
that's complex,

01:44:13.380 --> 01:44:16.950
but you might as well do it.

01:44:16.950 --> 01:44:20.721
And then maybe you
can keep the mystery

01:44:20.721 --> 01:44:22.489 align:start size:84% position:16%
of your partner
alive to some degree.

01:44:22.489 --> 01:44:24.491
And you won't be so tempted

01:44:24.491 --> 01:44:28.729
to find your romantic
adventure elsewhere.

01:44:28.729 --> 01:44:30.197
- You know, it's funny,

01:44:30.197 --> 01:44:31.898 align:start size:97% position:3%
this may not be the exact
example you're going for here.

01:44:31.898 --> 01:44:34.201 align:start size:84% position:16%
But I know and you
got to spend some time

01:44:34.201 --> 01:44:35.702
with David as well.

01:44:36.803 --> 01:44:38.305 align:start size:84% position:16%
We've been together
for 10 years.

01:44:38.305 --> 01:44:41.008
If I can make him laugh still,
which I try to do every day.

01:44:41.008 --> 01:44:42.209 align:start size:81% position:19%
And I'm usually
pretty good at it.

01:44:42.209 --> 01:44:44.511 align:start size:88% position:13%
It's like, to me, that's
the greatest thing.

01:44:44.511 --> 01:44:47.281 align:start position:0%
Like, wow, after 10 years, this
guy still thinks I'm funny.

01:44:47.281 --> 01:44:50.117 align:start size:97% position:3%
Like he knows all my tricks
and he still thinks I'm funny.

01:44:50.117 --> 01:44:51.818
That's pretty good right there.

01:44:52.686 --> 01:44:53.620
- Absolutely.

01:44:53.620 --> 01:44:56.156
Well, that's a form of play too.

01:44:56.156 --> 01:45:01.094 align:start size:88% position:13%
And Tammy and I put a
lot of effort into that.

01:45:02.195 --> 01:45:03.363
And it definitely paid off.

01:45:03.363 --> 01:45:07.401 align:start size:84% position:16%
And we put ourselves
on the line.

01:45:07.401 --> 01:45:10.037 align:start size:84% position:16%
She's quite
confident physically.

01:45:10.037 --> 01:45:12.105
Much more so than I was.

01:45:12.105 --> 01:45:14.474
Probably much more
so than I still am.

01:45:14.474 --> 01:45:16.677
And that was very helpful to me.

01:45:16.677 --> 01:45:19.946
But then it's another situation

01:45:19.946 --> 01:45:21.348
where you can imagine

01:45:21.348 --> 01:45:23.684 align:start size:94% position:6%
you could have the
perfect romantic evening.

01:45:23.684 --> 01:45:25.519
Well, you could maybe.

01:45:26.953 --> 01:45:30.057
It's not gonna be easy to get
to perfect that's for sure.

01:45:30.057 --> 01:45:33.827 align:start position:0%
But you could get to a
reasonable approximation of it.

01:45:33.827 --> 01:45:36.063
And why not?

01:45:36.063 --> 01:45:38.999
You don't have anything
better to do than that.

01:45:38.999 --> 01:45:40.801
So that's that chapter.

01:45:40.801 --> 01:45:42.035
It's very practical.

01:45:42.035 --> 01:45:44.004
The book is like the
first book I thought.

01:45:45.472 --> 01:45:50.177
I try to discuss things at the
highest level of abstraction.

01:45:50.177 --> 01:45:52.479 align:start size:84% position:16%
Even religious level
of abstraction,

01:45:52.479 --> 01:45:54.715
but it's all very practical.

01:45:54.715 --> 01:45:56.049
- It's unbelievably
practical actually.

01:45:56.049 --> 01:45:57.751
And I think that's the secret.

01:45:57.751 --> 01:45:58.985
Two more.

01:45:58.985 --> 01:46:00.954
So chapter 11 is, do
not allow yourself

01:46:00.954 --> 01:46:04.224 align:start size:84% position:16%
to become resentful
deceitful or arrogant.

01:46:04.224 --> 01:46:05.792
So there's sort of
been a through line

01:46:05.792 --> 01:46:08.929
through a lot of the other
chapters that have fit this.

01:46:08.929 --> 01:46:10.263
But it ain't easy.

01:46:10.263 --> 01:46:12.165 align:start size:84% position:16%
Even when you
know, you shouldn't

01:46:12.165 --> 01:46:15.268
- That chapter, I try
to deal forthrightly

01:46:15.268 --> 01:46:18.071 align:start size:81% position:19%
with the domain
of human problems.

01:46:18.071 --> 01:46:20.140 align:start size:88% position:13%
And that's the
mythological landscape.

01:46:20.140 --> 01:46:24.211
There's utter chaos when
everything falls apart.

01:46:24.211 --> 01:46:27.013 align:start size:97% position:3%
And that's symbolized often
by the dragon of chaos.

01:46:27.013 --> 01:46:30.016
An unbelievably ancient symbol.

01:46:30.016 --> 01:46:33.754 align:start position:0%
And it's a symbol of everything
that conspires against us.

01:46:33.754 --> 01:46:37.023 align:start size:97% position:3%
It's a symbol of malevolence
and evil, of predation.

01:46:37.023 --> 01:46:39.192 align:start size:84% position:16%
And it's also a
symbol of possibility.

01:46:39.192 --> 01:46:40.394
And it's complicated.

01:46:40.394 --> 01:46:41.595
And so I won't go into it.

01:46:41.595 --> 01:46:43.663
But dragon's hoard treasure.

01:46:43.663 --> 01:46:46.466
and that's enough said.

01:46:46.466 --> 01:46:48.902
And everyone knows that
even though it doesn't...

01:46:51.138 --> 01:46:53.306
It's a paradox.

01:46:53.306 --> 01:46:55.008
It's not logical,
it's a paradox.

01:46:55.008 --> 01:46:57.544 align:start size:84% position:16%
It's the coming
together of opposites.

01:46:57.544 --> 01:46:58.779
So there's that.

01:46:58.779 --> 01:47:01.214
There's the utter
chaos of existence.

01:47:02.582 --> 01:47:05.719 align:start size:97% position:3%
Then there's the natural
world conspiring to do you in

01:47:05.719 --> 01:47:08.255
at every level constantly.

01:47:08.255 --> 01:47:11.892 align:start size:94% position:6%
And that can be cancer,
which is life gone astray.

01:47:11.892 --> 01:47:15.328
It can be all the horrors

01:47:15.328 --> 01:47:18.598
of the eating and the digestion

01:47:18.598 --> 01:47:22.402 align:start size:88% position:13%
and the reproduction and
the whole horrid mess

01:47:22.402 --> 01:47:25.806 align:start size:88% position:13%
that's biology and that
we're subjected to.

01:47:25.806 --> 01:47:27.574
And in the beneficiaries of...

01:47:28.608 --> 01:47:29.543
And then there's culture,

01:47:29.543 --> 01:47:31.211
which is crushing you constantly

01:47:31.211 --> 01:47:34.481
to be a cog in the
functional wheel.

01:47:34.481 --> 01:47:37.184 align:start size:84% position:16%
And then there's
your own black heart.

01:47:37.184 --> 01:47:41.721 align:start size:97% position:3%
And all of those things
can twist you against life.

01:47:41.721 --> 01:47:44.925 align:start size:97% position:3%
And no wonder that makes
you resentful and no wonder.

01:47:44.925 --> 01:47:47.461
It's no wonder it's a miracle,

01:47:47.461 --> 01:47:48.662
that you can avoid that,

01:47:48.662 --> 01:47:51.531 align:start size:88% position:13%
but there's nothing
about it that's helpful.

01:47:51.531 --> 01:47:54.034 align:start size:84% position:16%
You become arrogant,
you can't learn.

01:47:54.034 --> 01:47:55.001
You become resentful.

01:47:55.001 --> 01:47:56.102
You make everything worse.

01:47:56.102 --> 01:47:58.138
You become deceitful.

01:47:58.138 --> 01:48:00.941
You corrupt being itself.

01:48:02.142 --> 01:48:03.610
It's so tempting.

01:48:04.778 --> 01:48:06.179
And so I outlined why it's like,

01:48:06.179 --> 01:48:07.981
"Look, here's what we face."

01:48:07.981 --> 01:48:11.751
It's bleak, it's bleak,
but it's not only bleak.

01:48:11.751 --> 01:48:13.286
We all die.

01:48:13.286 --> 01:48:17.290 align:start size:88% position:13%
We have to deal with
self-conscious finitude.

01:48:17.290 --> 01:48:19.826
And the knowledge of suffering.

01:48:19.826 --> 01:48:23.463 align:start size:97% position:3%
And the knowledge of injustice
and tyranny, all of that.

01:48:23.463 --> 01:48:24.764
And human evil.

01:48:33.073 --> 01:48:34.241
- What, that's it.

01:48:36.977 --> 01:48:41.982
- There's nothing good
down those pathways.

01:48:42.849 --> 01:48:44.084
Resentment in particular.

01:48:44.084 --> 01:48:45.919
I think resentment is
unbelievably useful.

01:48:45.919 --> 01:48:48.054
And so for practical purposes,

01:48:48.054 --> 01:48:50.390
for everyone who's listening.

01:48:50.390 --> 01:48:51.825
If you're resentful
about something.

01:48:51.825 --> 01:48:53.693 align:start size:97% position:3%
Well first of all, you
should notice I'm resentful,

01:48:53.693 --> 01:48:54.961
and that's kinda humiliating.

01:48:54.961 --> 01:48:57.664 align:start size:84% position:16%
Because no one's proud
of being resentful.

01:48:57.664 --> 01:48:59.232
It's a shameful emotion.

01:49:00.934 --> 01:49:02.502
So then why are you resentful?

01:49:02.502 --> 01:49:03.904
Well, there's only two reasons.

01:49:03.904 --> 01:49:05.171 align:start size:81% position:19%
As far as I've
been able to tell.

01:49:05.171 --> 01:49:07.707
One is that you're
allowing yourself

01:49:07.707 --> 01:49:09.109
to be taken advantage of

01:49:09.109 --> 01:49:11.411 align:start size:84% position:16%
or you're being
taken advantage of.

01:49:11.411 --> 01:49:12.879
Sometimes you're not
allowing yourself.

01:49:12.879 --> 01:49:15.949 align:start size:97% position:3%
Sometimes you're a child
being abused by their uncle.

01:49:15.949 --> 01:49:18.318
You're not allowing yourself.

01:49:18.318 --> 01:49:20.320
You can't do anything about it.

01:49:24.724 --> 01:49:26.960 align:start position:0%
You're resentful because
you're being taken advantage of

01:49:26.960 --> 01:49:30.330
or because you're immature.

01:49:33.700 --> 01:49:34.534
It's one of those two things.

01:49:34.534 --> 01:49:35.802
Figure out which it is.

01:49:35.802 --> 01:49:38.538
And then take the steps
necessary to address it.

01:49:38.538 --> 01:49:41.508 align:start size:97% position:3%
Living a life without
resentment is a great goal.

01:49:43.243 --> 01:49:44.945 align:start size:84% position:16%
You watch people
drop the resentment.

01:49:44.945 --> 01:49:46.112
They get younger.

01:49:46.112 --> 01:49:48.415 align:start size:88% position:13%
They get visibly younger
when they drop it.

01:49:48.415 --> 01:49:50.250
It's amazing to see that.

01:49:50.250 --> 01:49:51.985
I saw that my clinical
clients all the time.

01:49:51.985 --> 01:49:53.186
They'd be resentful
about something.

01:49:53.186 --> 01:49:55.388 align:start size:88% position:13%
Their face would
harden, they'd get old.

01:49:55.388 --> 01:49:57.257
And then they'd let it go.

01:49:57.257 --> 01:49:59.092
And sometimes that was
accompanied by tears.

01:49:59.092 --> 01:50:01.628
And you have to let it go lots.

01:50:01.628 --> 01:50:03.563
Just once won't do it.

01:50:03.563 --> 01:50:07.534 align:start size:88% position:13%
But they'd let it go
and then they'd be calm.

01:50:07.534 --> 01:50:10.270 align:start size:97% position:3%
And their face would be
unlined at least for a while.

01:50:10.270 --> 01:50:14.274
They'd sort of return to a
childlike state of trust.

01:50:14.274 --> 01:50:15.675
Or they would retain that.

01:50:15.675 --> 01:50:17.744
That's a better way
of thinking about it.

01:50:17.744 --> 01:50:20.814
- Yeah, and then you can be
happy for people around you.

01:50:20.814 --> 01:50:22.616
Which was one of the...

01:50:22.616 --> 01:50:24.184
One of the rules
in the first book.

01:50:24.184 --> 01:50:26.519
And I remember when I...

01:50:26.519 --> 01:50:28.488 align:start size:94% position:6%
I'm sure you remember
this when we were in Oslo

01:50:28.488 --> 01:50:30.423 align:start size:84% position:16%
and I walked into
the room and I said,

01:50:30.423 --> 01:50:31.791
"Jordan, I just got a call.

01:50:31.791 --> 01:50:33.460
I got a book deal."

01:50:33.460 --> 01:50:35.829 align:start size:84% position:16%
And you were sitting
in a swivel chair

01:50:35.829 --> 01:50:37.797 align:start size:84% position:16%
and you slapped
your hands together.

01:50:37.797 --> 01:50:39.833 align:start size:84% position:16%
You spun around in
the chair, you got up,

01:50:39.833 --> 01:50:42.202 align:start size:84% position:16%
you had a huge
smile on your face.

01:50:42.202 --> 01:50:43.670
You hugged me, Tammy got up.

01:50:43.670 --> 01:50:44.638
She had a huge smile.

01:50:44.638 --> 01:50:46.539
David came in, everybody hugged.

01:50:46.539 --> 01:50:49.643 align:start size:84% position:16%
And it was like, you
were genuinely...

01:50:49.643 --> 01:50:51.845
I mentioned a couple
of times in the book

01:50:51.845 --> 01:50:53.680
when I saw you live
up to the rules.

01:50:53.680 --> 01:50:55.782
But it was like you
were genuinely happy.

01:50:55.782 --> 01:50:57.083
There wasn't resentment.

01:50:57.083 --> 01:50:59.152
Like, "Oh, now this
guy got something."

01:50:59.152 --> 01:51:00.854
It was, this is great.

01:51:00.854 --> 01:51:02.422 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Well God, wouldn't
it be lovely

01:51:02.422 --> 01:51:06.426 align:start size:94% position:6%
to be able to enjoy
someone else's happiness.

01:51:06.426 --> 01:51:11.431 align:start size:94% position:6%
It's like, it's happiness
is precious and rare.

01:51:12.999 --> 01:51:15.535
And why deny yourself that?

01:51:15.535 --> 01:51:18.271 align:start size:84% position:16%
If it's from someone
else thank God

01:51:18.271 --> 01:51:22.842
they get a break from their
catastrophe of their life.

01:51:22.842 --> 01:51:24.044
And wouldn't it be wonderful

01:51:24.044 --> 01:51:26.413 align:start size:84% position:16%
if that was happening
all the time.

01:51:28.448 --> 01:51:31.051
You close yourself off
so badly being resentful.

01:51:31.051 --> 01:51:32.819
Because everyone
else's good fortune

01:51:32.819 --> 01:51:35.221
is equated to your misery.

01:51:35.221 --> 01:51:36.322
How terrible.

01:51:37.691 --> 01:51:41.094 align:start size:97% position:3%
And that's part of the
pathology of this insistence

01:51:41.094 --> 01:51:43.296 align:start size:84% position:16%
that our culture is
nothing but a tyranny.

01:51:44.698 --> 01:51:46.633
If that's the case, then
anyone who attains anything

01:51:46.633 --> 01:51:49.369
within it is only a tyrant.

01:51:49.369 --> 01:51:52.972 align:start size:97% position:3%
And then makes everyone's
accomplishments, an imposition

01:51:52.972 --> 01:51:55.842
and theft from everyone else.

01:51:55.842 --> 01:51:56.876
How awful.

01:51:58.378 --> 01:52:01.047
- Well you've been setting
it right for a long time.

01:52:01.047 --> 01:52:04.084
And I guess that brings us
perfectly to chapter 12.

01:52:04.084 --> 01:52:07.187
Which is be grateful in
spite of your suffering.

01:52:07.187 --> 01:52:10.256
And I think you probably know
a little something about it.

01:52:10.256 --> 01:52:11.191
- That's courage.

01:52:11.191 --> 01:52:14.027
That's courage to be grateful.

01:52:14.027 --> 01:52:15.995
To remind yourself.

01:52:15.995 --> 01:52:18.598
I say grace with my
wife every night.

01:52:19.866 --> 01:52:23.103
And I need to do that.

01:52:23.103 --> 01:52:26.106
I actually think in some ways,
it's the best part of my day.

01:52:26.106 --> 01:52:27.407
Strangely enough.

01:52:27.407 --> 01:52:29.509
Although that's not saying much.

01:52:31.978 --> 01:52:36.082 align:start size:94% position:6%
It's very useful to remind
myself to be grateful

01:52:36.082 --> 01:52:37.917
and the worst your situation,

01:52:37.917 --> 01:52:40.887 align:start size:84% position:16%
the more important
that is paradoxically.

01:52:40.887 --> 01:52:42.689
Because you can have
a terrible situation.

01:52:42.689 --> 01:52:47.460 align:start size:94% position:6%
And then also be
ungrateful, be resentful.

01:52:48.428 --> 01:52:49.896
And then it's worse.

01:52:49.896 --> 01:52:50.997
It's much worse.

01:52:54.200 --> 01:52:55.869
So it's courage.

01:52:55.869 --> 01:52:59.272
What do I have to
be grateful for?

01:53:01.341 --> 01:53:02.609
And to maintain that.

01:53:03.910 --> 01:53:05.512
That's better for everyone else.

01:53:06.479 --> 01:53:10.917
So that chapter is hard on me.

01:53:10.917 --> 01:53:13.353 align:start size:84% position:16%
It's sort of
traumatic to read it.

01:53:13.353 --> 01:53:14.788
Because I struggle with it.

01:53:19.926 --> 01:53:23.029
Because I'm in pain
most of the time,

01:53:24.230 --> 01:53:28.902
but I know that it's not
helpful to be resentful.

01:53:30.236 --> 01:53:31.638
It's better to be grateful.

01:53:32.839 --> 01:53:34.307
And you can do that
without being naive.

01:53:34.307 --> 01:53:35.608
It's an act of will.

01:53:37.043 --> 01:53:39.879 align:start size:84% position:16%
Or at least something
to practice.

01:53:41.614 --> 01:53:42.715
So that's the book

01:53:44.918 --> 01:53:49.522
- Jordan, I love you, man.

01:53:49.522 --> 01:53:52.225 align:start size:81% position:19%
You helped me turn
my life around,

01:53:52.225 --> 01:53:54.661
because of the things that
you were courageous enough

01:53:54.661 --> 01:53:56.029
to put out in the world.

01:53:56.029 --> 01:53:58.531
And I'm honored and humbled that

01:53:58.531 --> 01:54:01.334
I was this much of the adventure

01:54:01.334 --> 01:54:02.368
of the last couple of years.

01:54:02.368 --> 01:54:03.937
And I'm beyond thrilled.

01:54:03.937 --> 01:54:04.771
- We had such a good time.

01:54:04.771 --> 01:54:06.172
- We did man, we did.

01:54:07.774 --> 01:54:10.944
That's why it feels like a
dream because it was just...

01:54:10.944 --> 01:54:13.413
John, our tour manager
he used to always joke.

01:54:13.413 --> 01:54:15.748 align:start position:0%
He'd go, "This is the most
boring tour I've ever been on."

01:54:15.748 --> 01:54:18.284
Nobody's wrecking hotel rooms,

01:54:18.284 --> 01:54:20.587 align:start size:84% position:16%
and nobody is
beating anybody up.

01:54:20.587 --> 01:54:22.455 align:start size:84% position:16%
And I think our
running joke was that

01:54:22.455 --> 01:54:24.524 align:start size:97% position:3%
the worst thing that
happened on the entire tour,

01:54:24.524 --> 01:54:26.025
was we were in Calgary.

01:54:26.025 --> 01:54:27.660
And I accidentally said
that we were in Edmonton,

01:54:27.660 --> 01:54:29.329
which they were not happy about.

01:54:29.329 --> 01:54:30.163
- No you were in Toronto.

01:54:30.163 --> 01:54:31.731
- Was I in Toronto.

01:54:31.731 --> 01:54:33.099
- Even worse.

01:54:33.099 --> 01:54:36.502 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Right, those two or
at least Western Canada.

01:54:36.502 --> 01:54:37.971
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

01:54:37.971 --> 01:54:40.340 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Yeah, you committed a
cardinal sin for a Canadian.

01:54:40.340 --> 01:54:41.808
- But truly Jordan.

01:54:41.808 --> 01:54:43.743
It's just so good to
talk to you again.

01:54:43.743 --> 01:54:45.845
And I can't wait to
see you in person.

01:54:45.845 --> 01:54:47.847
And I'm thrilled
that you're back.

01:54:49.182 --> 01:54:51.718
- When we're out
of this COVID hell.

01:54:51.718 --> 01:54:56.556 align:start size:97% position:3%
And hopefully everything
returns to everything we had.

01:54:56.556 --> 01:54:58.291
When everything was normal.

01:54:58.291 --> 01:55:00.393
I walked down Queen
Street in Toronto.

01:55:00.393 --> 01:55:03.496
Kind of a trendy area,
populated by galleries

01:55:03.496 --> 01:55:06.432 align:start size:94% position:6%
and restaurants and
little independent shops.

01:55:06.432 --> 01:55:08.234 align:start size:88% position:13%
Where people have
poured their heart into

01:55:08.234 --> 01:55:09.469
and they're all shuttered.

01:55:09.469 --> 01:55:11.271 align:start size:84% position:16%
Every fourth business
is shuttered.

01:55:11.271 --> 01:55:13.773 align:start size:84% position:16%
And you see the
dreams that people...

01:55:13.773 --> 01:55:16.376 align:start size:97% position:3%
And effort that people
poured into these businesses.

01:55:17.577 --> 01:55:21.581
It's heart-wrenching to
see that all taken away.

01:55:21.581 --> 01:55:23.816
I'm amazed that people...

01:55:23.816 --> 01:55:26.386 align:start size:97% position:3%
I'm amazed at how well
people have done with this.

01:55:26.386 --> 01:55:29.022 align:start size:97% position:3%
I can't believe that
things haven't fallen apart

01:55:29.022 --> 01:55:30.623
more than they have.

01:55:30.623 --> 01:55:32.492 align:start size:84% position:16%
I don't know how
people are managing.

01:55:33.927 --> 01:55:36.863
But maybe we'll get lucky
in these vaccines will work

01:55:36.863 --> 01:55:40.199
and we'll get what we had back.

01:55:40.199 --> 01:55:43.836
And maybe we'll be wise enough
to appreciate it a bit more.

01:55:43.836 --> 01:55:44.771
That would be good.

01:55:44.771 --> 01:55:45.705
- When that be something.

01:55:45.705 --> 01:55:47.206
If we could only be appreciative

01:55:47.206 --> 01:55:49.008
of what we had a year ago today,

01:55:49.008 --> 01:55:50.777 align:start size:81% position:19%
before it all kind
of fell apart.

01:55:52.111 --> 01:55:55.214 align:start size:84% position:16%
- That would be
something, all right.

01:55:55.214 --> 01:55:56.749
- Jordan, welcome back.

01:55:56.749 --> 01:56:01.154
Welcome back and I-

01:56:01.154 --> 01:56:02.622
- Good talking to you.

01:56:02.622 --> 01:56:04.424
- I hope we can continue
on this adventure together.

01:56:04.424 --> 01:56:06.159
And it's good to see a smile.

01:56:06.159 --> 01:56:07.927
I'm thrilled you're back.

01:56:07.927 --> 01:56:09.395
So thank you.

